\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Key US weather monitoring offices understaffed as hurricane season starts\b0\par\par\uc2 Oliver Milman \par \uc2 1,687 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2025\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 National Weather Service offices are reeling from job cuts and a hiring freeze imposed by Trump\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen National Weather Service (NWS) forecast offices along the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/hurricanes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hurricane}}}\uc2 -prone Gulf of Mexico coast are understaffed as the US plunges into an expected active season for ruinous storms, data seen by the Guardian shows.\par \par \uc2 There is a lack of meteorologists in 15 of the regional weather service offices along the coastline from Texas to Florida, as well as in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/puerto-rico"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Puerto Rico}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an area that takes the brunt of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coast.noaa.gov/hurricanes/#map=4/32/-80"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost all}}}\uc2  hurricanes that hit the US. Several offices, including in Miami, Jacksonville, Puerto Rico and Houston, lack at least a third of all the meteorologists required to be fully staffed.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Hurricane Center}}}\uc2  (NHC), the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/miami"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Miami}}}\uc2 -based nerve center for tracking hurricanes, is short five specialists, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  has learned, despite assurances from the Trump administration that it is fully staffed ahead of what\u8217\'20\'19s anticipated to be a busy hurricane season that officially started on Sunday. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/01/trump-cuts-noaa-spam-emails"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Chaos\u8217\'20\'19: Trump cuts to Noaa disrupt staffing and weather forecasts}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 The center and local field offices work together to alert and prepare communities for incoming hurricanes, but they have been hit by job cuts and a hiring freeze imposed by the president, with more than 600 staff departing the \uc2 NWS\uc2  since Trump took power.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe system is already overstretched and at some point it will snap,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tom Fahy, legislative director of the National Weather Service Employees Organization, an independent labor union and provider of the office staffing data. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are at the snapping point now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An NHC spokesperson said the agency still has enough people to function properly. \u8220\'20\'1CNHC has a sufficient number of forecasters to fill mission-critical operational shifts during the 2025 hurricane season,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CNHC remains dedicated to its mission, providing timely tropical weather forecasts and warnings pursuant to our public safety mission.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But experts warned the turmoil unleashed by Trump upon the \uc2 NWS\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  (Fema), the national disaster agency that has had multiple leadership changes and still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/fema-hurricane-season-no-plan-39042ee6?gaa_at=eafs&gaa_n=ASWzDAgY0aCCYONIknpW4SjCNtDr90lhy5Tp09vo0ssNmdQA52xNGQURu9gEWDf6EFQ%3D&gaa_ts=6835f746&gaa_sig=Waf8kf-S_d1bowXJ-UWhcXtOPJ--fxcT6DrL7WJEelhx8SCEU7bBzfRpMVqi57HEglEokl9QLYPg-D-GDhMcnQ%3D%3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 does not have a completed plan}}}\uc2  for this year\u8217\'20\'19s hurricane season, will dangerously hamper the response to a summer that will likely bring storms, floods and wildfires across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStaff will put in an heroic effort but there is high probability of significant consequences because of the cuts,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Rick Spinrad\uc2 , who was administrator of the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  (Noaa) until January.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I were a citizen of Texas, Florida or Georgia, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be sure how well warned I would be of a hurricane. And if a hurricane was heading for a major city, I\u8217\'20\'19m not confident Fema would be able to respond to the forecasted warnings.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NWS\uc2 , which is part of Noaa, has been upended like other agencies by the efforts of Trump and the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge) to slash the government workforce. The weather service has scrambled to fill gaps with temporary secondments from other offices, but many roles remain unfilled in outposts that usually have about 25 employees each.\par \par \uc2 The agency is now asking for relocations to fill empty meteorologist-in-charge positions, the most senior role at a field office, in Houston, Texas, and Lake Charles, Louisiana \u8211\'20\'13 both places that have experienced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/jun/12/climate-crisis-to-blame-for-67bn-of-hurricane-harvey-damage-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 devastating}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/15/louisiana-lake-charles-climate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hurricanes}}}\uc2  in recent years. Cover for dozens of other meteorologist roles across the US is also being sought, including in the hurricane-prone areas of Miami and Key West, both in Florida, and Mobile, Alabama, according to an internal \uc2 NWS\uc2  document. <figure class="element element-interactive interactive" data-interactive="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/embed/iframe-wrapper/0.1/boot.js" data-canonical-url="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/05/archive-zip/giv-32554N1fZBNqI00qy" data-alt="Map showing shortstaffed National Weather Service stations in the Gulf of Mexico region"> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/05/archive-zip/giv-32554N1fZBNqI00qy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the loss of weather service personnel \u8211\'20\'13 from the firing of probationary workers and early retirements offered to veteran staff \u8211\'20\'13 has forced offices to shut down overnight rather than operate 24 hours a day as normal.\par \par \uc2 In seven of the 122 NWS stations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weather.gov/srh/nwsoffices"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 across the US}}}\uc2 , including in Jackson, Kentucky, where a tornado killed 19 people this month, there will be no round-the-clock operation from 1 June. Of the 122 offices, 30 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2025/05/02/weather/nws-forecasting-layoffs-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lack}}}\uc2  a meteorologist-in-charge.\par \par \uc2 While the weather service, which has existed in some form since 1870, has always had to shift around resources to deal with extreme events, former staff said the scale of the cuts place an unprecedented strain upon its ability to provide detailed, localized forecasts. The release of weather balloons has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/01/trump-cuts-noaa-spam-emails"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 been scaled back}}}\uc2 , technicians who maintain radar equipment have been fired and there are concerns that \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.omao.noaa.gov/aircraft-operations/noaa-hurricane-hunters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hurricane hunter}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D flights into storms will not be fully operational. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/29/appalachia-kentucky-floods-research-trump-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Flooding could end southern Appalachia\u8217\'20\'19: the scientists on an urgent mission to save lives}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI slept on the floor of the office during the hurricanes last year, but you can\u8217\'20\'19t do that every day because it leads to burnout. What the National Weather Service is doing now is a short-term fix of musical chairs, it\u8217\'20\'19s not sustainable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brian LaMarre, a 30-year weather service veteran who took early retirement in April from his role as meteorologist-in-charge of the office in Tampa, Florida. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s needed is for the National Weather Service and Noaa to be funded properly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While the tracking of hurricanes, which has improved markedly in recent years as technology and forecast models have advanced, will still be handled by the NHC, there are concerns that understaffed local offices won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to properly apply this information to affected areas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey can move the deckchairs on the Titanic but they just don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough bodies to do the job they are supposed to do,\u8221\'20\'1D said James Franklin, a retired \uc2 NWS\uc2  meteorologist who is a hurricane specialist. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m worried the local offices won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to communicate with local emergency services and local officials about threats because they won\u8217\'20\'19t have the bodies to do it. The uncertainty level of the forecast will go up, too.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Franklin said the cuts to jobs and to longer-term Noaa research aimed at improving forecasts will have a lasting impact. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not even shortsighted, it\u8217\'20\'19s no-sighted,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CEven if you don\u8217\'20\'19t see an impact this year, in five or 10 years you certainly will. They aren\u8217\'20\'19t even going to save any money doing this; it seems ideologically driven to me.\u8221\'20\'1D <figure class="element element-interactive interactive" data-interactive="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/embed/iframe-wrapper/0.1/boot.js" data-canonical-url="https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/05/archive-zip/giv-32554vkWyO0tj885D" data-alt="Stacked chart representing Staff shortages at National Weather Service offices"> {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2025/05/archive-zip/giv-32554vkWyO0tj885D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 The tumult within the US\u8217\'20\'19s premier weather agency comes as its leadership acknowledges that warm temperatures in the Gulf, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/08/hurricane-helene-milton-global-heating-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 symptom}}}\uc2  of the climate crisis, will probably spur {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/may/22/hurricane-season-scientists-prediction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an above-normal number of hurricanes}}}\uc2  this year. Six to 10 storms are expected to become hurricanes with winds of 74mph (119km/h) or higher, with as many as five reaching at least 111mph (179km/h).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEverything is in place for an above-average season,\u8221\'20\'1D Ken Graham, director of the \uc2 NWS\uc2 , said last week. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got to be prepared, right now. We\u8217\'20\'19ve got to be ready.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yet uncertainty is now commonplace throughout the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s apparatus to predict and respond to disasters. The situation at a depleted Fema is particularly parlous, with the agency {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fema.gov/press-release/20250522/fema-resumes-person-trainings-national-schoolhouses-strengthen-state-and"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reducing training}}}\uc2  for state and local emergency managers and lagging months behind schedule in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wired.com/story/fema-cancel-strategic-plan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preparing}}}\uc2  for hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 Trump and \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , the secretary of homeland security, have both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/24/trump-fema-emergency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 openly}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/policy/energy-environment/5220996-noem-trump-fema-elimination-concerns/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mulled}}}\uc2  dismantling Fema and its new acting administrator has struck a belligerent tone. \u8220\'20\'1CI, and I alone in Fema, speak for Fema. I\u8217\'20\'19m here to carry out the president\u8217\'20\'19s intent for Fema,\u8221\'20\'1D David Richardson, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fema.gov/about/organization/david-richardson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a former marine}}}\uc2  with no emergency management experience, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/fema-head-says-he-will-run-right-over-staff-who-resist-agency-changes-2025-05-09/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told staff in May}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI will run right over you,\u8221\'20\'1D he warned staff. \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t get in my way \u8230\'20\'26 I know all the tricks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a chaotic time at Fema, the constant departure of employees and the lack of leadership has distracted the agency from its mission,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Coen, who was chief of staff to the Fema administrator during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of confusion among states over what level of support they will get from the federal government. My concern is that if Fema has to respond to concurrent events, two hurricanes or a flood and a storm, it won\u8217\'20\'19t have the capacity to provide the proper level of support.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Such warnings have rattled some lawmakers amid a federal budget negotiation process that will probably conclude at some point during hurricane season, which stretches until November. Under a budget {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Fiscal-Year-2026-Discretionary-Budget-Request.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposal}}}\uc2  outlined by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s White House, Noaa\u8217\'20\'19s $6bn budget would be shrunk by around a quarter, effectively eliminating its climate and weather research.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans and Democrats are concerned about this because they know a tornado doesn\u8217\'20\'19t care if you live in a red state or a blue state,\u8221\'20\'1D said Fahy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMembers of Congress are concerned, I\u8217\'20\'19ve had several phone calls asking if we have enough people. The National Weather Service\u8217\'20\'19s budget is the cost of a cup of coffee for every person across the United States. I think people would say that\u8217\'20\'19s pretty good value.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A Fema spokesperson said that the Trump administration is \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to ensuring Americans affected by emergencies will get the help they need in a quick and efficient manner\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll operational and readiness requirements will continue to be managed without interruption in close coordination with local and state officials ahead of the 2025 hurricane season,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250602el62001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
