\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Going to increase prices on everybody\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 US energy department\uc2  workers sound alarm over cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2025\par 19:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees say cuts and deregulation undermine department\u8217\'20\'19s ability to function and will cause cost hikes\par \par \uc2 Workers at the \uc2 US Department of Energy\uc2  say cuts and deregulations are undermining the ability for the department to function and will result in significant energy cost hikes for consumers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbig, beautiful bill\u8221\'20\'1D will raise energy costs for American households by as much as 7% in 2035 due to the repeal of energy tax credits and could put significant investment and energy innovation at risk, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rhg.com/research/ways-and-means-brings-the-hammer-down-on-energy-credits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the Rhodium Group. The non-partisan thinktank Energy Innovation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/may/22/trump-republican-tax-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calculated}}}\uc2  the average US household will see its utility bills rise by over $230 by 2035 as a result of cuts to renewable energy investments.\par \par \uc2 The rises are being driven in part by cuts to the agency. Trump has proposed{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.utilitydive.com/news/trump-proposed-budget-department-energy-slashed-billions-iija-eere/747100/#:~:text=The%20president%20proposed%20making%20deep,Energy%20Efficiency%20and%20Renewable%20Energy.&text=The%20White%20House's%202026%20budget,the%20Office%20of%20Environmental%20Management."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  cutting }}}\uc2 the department\u8217\'20\'19s budget by $19.3bn.\par \par \uc2 More than 3,500 employees at the Department of Energy have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eenews.net/articles/details-emerge-on-surging-doe-departures/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  taken delayed resignation buyout offers, though the Department of Energy declined to provide final numbers or an estimate on the departures. Some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://federalnewsnetwork.com/workforce/2025/04/energy-department-extends-hiring-freeze-deems-43-workforce-non-essential-in-reorganization-plan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 43%}}}\uc2  of its workforce of nearly 16,000 employees was deemed \u8220\'20\'1Cnon-essential\u8221\'20\'1D, not including 555 probationary employees that were fired earlier this year. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/27/trump-safety-cuts-workplace-risks-niosh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s safety research cuts heighten workplace risks, federal workers warn}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Department of Energy\uc2  announced on 12 May plans to eliminate 47 regulations, comprising mostly of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.energy.gov/articles/energy-department-slashes-47-burdensome-and-costly-regulations-delivering-first-milestone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 energy efficiency standards}}}\uc2  for appliances, claiming the cuts would save nearly $11bn, but did not provide any analysis or data for how it came to that savings estimate.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Energy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.utilitydive.com/news/trump-doe-continues-attack-appliance-efficiency-program/743432/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  in December 2024 that stronger energy efficiency appliance standards would save consumers about $1trn over the next three decades. An analysis by the Appliance Standards Awareness Project found the energy efficiency cuts would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theverge.com/news/671615/energy-efficiency-rollbakcs-electricity-costs-utility-bills-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 add $54bn}}}\uc2  in utility energy costs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe impact of a lot of what I was working on in the energy efficiency and electrification space is aimed at saving folks money. The business case around energy efficiency has been made for the past 30 years. Reducing the cost of energy, any of those fixed costs for folks, can really be life changing, freeing up their budget for other necessities,\u8221\'20\'1D said a \uc2 US Department of Energy\uc2  employee who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation as they have accepted a resignation buyout offer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CChanging that has so many effects down the line,\u8221\'20\'1D they added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe already know things are getting more expensive. Budgets are getting tighter for many households in the state, and also territories and tribes. The work that I did was not only with states, but also with us, territories and tribes as well, and a lot of these communities, every dollar matters, and that\u8217\'20\'19s not unique to red or blue areas or anything like that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another employee at the \uc2 US Department of Energy\uc2  said morale at the department sank after attacks on civil servants by the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge) and the chaos and uncertainty of the firings of probationary employees, contractors, and employees resigning, leaving a drain on resources, talent and knowledge throughout the agency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAppointees came in with a clear agenda to dismantle programs and shrink staff,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is very clear they don\u8217\'20\'19t care about the work or the workforce. Many were looking to score points with Doge and made quick cuts without concerns for long-term damage, such as the chaos and lost knowledge caused by the delayed resignation program.\u8221\'20\'1D <aside class="element element-pullquote element--supporting">\par \par \uc2 It is very clear they don\u8217\'20\'19t care about the work or the workforce. Many were looking to score points with Doge <footer> \uc2 US energy department\uc2  employee </footer> </aside>\par \par \uc2 A former senior Department of Energy official who requested to remain anonymous explained the totality of the cuts to personnel, grants, regulations and budget for the department are \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing to increase prices on everybody\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs much as the election was on affordability, there\u8217\'20\'19s a reason that Trump is doing incredibly poorly on affordability and inflation. I think what\u8217\'20\'19s happening at the Department of Energy is just such a great example of a whole variety of efforts that near-term, medium-term and longer-term are going to raise prices on consumers, on companies, and make us less competitive internationally,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe efficiency regulations end up saving consumers an awful lot of money, certainly as a percentage of their budget. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think there is any truth whatsoever, if you talk to anyone who\u8217\'20\'19s ever done analysis and rigor on this, that somehow not doing these regulations is actually saving money. It\u8217\'20\'19s the exact opposite if you think of the whole system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They also criticized the fact that many of these actions will result in lawsuits and legal changes, and the negative impacts of research and development cuts to renewable energy.\par \par \uc2 They cited the demand for energy to power emerging AI and data centers and energy consumption is expected to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.deseret.com/utah/2025/03/27/panel-details-explosive-growth-in-energy-demand/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rise significantly}}}\uc2  and wind, solar, and battery energy storage are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20250409211138/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/17/climate/renewable-energy-trump-electricity.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 relatively quick and cheap}}}\uc2  to construct.\par \par \uc2 About {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canarymedia.com/articles/clean-energy/chart-96-percent-of-new-us-power-capacity-was-carbon-free-in-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 96%}}}\uc2  of added US energy capacity to the grid in 2024 was from carbon-free sources. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/14/trump-federal-layoffs-ripple-effect-us-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18So many are devastated\u8217\'20\'19: Trump\u8217\'20\'19s federal firings and their ripple effect}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you stop any research for next generation solar or battery technology, or wind or geothermal or other pieces, what you\u8217\'20\'19re effectively doing is compromising a huge range of technology that has the potential to reduce costs, and of course, has the potential to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. But even if you don\u8217\'20\'19t care about that, these are the technologies that could reduce costs for consumers,\u8221\'20\'1D they added. \u8220\'20\'1CThe chaos with the tariffs, with the regulations, with the not fully thought through and analyzed nature of this is just causing a lot of confusion, a lot of incoherence, a lot of inconsistency and uncertainty. And that\u8217\'20\'19s just not good for businesses, let alone consumers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 US Department of Energy\uc2  refuted claims of costs due to eliminating regulations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump and Secretary Wright pledged to restore commonsense to our regulatory policies and lower costs for American consumers \u8211\'20\'13 that is exactly what these {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.energy.gov/articles/energy-department-slashes-47-burdensome-and-costly-regulations-delivering-first-milestone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deregulatory actions}}}\uc2  do. To argue consumers benefit from being forced to purchase more-expensive, time-intensive products that are often less energy efficient because they don\u8217\'20\'19t do the job right the first time is total nonsense,\u8221\'20\'1D they said in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CDOE\u8217\'20\'19s approach recognizes that consumer choice and market-driven innovation, not bureaucratic mandates, lead to better-performing and more affordable consumer products. DOE\u8217\'20\'19s deregulatory actions empower consumers to choose products that meet their needs and budgets, while also supporting American manufacturers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250531el5v0018q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
