\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s safety research cuts heighten workplace risks, federal workers warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2025\par 17:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Layoffs at US occupational safety and health agency leave people vulnerable to illness and injury, ex-workers caution\par \par \uc2 Drastic cuts at a federal workplace safety research agency increase the risk of illness and injury for workers across the US and undermine preparations for public health emergencies, fired employees warn.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  ordered widespread layoffs at the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2 , inside the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , when it issued a \u8220\'20\'1Creduction in force\u8221\'20\'1D notice to some 85% of the agency\u8217\'20\'19s 1,100 workers employees on 1 April.\par \par \uc2 While some of those terminations were later reversed following pushback from labor unions and the public, only 328 employees were reinstated. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/14/trump-federal-layoffs-ripple-effect-us-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18So many are devastated\u8217\'20\'19: Trump\u8217\'20\'19s federal firings and their ripple effect}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn immediate impact is that we\u8217\'20\'19re not as prepared for some type of public health emergency,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Micah Niemeier-Walsh, an industrial hygienist at \uc2 Niosh\uc2  in Ohio, who was fired, and then reinstated. \u8220\'20\'1CLong term, the Trump administration talks about wanting to bring back or expand certain sectors of the economy like mining or manufacturing. Those are jobs that really rely on \uc2 Niosh\uc2  work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jennica Bellanca, for example, worked to train emergency responders in mining in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. Her role was terminated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s such a hard thing. Everyone here works so hard to help support the health and safety of mine workers and other workers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, noting that small companies do not have the resources to invest in alternative safety programs. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m just concerned that nobody else is going to fill this gap. There\u8217\'20\'19s a reason that the government provides this safety net.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn our case, the safety net is to help workers go home to their families every day and make sure that nothing bad happens.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bellanca questioned a central argument for the cuts \u8211\'20\'13 efficiency in federal government spending \u8211\'20\'13 by noting that long-term research projects may now go unreleased. <aside class="element element-pullquote element--supporting">\par \par \uc2 When we\u8217\'20\'19re gone, there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be nobody to get this information out <footer> Jennica Bellanca, former trainer for emergency responders </footer> </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we\u8217\'20\'19re gone, there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be nobody to get this information out,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd because we were so abruptly, sort of cut off in the middle of projects, all of this work that we\u8217\'20\'19ve done, we\u8217\'20\'19re not able to get this out, released, as a full public product. In my mind, that\u8217\'20\'19s a waste of government money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Niemeier-Walsh, the vice-president of American Federal of Government Employees Local 3840, said the reduction in force was the \u8220\'20\'1Cfinal, massive blow to our work\u8221\'20\'1D after earlier limitations on travel, communications and remote work imposed since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  took office in January.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur ability to be as successful as we have been as an institute relies on the rest of the \uc2 Niosh\uc2  employees coming back because our work is so interconnected,\u8221\'20\'1D said Niemeier-Walsh. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very, very concerned what this means for every single American worker if we\u8217\'20\'19re not able to fully restore \uc2 Niosh\uc2 . These cuts are not based in science. They\u8217\'20\'19re not based on the public health need. They\u8217\'20\'19re based on politics, and that\u8217\'20\'19s bad for the health of the American people.\u8221\'20\'1D <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/03/mass-resignations-at-labor-department-threaten-workers-in-us-and-overseas-warn-staff-as-more-cuts-loom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mass resignations at labor department threaten workers in US and overseas, warn staff \u8211\'20\'13 as more cuts loom}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 She cited as an example the employees in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/hhe/default.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 health hazard evaluation program}}}\uc2 , which was established to reduce workplace risks and recommend ways to mitigate dangers, who were reinstated after earlier cuts. But they rely on chemists to develop analytical methods to measure chemicals in the workplace, and engineers to design solutions; these chemists and engineers have not been reinstated, she said.\par \par \uc2 Since the agency was founded in 1970, recordable workplace illnesses and injuries and fatalities have been drastically reduced in the US. The rate of non-fatal workplace injuries and illnesses {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/data/commonstats#:~:text=OSHA%20is%20Making%20a%20Difference%20*%20In,1972%20to%202.4%20per%20100%20in%202023."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined}}}\uc2  from 10.9 cases per 100 full-time workers in 1972 to 2.4 in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the largest federation of labor unions in the US, and several other labor unions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/press/releases/labor-and-workplace-health-and-safety-groups-sue-restore-programs-national-institute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  this month to restore the cut programs at \uc2 Niosh\uc2 , arguing the cuts \u8220\'20\'1Cdirectly threaten the lives of workers whose safety and health depend on \uc2 Niosh\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D services that are congressionally mandated.\par \par \uc2 Even though some of the initial cuts have been reversed,\u8220\'20\'1Cwe have deep concerns that the whole reason \uc2 Niosh\uc2  was started to begin with is still eliminated,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rebecca Reindel, the director of occupational safety and health at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , who noted that the agency\u8217\'20\'19s founding mandate was to assure every man and woman in the US has safe and healthful working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Reindel expressed particular concern about disruption to long-term research projects. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/08/trump-job-cuts-workers-impacts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US workers feel effects of Trump cuts: \u8216\'20\'18I am seeing my work dry up\u8217\'20\'19}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of this research work that they do, where they have these big cohorts, they\u8217\'20\'19ve been following people for 40 years,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd now they\u8217\'20\'19re just cut off for these occupational prospective cohorts, where they follow them over years to see what kind of diseases are developing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we\u8217\'20\'19re just staffing those cohorts now, we\u8217\'20\'19re really losing 40 years worth of work. Even if they restart them, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have lost so many people to follow up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  did not comment on the record. Its secretary, Robert F Kennedy Jr, was working to ensure \uc2 Niosh\uc2  critical services remain intact and continue as the agency streamlines its operations, they claimed.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250527el5r001e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
