\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Firms \u8216\'20\'18rebranding\u8217\'20\'19 diversity initiatives to avoid unwanted political attention\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Osuh and Aamna Mohdin \par \uc2 1,135 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 May 2025\par 11:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 British business figures say many companies in UK and US disguising inclusion policies in face of rightwing attacks\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/may/26/corporate-diversity-policies-under-fire-from-right-also-left-dei">Corporate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/may/26/corporate-diversity-policies-under-fire-from-right-also-left-dei">Corporate}}}\uc2  diversity policies are under fire from the right \u8211\'20\'13 but also from the left</a>\par \par \uc2 Senior figures in British business have described a chilling effect on diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) initiatives, saying they are being \u8220\'20\'1Crebranded\u8221\'20\'1D to avoid attracting unwanted political attention.\par \par \uc2 Five years after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/george-floyd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the murder of George Floyd}}}\uc2  in the US shone a spotlight on racial inequality, efforts to create a level playing field in the workplace are facing a rightwing backlash on both sides of the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 But experts say many organisations are resisting the attacks by reframing policies previously known as DEI in the US, and sometimes EDI (equality, diversity and inclusion) in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Paul Sesay, the founder and chief executive of the National Diversity Awards, whose sponsors include Amazon, Auto Trader and \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s rebranded to \u8216\'20\'18wellbeing\u8217\'20\'19, \u8216\'20\'18belonging\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18culture\u8217\'20\'19. Even with roles, it\u8217\'20\'19s no longer heads of DEI, it\u8217\'20\'19s heads of culture, heads of people, heads of wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re basically embedding diversity and getting rid of the word \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily see it as a bad thing. When I first started, back in 2003, it was about diversity \u8211\'20\'13 and that was the main word \u8211\'20\'13 but then, as it progressed, it was inclusion. It\u8217\'20\'19s advancing now to culture, transformation, that kind of thing, as culture affects the whole organisation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the UK, the Reform party has vowed to scrap DEI initiatives from councils it controls, months after the Trump administration began {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/21/trump-hegseth-joint-chiefs-cq-brown-jr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sacking black public servants}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/13/trump-war-dei-american-straight-white-man-diversity-equity-inclusion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ripping up fairness policies}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Experts believe a full-scale rollback is unlikely in the UK, where the Equalities Act and the legal risk of discrimination cases present barriers to turning back the clock.\par \par \uc2 But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/08/barclays-ditches-gender-and-ethnicity-targets-in-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some companies, particularly US-headquartered firms, have already scaled back some commitments,}}}\uc2  and are less keen to celebrate inclusion work publicly.\par \par \uc2 Sesay said US businesses were finding ways to disguise DEI work so they could still promote the best talent and address inequalities. \u8220\'20\'1CAmerican companies have had to change, simply to survive in America. They have to take the word diversity out of absolutely everything. I\u8217\'20\'19ve heard a few firms that say inclusion is allowed within their policies, but the word diversity isn\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Noreen Biddle Shah, who founded Reboot, a campaigning group of senior financial service professionals, to tackle racial inequality in the industry in 2019, said the \u8220\'20\'1Clandscape feels very different\u8221\'20\'1D now.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDEI has become a politically charged term, and many firms are either stepping back from the work \u8211\'20\'13 a trend we saw coming from the US, but it is impacting UK financial services too,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shah.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot only do fewer organisations want to partner with us, but they\u8217\'20\'19re also discouraging their own employees from speaking out. In our latest annual Race to Equality: Financial Services report, we found that in light of the DEI backlash, ethnic minority professionals in the sector feel muzzled for speaking out \u8211\'20\'13 many expressed fear of losing their jobs if they raised race-related concerns. And sadly, 70% said there has been little progress since the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/14/diversity-equity-inclusion-dei-us-trump-google-amazon-walmart"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the retrenchment driven by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return}}}\uc2 , there was concern {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/europe/2024/10/18/city-of-london-given-up-on-inclusion-black-workers-say/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 companies were drawing back from promises }}}\uc2 made in 2020, when the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  sparked a conversation across the west about societal fairness.\par \par \uc2 Nina Mohanty, a finalist in Veuve Clicquot\u8217\'20\'19s Bold Future awards 2025, which celebrates rising female entrepreneurs, and who is the founder of a tech startup, Bloom Money, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the world of startups and tech companies, you had a lot of VCs [venture capitalists] that were saying, \u8216\'20\'18we\u8217\'20\'19re gonna do special programmes for black entrepreneurs, we\u8217\'20\'19re gonna back more women and women of colour\u8217\'20\'19. And there are a lot of initiatives and there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of talk. Five years on, where are those things? The budgets are gone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 James Hockin, an employment law expert and partner at global law firm Withers who advises senior executives in New York and London, said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s unpopularity among some people also meant employees were exerting pressure on British employers to keep policies in place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got more people in the UK now saying, \u8216\'20\'18well, what are we saying on this? I don\u8217\'20\'19t like what Trump is doing on DEI\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere has been for a little while, a bit of a temptation just to keep quiet and let it happen and just go, \u8216\'20\'18OK, let\u8217\'20\'19s just wait and see\u8217\'20\'19. But I think increasingly, with pressure from employees, there is a desire for employers to be seen to be taking a stance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Agreeing that DEI would survive but in a \u8220\'20\'1Crebranded\u8221\'20\'1D form, Hockin said: \u8220\'20\'1CDEI, for employers at least, started from a place of sorting out the culture in an organisation so that you weren\u8217\'20\'19t leaving yourself vulnerable to a [legal] claim against your organisation \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2025/apr/07/uk-firms-diversity-and-inclusion-lawsuits-us-trump#:~:text=British%20businesses%20face%20a%20greater,on%20employment%20law%20has%20warned."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 employers were keen to mitigate their risk}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere employers are getting on side with the Trump agenda, inadvertently, what they\u8217\'20\'19re doing, is potentially opening themselves up to that risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/14/ditching-diversity-risks-progress-co-op"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Earlier this year, Shirine Khoury-Haq, the CEO of Co-op group, warned that a DEI rollback risked undoing decades of progress.}}}\uc2  Zahoor Ahmad, Co-op\u8217\'20\'19s head of social mobility, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe key assumption is that if you didn\u8217\'20\'19t have [DEI], everything will be a level playing field. All of human history tells us that is not true. It\u8217\'20\'19s very hard to have a true meritocracy, when we are not born equal. What we are trying to do is eliminate inequalities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A number of trade bodies have also doubled down on their commitment to inclusion in the face of the backlash.\par \par \uc2 The Law Society\u8217\'20\'19s president, Richard Atkinson, said that while \u8220\'20\'1Cthe current climate in the United States has made diversity and inclusion work increasingly difficult\u8221\'20\'1D, the organisation would never waver from its \u8220\'20\'1Cwholehearted\u8221\'20\'1D commitment to a more diverse and inclusive legal profession.\par \par \uc2 UK Finance\uc2 , which represents the banking and financial service sector, said greater diversity was \u8220\'20\'1Ca driver for economic success,\u8221\'20\'1D while the Local Government Association said the case for DEI remained \u8220\'20\'1Ccompelling\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dawar Hashmi, a director at Penna, a leading recruiter for senior local government executives, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThere has been a noticeable pullback in the UK within some sectors \u8211\'20\'13 when the US sneezes, the UK catches a cold. This trend concerns me. But the public sector, our primary client base, continues to champion these values. Embracing DEI isn\u8217\'20\'19t just morally right, its actually a strategic advantage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250526el5q001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
