\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Bradfield counting paused until Monday \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Caitlin Cassidy and Rafqa Touma (earlier) \par \uc2 11,371 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2025\par 07:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closed\par \par \uc2 * <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/election-vote-count-tim-wilson-zoe-daniel-goldstein-gisele-kapterian-nicolette-boele-bradfield-ntwnfb">Bradfield"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/election-vote-count-tim-wilson-zoe-daniel-goldstein-gisele-kapterian-nicolette-boele-bradfield-ntwnfb">Bradfield}}}\uc2  and Goldstein face nervous weekend wait for election result but Tim Wilson \u8216\'20\'18very confident\u8217\'20\'19</a>\par \par \uc2 * Get our <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl">breaking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl">breaking}}}\uc2  news email</a>, <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3">free"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3">free}}}\uc2  app</a> or <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl">daily"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl">daily}}}\uc2  news podcast</a>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T07:50:45.615Z">8.50am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 What we learned: Friday, 16 May\par \par \uc2 With that, we will wrap the blog for the evening. Have a lovely weekend and thanks for reading.\par \par \uc2 Here were the major developments of the day:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Candidates in the Sydney seat of Bradfield face a nervous weekend with the \uc2 Australian Electoral Commission\uc2  confirming there will be no more vote counting until Monday. Meanwhile, independent Zoe Daniel has narrowed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/15/tim-wilson-urges-scrutineers-to-knock-out-informal-votes-for-zoe-daniel-in-leaked-whatsapp-messages-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tim Wilson\u8217\'20\'19s}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/15/tim-wilson-urges-scrutineers-to-knock-out-informal-votes-for-zoe-daniel-in-leaked-whatsapp-messages-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  lead}}}\uc2  in the seat of Goldstein, but the Liberal, who says he remains \u8220\'20\'1Crelaxed\u8221\'20\'1D, is still likely to win the seat.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 ANZ has lowered its interest rate cut prediction to just two for 2025 and expects a third in 2026, becoming the first major bank to push its prediction back that far.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government has advocated for an above-inflation rise in the minimum wage for nearly 3 million workers, ahead of the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 FWC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s) annual decision next month.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Former Victorian opposition leader John Pesutto has been ordered to pay $2.3m in costs after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/bankruptcy-fears-john-pesutto-moira-deeming-defamation-costs-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 losing a defamation case}}}\uc2  brought by Liberal MP Moira Deeming .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ben Roberts-Smith\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/ben-roberts-smith-loses-appeal-defamation-ruling-case-war-crimes-afghanistan-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost his appeal}}}\uc2  against a defamation case ruling, with three justices of the federal court agreeing he was not defamed by Nine newspapers and journalists Nick McKenzie and Chris Masters when they published reports in 2018 which claimed he had committed war crimes.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T07:53:14.890Z">at 8.53am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T07:14:47.801Z">8.14am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Zoe Daniel trails Tim Wilson by 206 votes\par \par \uc2 Liberal Tim Wilson \u8217\'20\'19s lead over independent Zoe Daniel in Goldstein has shrunk again to 206 votes.\par \par \uc2 According to the AEC, there were 256 ballots awaiting processing shortly before 5pm.\par \par \uc2 But the AEC commissioner, Jeff Pope , said \u8220\'20\'1Ca few thousand\u8221\'20\'1D votes for electorates across the country would come in on Friday, including around 120 from Nairobi:\par \par \uc2 We will have staff potentially out at Sydney airport at late hours tonight, before midnight, trying to pick up some of these votes.\par \par \uc2 Pope said it was difficult to estimate how many of these votes could be for tight seats such as Bradfield or Goldstein, but suggested around 30-50.\par \par \uc2 Postal and overseas votes received before the midnight Friday deadline will be counted on Monday.\par \par \uc2 If the vote margin is less than 100 after all preferences are distributed, a recount is automatically called.\par \par \uc2 Pope said candidates could request a recount once all preferences had been distributed, even if the margin was higher than 100.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T07:30:15.603Z">at 8.30am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:52:19.329Z">7.52am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Aussie shares hit three-month high as streak continues\par \par \uc2 The Australian share market has clocked its highest close in three months after eight straight sessions of gains.\par \par \uc2 The S&P/ASX200 retreated after an early spike but finished 38.2 points, or 0.46%, higher at 8,335.7.\par \par \uc2 The broader All Ordinaries rose 41.6 points, or 0.49%, to 8,571.4.\par \par \uc2 The top-200 is within 3% of its all-time peak after local shares rallied on this week\u8217\'20\'19s US-China tariff de-escalation and a likely Reserve Bank interest rate cut next week.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  also pipped a new intraday all-time high of $172.92, but ran into heavy selling to trade back below $170.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar is buying 64.32 US cents, down slightly from 64.39 on Thursday afternoon after a surprise slip in US producer prices weighed on the greenback.\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Australian Associated Press\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:54:13.825Z">at 7.54am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:36:47.432Z">7.36am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Election result \u8216\'20\'18bittersweet\u8217\'20\'19 for Greens: Faruqi\par \par \uc2 Faruqi said the Greens would do a \u8220\'20\'1Cdetailed evaluation\u8221\'20\'1D of the election result, adding there had been \u8220\'20\'1Chighs and there\u8217\'20\'19s been lows\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We lost wonderful, wonderful colleagues and representatives of their communities. Where changes need to be made, we\u8217\'20\'19ll make the changes \u8230\'20\'26 It\u8217\'20\'19s been bittersweet for us, losing those lower house seats, but then getting sole balance of power in the Senate and tens of thousands of people in migrant and multicultural communities really backing our stance on the cost-of-living crisis, a strong stand against the genocide in Gaza and against racism.\par \par \uc2 Pressed on whether she was \u8220\'20\'1Ccomfortable\u8221\'20\'1D with the Greens\u8217\'20\'19 position on Gaza given how the \u8220\'20\'1Cpublic debate \u8230\'20\'26 played out in the domestic scene\u8221\'20\'1D, Faruqi replied:\par \par \uc2 Our position against a genocide in Gaza is not a position taken because we would win votes or lose votes.\par \par \uc2 I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t sleep at night if the Greens hadn\u8217\'20\'19t spoken up in the strongest way against the massacre of tens of thousands \u8211\'20\'13 of if not hundreds of thousands of \u8211\'20\'13 innocent people who are now being starved. There were just 100 people in Gaza that were murdered last night. So, we took a strong moral position and a position of justice. And we will not back away from that.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:43:22.299Z">at 7.43am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:34:24.249Z">7.34am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Mehreen Faruqi defends Greens\u8217\'20\'19 record in previous parliament\par \par \uc2 The deputy Greens leader, Senator Mehreen Faruqi, appeared on ABC Afternoon Briefing earlier after being re-endorsed for her position.\par \par \uc2 Asked if the approach her party had taken was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot effective\u8221\'20\'1D given they lost three lower house seats, Faruqi said the Greens had the trust of \u8220\'20\'1Cmillions of people who have put us in the sole balance of power in the Senate\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s tens of thousands of voters who have voted for the Greens for the first time in communities that had not done that before \u8230\'20\'26 migrant and multicultural communities \u8230\'20\'26 to hold the Labor government to account. And that will take many shapes and forms. Of course we\u8217\'20\'19ll work constructively and cooperatively when needed. But we\u8217\'20\'19ll also fight for people and the planet when needed. That\u8217\'20\'19s what people are asking us to do.\par \par \uc2 On whether the Greens had been too obstructive in the last parliament, Faruqi said that was a narrative pushed by Labor and her party had negotiated a number of concessions.\par \par \uc2 Because of the pressure we put on Labor for the last three years and even beyond that, wiping all student debt, making price gouging illegal for supermarkets, and on free GPs, because of Greens pressure, Labor took on those policies and I think the first cab off the rank in the next parliament should be for Labor to do that work and we are ready and willing to do that.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:39:38.601Z">at 7.39am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:20:06.222Z">7.20am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 ANZ predicts just two interest rate cuts this year\par \par \uc2 ANZ has lowered its interest rate cut prediction to just two for 2025 and expects a third in 2026, becoming the first major bank to push its prediction back that far.\par \par \uc2 Even a heavily tipped rate cut from the Reserve Bank next week is no longer a \u8220\'20\'1Cnear certainty\u8221\'20\'1D but only a two-in-three chance, ANZ analysts wrote on Friday, stepping back from their previous assurance:\par \par \uc2 We have lessened our conviction on this, though we still consider a May cut more likely than not.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets, though, were this morning predicting a 95% chance of the \uc2 RBA\uc2  board will cut when it meets on Tuesday. Markets expect a further two in 2025, as do \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Westpac\uc2 , the latter of which says holding rates was out of the question in its own note today.\par \par \uc2 But ANZ thinks even that figure is too high, after signs the US and China are getting closer to a deal on lower tariffs. At home, slight improvements in business and household confidence and sustained strength in job growth and wage levels add to arguments for a hold, the bank\u8217\'20\'19s analysts say.\par \par \uc2 All of these predictions are well below the five or more cuts markets were expecting in the wake of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tariff announcement in April. \uc2 NAB\uc2  is the only major bank expecting five cuts this year, including a double-sized rate cut on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That said, ANZ still expects Australia\u8217\'20\'19s economy will need lower rates to manage the damage it sustains from heightened global uncertainty in the wake of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs. The bank is expecting unemployment to rise to 4.4% over 2025, from its current level of 4.1%, and the economy to grow just 2%, below the 2.4% the \uc2 RBA\uc2  predicted in February.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:26:12.361Z">at 7.26am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:58:18.851Z">6.58am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Police officer allegedly punched and stabbed in Wagga Wagga\par \par \uc2 A man is in custody after a female police officer was allegedly stabbed and punched in the New South Wales city of Wagga Wagga this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The, officer aged in her 20s, was allegedly attacked in Kooringal shortly before 2pm today when trying to stop a man who was riding a bike without a helmet.\par \par \uc2 Police allege the 31-year-old man punched her multiple times in the head before stabbing her in the arm and abdomen with a small screwdriver.\par \par \uc2 The officer was treated at the scene by NSW Ambulance paramedics before being taken to Wagga Wagga Base hospital in a stable condition. She was also being checked for suspected fractures to her fingers and a head injury.\par \par \uc2 The man was arrested at the scene and taken to Wagga Wagga police station.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:22:48.887Z">at 7.22am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:44:31.185Z">6.44am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Algal bloom linked to surge in shark sightings in South Australia\par \par \uc2 A sharp rise in shark sightings could be linked to an algal bloom that has killed about 100 species of fish and sharks amid a nine-month long marine heatwave across southern Australia, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 A shark attack at a popular Adelaide beach on Thursday, in which a swimmer aged in his 60s had his leg mauled, has focused attention on the growing number of sightings and strandings since the bloom formed in March.\par \par \uc2 South Australia\u8217\'20\'19s shark sightings log shows 195 reports so far in 2025, compared with 313 in 2024, 148 (2023), 153 (2022), 264 (2021) and 84 (2020).\par \par \uc2 Since the microalgae bloom, karenia mikimotoi, was identified{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.sa.gov.au/blog/2025/03/25/microalgae_bloom_on_the_fleurieu_peninsula_identified"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  off the Fleurieu Peninsula in March}}}\uc2 , there have been hundreds of reports of marine deaths, ranging from sharks and penguins, to popular fishing species such as flathead, squid, crabs, and rock lobsters.\par \par \uc2 The state environment minister, Susan Close, said the bloom had grown to an unprecedented scale, close to the size of Kangaroo Island, which is 4,405 sq km, and as deep as 20m.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in May, authorities removed a dead white shark washed up on Henley beach in Adelaide and two other sharks were reported dead at Port Willunga in Adelaide\u8217\'20\'19s south and at Port Moorowie, on the south coast of Yorke Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:02:14.277Z">at 7.02am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:25:13.584Z">6.25am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Wet and wintry weekend ahead for south-east Australia\par \par \uc2 Get your umbrellas and thick socks outs, winter is coming for south-eastern Australia this weekend.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Meteorology\uc2  forecasting rain, hail and even snow for some parts of the country. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/new-south-wales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New South Wales}}}\uc2 , wet and windy conditions could see a \u8220\'20\'1Creally rough end to the weekend\u8221\'20\'1D, Miriam Bradbury , a senior meteorologist at the bureau, said.\par \par \uc2 Read the story from Petra Stock here: <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/may/16/wintry-weather-south-east-australia-weekend-rain-wind-temperature-bom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Rough end to the weekend\u8217\'20\'19: wintry weather on the way for south-east Australia}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:27:02.686Z">at 6.27am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:09:11.432Z">6.09am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Pfas contamination in Blue Mountains may be due to 1992 crash, WaterNSW says\par \par \uc2 A rural fire station and a petrol tanker crash in 1992 may be possible causes of elevated levels of Pfas chemicals in untreated water in the NSW Blue Mountains, WaterNSW says.\par \par \uc2 It completed an initial investigation into the source of Pfas in the Adams Creek and Medlow catchments after disconnecting two dams from the water network last year due to the detection of elevated levels of so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cforever chemicals\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The state water agency said on Friday it was commencing a more detailed investigation, with support from the \uc2 Environment Protection Authority\uc2 , to try to confirm the source and identify possible management measures.\par \par \uc2 The Medlow and Greaves Creek dams are part of the raw water supply network that feeds into the Cascade filtration plant for treatment.\par \par \uc2 The agency said in a statement the two dams \u8220\'20\'1Cwill only be returned to the raw water supply network once WaterNSW is confident appropriate permanent mitigation measures are in place\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 With these disconnections in place and according to the most recent monitoring results, \uc2 NSW Health\uc2  and \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2  have advised drinking water in the Blue Mountains meets the existing Australian Drinking Water Guidelines and is safe to drink.\par \par \uc2 WaterNSW said its investigation included taking more than 250 samples from 37 different locations.\par \par \uc2 The initial report finds the Medlow Bath fire station on the Great Western Highway in the upper portion of the Adams Creek catchment and two vehicle crashes on the highway \u8211\'20\'13 one in 1992 and one in 2002 \u8211\'20\'13 were potential sources of the Pfas contamination.\par \par \uc2 The report said fire-fighting foam containing Pfas had reportedly been used at both vehicle accidents.\par \par \uc2 The report recommended further investigation to confirm if any of the three options were the source of the contamination.\par \par \uc2 It found Pfas concentrations were higher in the upper Adams Creek catchment than downstream in Lake Medlow or Lake Greaves.\par \par \uc2 WaterNSW said this suggested that \u8220\'20\'1Cany contamination may be moving downstream into the Medlow/Greaves catchment via rainfall and surface water runoff, and previous water transfers between dams\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:15:45.650Z">at 6.15am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T05:03:36.010Z">6.03am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining me on the blog today. Handing over now to Caitlin Cassidy who will keep you posted with the afternoon\u8217\'20\'19s news.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:54:08.132Z">5.54am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Government invests in new ADF munitions\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government is investing in developing precision loitering munitions for the Australian Defence Force.\par \par \uc2 Mission Talon Strike has been launched as an advanced strategic capabilities accelerator mission to \u8220\'20\'1Csupport the development of medium-range precision loitering munition systems that carry a kinetic payload and have the precision strike capabilities of a guided missile\u8221\'20\'1D, according to a statement.\par \par \uc2 This comes after last week\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CMission Syracuse\u8221\'20\'1D announcement, which supports the ADF\u8217\'20\'19s drone countering capabilities. The minister for defence industry, \uc2 Pat Conroy\uc2  , said:\par \par \uc2 The launch of Mission Talon Strike by ASCA is about developing a priority capability at an accelerated pace.\par \par \uc2 This mission is aligned with the Albanese government\u8217\'20\'19s work over the last three years, in conjunction with the ADF, to develop a domestically manufactured precision loitering munition capability that addresses threats quickly and accurately.\par \par \uc2 Through Talon Strike, we are continuing to back Australian industry and ingenuity while demonstrating support for a critical pathway to meet the needs of the ADF.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:59:56.776Z">at 5.59am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:36:55.238Z">5.36am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Bradfield candidates to wait until Monday before vote counting continues\par \par \uc2 Candidates in the Sydney seat of Bradfield face a nervous weekend with the \uc2 Australian Electoral Commission\uc2  confirming there will be no more vote counting until Monday.\par \par \uc2 Scrutineers had expected to receive an additional dispatch of votes this afternoon, but have just been told that will no longer happen.\par \par \uc2 All outstanding ballot papers will be opened and added to the count on Monday, before the full distribution of preference. This count may take up most of next week.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, Liberal candidate Gisele Kapterian leads the independent candidate Nicolette Boele by just 43 votes.\par \par \uc2 There are slightly more than 300 votes left to count at this stage, but that number may increase if more postal votes are received before tonight\u8217\'20\'19s midnight deadline. As mentioned earlier, some of these votes will be coming from Nairobi.\par \par \uc2 If the margin in Bradfield remains under 100 once all remaining votes are processed then there will be an automatic recount. Liberal sources concede that is the likely outcome.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T06:14:52.891Z">at 7.14am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:27:25.510Z">5.27am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Eye-watering\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 CBA\uc2  shares break all-time highs\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest company and the world\u8217\'20\'19s most expensive bank stock has broken its price record three times in a month.\par \par \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  shares spiked almost 2% to $172.92 on Friday morning, thanks to broader cyclical flows into the Australian share market and narrowing bets on an interest rate cut next week.\par \par \uc2 However, as the price snapped back by the afternoon, analysts say the stock is overvalued and its profit growth cannot justify its lofty valuation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s eye-wateringly overvalued still,\u8221\'20\'1D Capital.com\u8217\'20\'19s senior market analyst, Kyle Rodda, told \uc2 AAP\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s priced like a high-value, growth tech stock in the United States, when it\u8217\'20\'19s a bank that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really have strong growth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 CBA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s price-to-earnings ratio, a key-measure of a stock\u8217\'20\'19s value, is around 28.7, while \uc2 NAB\uc2 , \uc2 Westpac\uc2  and ANZ \u8211\'20\'13 still considered expensive \u8211\'20\'13 range from the low-to-mid teens.\par \par \uc2 Its earnings ratio is on par with \uc2 Facebook\uc2  owner \uc2 Meta\uc2 , at 25.7, but lower than the world\u8217\'20\'19s two largest companies \uc2 Apple\uc2 , at roughly 33, or chipmaker \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 45.3, and is currently more than double investment giant \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 13.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:33:25.202Z">at 5.33am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:12:15.675Z">5.12am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Blue Mountains drinking water contaminated by Pfas since early 90s\par \par \uc2 Residents in a tourist hotspot have been exposed to cancer-causing \u8220\'20\'1Cforever chemicals\u8221\'20\'1D in their water supply for decades, a major investigation has revealed.\par \par \uc2 Pfas, known as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.health.gov.au/topics/environmental-health/about/environmental-toxins-and-contaminants/pfas#:~:text=Per%2D%20and%20polyfluoroalkyl%20substances%20(PFAS)%20are%20synthetic%20chemicals%20that,doing%20about%20PFAS%20in%20Australia."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (Pfas)}}}\uc2 , are a group of 15,000 highly toxic, synthetic chemicals resistant to heat, stains and grease, dubbed \u8220\'20\'1Cforever chemicals\u8221\'20\'1D because of their inability to break down.\par \par \uc2 High-level contamination was detected in the drinking water catchment serving 30,000 people in the Blue Mountains in NSW in mid-2024.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.waternsw.com.au/community-news/media-releases/2025/first-stage-of-waternsw-investigation-into-blue-mountains-pfas-completed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WaterNSW investigation}}}\uc2  released on Friday zeroed in on three potential sources of contamination, dating as far back as 33 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Two separate motor vehicle accident sites on the Great Western Highway in 1992 and 2002 near the Medlow Bath township and the Medlow Bath Rural Fire Brigade station were all identified as possible source of contamination in the Adams Creek and Medlow catchments.\par \par \uc2 Test samples at all three sites revealed the chemical compound signature consistent with the historical use of firefighting foam which contained Pfas, which were banned nationwide in 2007.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Australian Associated Press\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:52:01.302Z">at 5.52am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:59:26.575Z">4.59am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 NSW man arrested after information over alleged possession of child abuse material\par \par \uc2 A 33-year-old man has been charged over the alleged possession of child abuse and bestiality material after a search warrant was issued at a home at Lavington in southern New South Wales this morning.\par \par \uc2 Officers initiated inquiries on Monday into the allegations, acting on information provided by State Crime Command\u8217\'20\'19s Child Internet Exploitation Unit (CIEU).\par \par \uc2 At about 6.20am today, police attended the home where an electronic device was seized and taken for further examination. The man was arrested at the home and taken to Albury police station where he was charged with possess child abuse material, person possesses bestiality material and use carriage etc to access child abuse material.\par \par \uc2 The man has been refused bail to appear in Albury local court today.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:01:52.291Z">at 5.01am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:51:43.807Z">4.51am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Death cap mushrooms detected in samples from dehydrator, expert tells Erin Patterson\u8217\'20\'19s murder trial\par \par \uc2 Back to Erin Patterson\u8217\'20\'19s murder trial.\par \par \uc2 A scientific expert has told the trial that tests detected death cap mushrooms in two out of seven test tubes containing samples from a dehydrator dumped by Patterson in the days after the mushroom lunch.\par \par \uc2 He says the positive test tube results had a 99% similarity to the DNA of death cap mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 Dr David Lovelock worked as a virologist at Agriculture Victoria in August 2023 when the Department of Health requested he analyse leftovers of the beef wellington, the court hears.\par \par \uc2 Victoria police later provided him with a fruit platter, a jug of gravy and seven test tubes containing debris from dehydrator dumped by Patterson in the days after the lunch.\par \par \uc2 Under questioning by prosecutor Nanette Rogers SC , Lovelock says only DNA from button mushrooms were found in ziplock bags containing the beef wellington leftovers.\par \par \uc2 The court previously heard that two days after the lunch a police officer had fished leftovers of the beef wellington meal from a bin at Patterson\u8217\'20\'19s property in Leongatha, with her permission.\par \par \uc2 The court has adjourned for the day. The trial will resume from 10.30am on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T04:00:15.394Z">at 5.00am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:45:24.793Z">4.45am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Murder-accused mum\u8217\'20\'19s matter in court after house fire\par \par \uc2 A mother of five charged with murder has had her matter heard in court, more than a week after a horror house fire that killed three of her children.\par \par \uc2 The 36-year-old remains in hospital after the Toowoomba blaze west of Brisbane that devastated her family.\par \par \uc2 She did not appear in Brisbane magistrates court when her matter was heard on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The mother had been airlifted to a Brisbane hospital in a critical condition and placed under police guard after the family home went up in flames on 7 May.\par \par \uc2 Neighbours helped six people escape the Toowoomba house fire in the early hours, with some reportedly smashing windows to assist the family\u8217\'20\'19s rescue.\par \par \uc2 A body, believed to be the woman\u8217\'20\'19s nine-year-old son, was found in the charred remains. Two of the woman\u8217\'20\'19s daughters, aged four and seven, later died from their injuries after being airlifted to Brisbane hospitals along with their mother.\par \par \uc2 A 34-year-old man \u8211\'20\'13 the father of some of the children \u8211\'20\'13 and the woman\u8217\'20\'19s two other sons, aged 18 and 11, also escaped the house. The man underwent surgery on his arm while the 18 and 11-year-olds were treated for minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 Counsel representing the woman on Friday said they were seeking a brief of evidence, which magistrate Peter Saggers listed for 21 July.\par \par \uc2 The 36-year-old\u8217\'20\'19s case will next be mentioned in Toowoomba on 19 August. She was remanded in custody.\par \par \uc2 The mother has been charged with three counts of murder and attempted murder along with one count of arson.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:49:40.681Z">at 4.49am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:37:19.557Z">4.37am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Tim Wilson \u8216\'20\'18very relaxed\u8217\'20\'19 about Goldstein election result\par \par \uc2 As the vote count in Goldstein tightens further, Liberal Tim Wilson says he is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery relaxed about the result\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Wilson was declared the projected winner of Goldstein last week, a seat he lost at the 2022 election, but the gap in votes narrowed considerably in recent days, with the former assistant minister now leading by just 254 votes.\par \par \uc2 Wilson, who yesterday urged scrutineers to help knock out invalid votes for the teal independent Zoe Daniel , is keeping a close eye on the count:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m extremely grateful to my wonderful scrutineers that are ensuring this vote count has integrity and will deliver the will of the people. And I\u8217\'20\'19m very relaxed about the result because it is the will of the people of Goldstein.\par \par \uc2 So far, there are 474 remaining votes ready to be counted. But this number may increase by 50-100 votes. Postal votes received before midnight will be counted on Monday. Some of those votes are coming from as far away as Nairobi.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T07:15:22.463Z">at 8.15am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:23:35.647Z">4.23am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 John Pesutto issues statement after being ordered to pay $2.3m in legal costs\par \par \uc2 Former Victorian opposition leader, John Pesutto , has issued a statement after the federal court ordered him to pay more than $2.3m in costs after losing a defamation case brought by Liberal MP Moira Deeming .\par \par \uc2 In the statement posted on X, the Hawthorn MP said he would take time to review the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision with his family and advisers:\par \par \uc2 I reiterate that I am determined to continue serving the people of my electorate of Hawthorn and the people of Victoria for as long as they will have me.\par \par \uc2 I am grateful for the support I am receiving from the community and am hopeful with this support that I will be able to fulfil these obligations and continue serving the people of Victoria.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Liberal sources have said the ruling could force Pesutto to declare bankruptcy, which would disqualify him from being a member of parliament and trigger a byelection in his seat of Hawthorn, held by a slim margin of 1.74%.\par \par \uc2 They say Liberal figures have been exploring the possibility of the party or donors covering Pesutto\u8217\'20\'19s costs in an effort to prevent voters from going back to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:31:32.334Z">at 4.31am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:10:01.229Z">4.10am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Brutal boulevard for \uc2 Opera Australia\uc2  as it posts a $10m loss\par \par \uc2 Opera Australia\uc2  has plunged to an operating deficit of $10.6m for 2024, thanks in part to its production of Sunset Boulevard.\par \par \uc2 The musical starring Sarah Brightman did indeed prove to be a brutal boulevard for the company \u8211\'20\'13{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/stage/article/2024/may/30/sunset-boulevard-review-sarah-brightman-disappoints-in-andrew-lloyd-webbers-mawkish-musical"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  it saw poor reviews}}}\uc2  and Brightman herself unable to perform after an injury.\par \par \uc2 The annual financial reports released Friday reveal the sheer extent of the financial pressures the company was under as the co-production wrapped in Melbourne in the second half of 2024.\par \par \uc2 One of the company\u8217\'20\'19s directors offered \uc2 Opera Australia\uc2  a $6m interest-free loan last September, of which the company drew down $2m that was repaid by the end of December.\par \par \uc2 Sunset Boulevard did manage to return to the black following a more successful run in Sydney, but as widely expected it missed box office forecasts.\par \par \uc2 But the 2024 operating figures for Australia\u8217\'20\'19s national opera company are even worse than in 2023, when it returned a loss of $7.8m.\par \par \uc2 Total box office was $50.7m, making up just under half the company\u8217\'20\'19s $106m revenue.\par \par \uc2 The company also relies on taxpayer funding, with $23.9m from \uc2 Creative Australia\uc2 , $3.8m from Create NSW and another $1m from Creative Victoria.\par \par \uc2 Opera Australia\uc2  also raided its savings in 2024 to boost its bottom line by $4m, making for an overall deficit of $6.1m.\par \par \uc2 - Australia Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T03:16:42.395Z">at 4.16am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:55:20.263Z">3.55am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Gambling company pays $500k fine for spam breaches\par \par \uc2 Online gambling company \uc2 PointsBet\uc2  has paid a $500,000 fine and been subjected to an enforceable undertaking by the media regulator, which has accused it of breaching spam and self-exclusion laws.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Communications and Media Authority\uc2  (\uc2 Acma\uc2 ) has accused \uc2 Pointsbet\uc2  of sending 508 marketing messages to people who had self-excluded themselves from gambling.\par \par \uc2 Acma\uc2  has also accused the company of sending 705 emails containing a direct link to betting products without including an unsubscribe function. An investigation found a further seven emails were sent \u8220\'20\'1Cwithout receipt consent\u8221\'20\'1D along with 90 \u8220\'20\'1Ccommercial texts that did not have sender contact information\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 While the self-excluded people were not able to gamble, the authority\u8217\'20\'19s chair, Nerida O\u8217\'20\'19Loughlin , said there was no excuse for gambling companies that breach the law and contact people experiencing gambling harms.\par \par \uc2 It is deeply concerning that these failures have impacted \uc2 PointsBet\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s customers, some of whom had taken proactive steps to exclude themselves from online wagering.\par \par \uc2 Wagering providers must also appropriately identify where messages promote or advertise their services and ensure that those messages comply with the rules, including the obligation to promote the national self-exclusion register.\par \par \uc2 The $500,800 fine relates only to the breaches of spam laws.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:58:52.687Z">at 3.58am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:38:37.392Z">3.38am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Aussie shares hit three-month highs as rate cut looms\par \par \uc2 The Australian share market has surged to its highest level in three months after economic data paved the way for interest rate cuts in Australia and the United States.\par \par \uc2 The S&P/ASX200 rallied by 60.1 points, or 0.72%, to 8,357.6, as the broader All Ordinaries jumped 61.4 points, or 0.72 per cent, to 8,591.3.\par \par \uc2 The rally followed a mixed Wall Street session, but the S&P500 is up 22.8% in 27 trading days, while the tech-heavy Nasdaq has surged roughly 30% since early April\u8217\'20\'19s Liberation Day sell-off.\par \par \uc2 Despite hotter than expected jobs figures on Thursday, money markets are still tipping a 97% chance the Reserve Bank will cut interest rates by 25 basis points next week, which will give equities room to push higher.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, US producer inflation slipped by the largest amount in five years, raising the likelihood of a cut in the United States.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:42:18.630Z">at 3.42am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:27:36.578Z">3.27am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Tim Wilson's margin over Zoe Daniel shrinks further\par \par \uc2 Another update from Goldstein, in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s south-eastern suburbs.\par \par \uc2 The Liberal candidate Tim Wilson says his margin over teal independent Zoe Daniel has now further shrunk to 258 votes , with 474 votes remaining. That remaining vote figure may grow, however, with postals still coming in until midnight.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s how an AEC spokesperson put it:\par \par \uc2 Postal votes can be received by the AEC until midnight tonight, so it\u8217\'20\'19s possible we won\u8217\'20\'19t have absolute final figures until Monday. For both seats our expectation is that we\u8217\'20\'19ll be receiving a couple of hundred additional votes before the deadline.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:32:49.153Z">at 3.32am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:05:29.689Z">3.05am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Tense wait as Goldstein and Bradfield counts inch forward\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re glued to the \uc2 Australian Electoral Commission\uc2  website hoping for the latest details on the vote count in Goldstein and Bradfield , perhaps come back later this afternoon. It\u8217\'20\'19s been pretty slow progress so far.\par \par \uc2 Scrutineers in Bradfield \u8211\'20\'13 where Liberal Gisele Kapterian leads by just 43 votes \u8211\'20\'13 expect to begin counting new votes from about midday.\par \par \uc2 In Goldstein, Liberal Tim Wilson \u8217\'20\'19s lead has dropped from 292 votes to 289. This morning, scrutineers conducted a second count of votes they dealt with yesterday. This likely explains the tiny change. They expect to deal with new votes at some stage later today.\par \par \uc2 So we\u8217\'20\'19ll get a better sense of how the votes are tracking later this afternoon. Postals can still come in until midnight. Votes received late today may be counted on Monday. If the vote remains very tight in Bradfield, they may be key.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:12:52.615Z">at 3.12am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T01:56:24.232Z">2.56am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Mushroom lunch trial hears from toxicologist\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re following Erin Patterson \u8217\'20\'19s triple murder trial today and will bring you updates.\par \par \uc2 Patterson, 50, faces three charges of murder and one charge of attempted murder relating to a beef wellington lunch she served at her house in Leongatha, regional Victoria, on 29 July 2023. She has pleaded not guilty to all charges.\par \par \uc2 The jury has been hearing more evidence this morning from Dr Dimitri Gerostamoulos , the head of forensic science and chief toxicologist at the \uc2 Victorian Institute of Forensic Medicine\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under cross-examination by Patterson\u8217\'20\'19s defence team, Gerostamoulos says there are \u8220\'20\'1Clots of variables\u8221\'20\'1D that need to be considered in determining the lethal dose of death cap mushroom toxins. He says factors like an individual\u8217\'20\'19s age, weight and health can influence their response to toxins.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Gerostamoulos told the court death cap mushroom toxins were discovered in samples taken from Patterson\u8217\'20\'19s father-in-law, Don Patterson , and Ian Wilkinson , the uncle of Patterson\u8217\'20\'19s estranged husband Simon Patterson .\par \par \uc2 The toxins were also uncovered in vegetable matter and debris found on a food dehydrator dumped by Patterson at a local tip days after the lethal lunch, Gerostamoulos told the court on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T01:59:47.811Z">at 2.59am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T01:16:08.012Z">2.16am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Firefighters down helmets as Victoria tax hike ticked off\par \par \uc2 Volunteer firefighters have left their posts as a controversial Victorian emergency services tax hike passed into law in the dead of night.\par \par \uc2 Regional communities were without local brigades on Thursday night after crews took themselves offline as parliament debated legislation to enact the emergency services and volunteers fund.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Country Fire Authority\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s acting chief officer, Garry Cook , said leaders were working to understand whether it was in protest against the incoming levy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe word strike, I probably wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t use that,\u8221\'20\'1D he told ABC Radio Melbourne on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 But what we\u8217\'20\'19ve done is had brigades book themselves off by saying that [they have] a shortage of crews available to respond.\par \par \uc2 The bill to establish the levy passed the Victorian parliament in the early hours of Friday after the state Labor government brokered a deal to secure crossbench support.\par \par \uc2 The levy will replace the fire services property levy from 1 July and expand coverage to other emergency and disaster bodies, including the State Emergency Service.\par \par \uc2 It was originally projected to lift the average annual bill for residential homeowners by $63 and $678 for primary producers, sparking protests from farmers and firefighters on the steps of state parliament.\par \par \uc2 But under last-minute changes, the Allan government agreed to lower the rate for primary production land and rebates for CFA and SES volunteers and life members.\par \par \uc2 Farmers will also be offered partial rebates but they are limited to those eligible for a temporary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://agriculture.vic.gov.au/farm-management/dry-seasons-and-drought-support"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drought support package}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The agriculture minister, Ros Spence , announced $15.9m to expand the program to another 13 local government areas, taking the total to 24.\par \par \uc2 The expanded levy was originally forecast to raise an extra $2.14bn over the next three financial years when unveiled in the mid-year budget update.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T02:05:09.706Z">at 3.05am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:59:26.685Z">1.59am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Labor calls for real rise in minimum wage\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government has advocated for an above-inflation rise in the minimum wage for nearly 3 million workers, ahead of the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 FWC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s) annual decision next month.\par \par \uc2 In this morning\u8217\'20\'19s submission to the \uc2 FWC\uc2 , the government said it \u8220\'20\'1Cbelieves workers should get ahead with a real wage increase\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The annual minimum wage determination affects 2.9 million employees. It\u8217\'20\'19s currently $24.10 per hour, or $915.90 a week.\par \par \uc2 Last year the \uc2 FWC\uc2  granted a 3.75% increase.\par \par \uc2 Inflation was 2.4% in the year to March, and 2.9% in underlying terms.\par \par \uc2 In line with longstanding Labor practice, the government did not nominate a figure.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, employer groups, such as the \uc2 Australian Chamber of Commerce and Industry\uc2 , are arguing for an increase of 2.5-2.6%, citing the steep rise in costs for businesses.\par \par \uc2 The small business lobby group, Cosboa, says the increase should be between 2% and 2.5%, with a strong preference for the lower end of that range as employers will also be up for an extra 0.5% in compulsory super from July.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the \uc2 ACTU\uc2  has advocated for an increase of 4.5% to help low-paid workers make up some of the lost ground to high cost of living over recent years.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  and employment and workplace relations minister Amanda Rishworth said their argument in favour of a real wage increase was \u8220\'20\'1Cboth economically responsible and fair\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 An increase in minimum and award wages is consistent with inflation sustainably remaining within the \uc2 RBA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s target band, and will provide further relief to lower income workers who are still doing it tough.\par \par \uc2 While nearly a quarter of employees are on awards that are affected by the change in the minimum wage decision, only a tiny fraction \u8211\'20\'13 fewer than 200,000 \u8211\'20\'13 are actually paid the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T01:30:27.457Z">at 2.30am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:45:05.104Z">1.45am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Court orders Pesutto to pay $2.3m in costs over Deeming defamation\par \par \uc2 Former Victorian opposition leader John Pesutto has been ordered to pay $2.3m in costs after losing a defamation case brought by Liberal MP Moira Deeming .\par \par \uc2 Federal court registrar Alison Legge made the ruling during a short hearing on Friday. She said:\par \par \uc2 It is uncontroversial that the purpose of a costs order is not to punish the unsuccessful party. The purpose \u8211\'20\'13 no more and no less \u8211\'20\'13 is to compensate the successful party. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/bankruptcy-fears-john-pesutto-moira-deeming-defamation-costs-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Former Victorian Liberal leader John Pesutto ordered to pay $2.3m in costs over Moira Deeming defamation }}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:50:29.984Z">at 1.50am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:38:43.224Z">1.38am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Sophisticated phishing scam impersonates \uc2 Australia Post\par \par \uc2 People are being urged to be on alert as a sophisticated scam is circulating in text messages and emails, impersonating one of the country\u8217\'20\'19s largest organisations.\par \par \uc2 Dubbed \u8220\'20\'1CDarcula\u8221\'20\'1D, the phishing scam sends out fraudulent messages claiming that a delivery from \uc2 Australia Post\uc2  failed due to an invalid postcode.\par \par \uc2 It lures recipients into clicking malicious links, designed to steal personal information.\par \par \uc2 The scam uses advanced techniques to bypass telco and network filters, allowing it to spread over iMessage and Rich Communication Services.\par \par \uc2 It comes as new data released by the postal giant reveals that more than 90% of Australians have been targeted by scammers in their lifetime.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, 74% of people have reported that scams commonly impersonate shipping and parcel delivery.\par \par \uc2 This is a worrying statistic, according to Adam Cartwright, chief information security officer at \uc2 Australia Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said it has never been more important to rely on trusted channels when managing deliveries.\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re expecting a parcel, don\u8217\'20\'19t click on suspicious links or respond to unexpected messages, always check the [AusPost] app first.\par \par \uc2 Australians reported close to 250,000 scams last year, with financial losses totalling $318.8m according to the National Anti-Scam Centre\u8217\'20\'19s Scamwatch service.\par \par \uc2 Phone scams had the highest overall losses for contact method with $107.2m reported lost across 2,179 reporters.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:55:30.286Z">at 1.55am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:21:23.333Z">1.21am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Ben Roberts-Smith\uc2  loses bid to overturn defamation loss\par \par \uc2 Ben Roberts-Smith\uc2  has lost his appeal against a defamation case ruling, with three justices of the federal court agreeing he was not defamed by Nine newspapers and journalists Nick McKenzie and Chris Masters when they published reports in 2018 which claimed he had committed war crimes.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:44:18.907Z">at 1.44am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:16:07.629Z">1.16am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 eSafety chief faces new lawsuits from X and \uc2 Telegram\uc2  over rules on child abuse material\par \par \uc2 The Australian online safety regulator, the eSafety commissioner, is facing two new lawsuits from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s X and \uc2 Telegram\uc2  over moves to force the platforms to comply with regulations around child abuse material.\par \par \uc2 Telegram\uc2  is challenging in the federal court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/feb/24/australian-esafety-telegram-fine-reporting-delay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a nearly $1m fine issued in February}}}\uc2  for failing to respond on time to questions about what the company does to tackle terrorism and child abuse material on its platform. \uc2 Telegram\uc2  has argued it complied with the reporting requirements.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the second challenge of such a fine, with a similar case launched by X still ongoing.\par \par \uc2 This week X has also separately launched a legal challenge related to eSafety standards around {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/jun/21/australia-esafety-commissioner-child-abuse-detection-online-safety"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 what action platforms need to take to stop child abuse material on their platforms}}}\uc2 , which are due to come into effect next month.\par \par \uc2 An eSafety spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 eSafety is aware X has filed a judicial review in the federal court as to whether eSafety\u8217\'20\'19s relevant electronic services (RES) standard should apply to its X platform. We are assessing the documents filed and will consider next steps accordingly.\par \par \uc2 The RES standard requires online platforms to take meaningful action to tackle the worst of the worst online content, including child exploitation and abuse material.\par \par \uc2 eSafety is prioritising its efforts on ensuring all platforms and services meet their obligations under the Online Safety Act to address high-impact and harmful content to keep Australians safe online.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:25:00.704Z">at 1.25am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:06:30.885Z">1.06am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Ley says new deal with Nationals shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be rushed amid internal push to scrap net zero\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader, \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2 , says progress on a new cooperation agreement with the Nationals will take time, promising productive talks amid an internal push to ditch net zero policies and even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theaustralian.com.au/nation/coalition-agreement-not-a-foregone-conclusion-amid-climate-policy-rift/news-story/d71e8270cd0c816bafad4b7c345da4a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end the longstanding coalition with the Liberals}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ley met with the Nationals leader, \uc2 David Littleproud\uc2 , in Albury on Thursday for the first formal talks between the pair since the Coalition was thrashed in the 3 May election.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story: <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/sussan-ley-says-new-agreement-with-nationals-will-take-time-amid-internal-push-to-ditch-net-zero"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sussan Ley says new agreement with Nationals will take time amid internal push to ditch net zero}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:14:44.830Z">at 1.14am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:38:10.516Z">12.38am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Chemical probe in pipeline amid clean water scrutiny\par \par \uc2 Residents in a New South Wales tourist hotspot are awaiting the latest in a string of investigations of cancer-causing chemicals in their water supply.\par \par \uc2 Pfas, or per and polyfluoroalkyl substances, are a group of 15,000 highly toxic, synthetic chemicals resistant to heat, stains and grease, dubbed \u8220\'20\'1Cforever chemicals\u8221\'20\'1D because of their inability to break down.\par \par \uc2 High-level contamination was detected in the drinking water catchment serving 30,000 people in the NSW Blue Mountains in mid-2024.\par \par \uc2 Pfas levels were found to be about 300 times higher than Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s main drinking water source but they still met Australian guidelines for safe drinking water.\par \par \uc2 A WaterNSW investigation into Blue Mountains drinking water is set to be released on Friday.\par \par \uc2 John Dee , a veteran environmental activist who started the group Stop Pfas, has urged the buck-passing between various agencies such as Sydney Water, the \uc2 NSW Environmental Protection Authority\uc2  and WaterNSW to stop.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been shown that \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2  and WaterNSW have not been fully transparent with the Pfas testing of our local drinking water.\par \par \uc2 Dee has called for a single, independent statewide authority with comprehensive responsibility for overseeing Pfas contamination management and removal.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:34:23.723Z">at 1.34am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:25:46.198Z">12.25am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Homes over $1m proliferate across country\par \par \uc2 One in three Australian homes are now worth $1m or more, a share that\u8217\'20\'19s tripled over the past decade as surging demand and slow-growing supply worsen housing affordability, new data shows.\par \par \uc2 Ten years ago, just one in 10 homes around the country had broken the million-dollar threshold, but that has soared, led by the capital cities, where four in 10 are now over $1m.\par \par \uc2 In regional Australia, two in 10 have cracked that barrier, or nearly 20%, where only 0.5% had reached that level a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Cotality\u8217\'20\'19s head of research, Eliza Owen , said the data out today reflected that Australia was a wealthy nation where some could afford to spend millions on homes, but high prices had driven up household debt and priced younger generations out of home ownership. She said:\par \par \uc2 The rate of home ownership has gradually declined over time, particularly among younger, low-income households where income cannot keep pace with growth \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 With values expected to continue rising on the back of rate falls in 2025, the wealth divide between homeowners and non-homeowners is also likely to expand.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll get a sense of just how far interest rates will fall on Tuesday when the Reserve Bank meets and decides whether to deliver a second cut this year.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney, nearly two in three homes have surpassed $1m in value, or about six in 10. For Brisbane it\u8217\'20\'19s about four in 10, Melbourne is just over three in 10, and it\u8217\'20\'19s just under three in 10 in Perth and Adelaide.\par \par \uc2 At the other end of the scale, Hobart went backwards from two in 10 to one in 10, while Darwin has held steady with less than one in 50 cracking $1m.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:40:35.910Z">at 12.40am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:11:51.886Z">12.11am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 PM bound for Rome for papal mass and talks with world leaders\par \par \uc2 After a flying visit to Jakarta on Thursday, Anthony Albanese is on his way to Rome on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister met with Indonesia\u8217\'20\'19s president, \uc2 Prabowo Subianto\uc2  , for the first time, part of his first international trip since his 3 May election victory. The visit to Rome for the inaugural mass of Pope Leo XVI was added on after the papal conclave wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 Despite the historic nature of events at the Vatican on Sunday, the mass might be overshadowed by planned meetings with world leaders. Albanese has flagged he intends to hold pull aside meetings, an agenda which could include talks with French president \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  and Canadian prime minister \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 It appears unlikely Albanese will meet US president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Signals from the White House suggest the American delegation will be led by Vice-president \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  . That could mean the first talks between Albanese and Trump happen next month during the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders meeting in Canada.\par \par \uc2 A possible visit to the White House is also being speculated as part of that trip.\par \par \uc2 Albanese this week politely declined an offer of honorary citizenship from the Italian city of Barletta, the birthplace of his late father, over fears the gesture could conflict with Australia\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional rules on foreign allegiance.\par \par \uc2 Barletta, about four hours from Rome, is not part of Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s trip. His father, Carlo, lived there until his death in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:47:43.595Z">at 12.47am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:04:08.019Z">12.04am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Woman charged after alleged bomb threats sent to Queensland childcare centres\par \par \uc2 A 50-year-old woman has been charged over five bomb hoaxes sent to childcare centres and a primary school in Brisbane yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Queensland Police allege a series of threatening messages were sent to childcare centres and a primary school in Bayside early Thursday morning. They also allege the woman was not known to or connected with the centres or school.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday afternoon, detectives charged a Cleveland woman with \u8220\'20\'1Cfive counts of making a bomb hoax \u8211\'20\'13 falsely inform bomb present\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Police investigations found there was no risk to any children or the community. The woman will appear before Cleveland magistrates court on 5 June.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:09:29.624Z">at 12.09am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:50:28.948Z">11.50pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Where Waters may take the Greens\par \par \uc2 One of those new party leaders is, of course, \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  , who took the mantle of Greens\u8217\'20\'19 leader this week.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s said she was reluctant to become party leader ahead of some more high-profile names. But she is regarded by colleagues as smart, warm and friendly, and \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps crucially \u8211\'20\'13 is seen as pragmatic by Labor counterparts with whom she will have to deal if she wants to help the government be bolder.\par \par \uc2 Our chief political correspondent, Tom McIlroy , assesses where she might take the party. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/may/16/larissa-waters-vows-politics-with-heart-but-dont-expect-compromise-on-core-greens-principles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Larissa Waters vows politics with \u8216\'20\'18heart\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 but don\u8217\'20\'19t expect compromise on core Greens principles}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:55:35.535Z">at 11.55pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:45:32.502Z">11.45pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 What the party leadership changes could mean to Australian politics \u8211\'20\'13 podcast\par \par \uc2 The Liberal party elected its first female leader in \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  this week, but she\u8217\'20\'19s already fighting to keep the factional sharks at bay. Same goes for the Nationals, who\u8217\'20\'19ve re-elected \uc2 David Littleproud\uc2  in a leadership challenge that revealed deep divisions. The Greens also elected a new leader on Thursday, but will that mean a change in strategy after their stinging election loss?\par \par \uc2 Reged Ahmad talks to head of newsroom Mike Ticher , national news editor Jo Tovey and chief political correspondent Tom McIlroy about whether changes in leadership could mean a change in our politics.\par \par \uc2 Listen to the podcast here: <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2025/may/16/newsroom-edition-three-leadership-contests-and-the-future-of-australian-politics-full-story-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Newsroom edition: three leadership contests and the future of Australian politics - podcast}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:48:41.865Z">at 11.48pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:30:34.027Z">11.30pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Court to rule on John Pesutto costs over Moira Deeming defamation case\par \par \uc2 Former Victorian opposition leader John Pesutto will learn today what costs he will have to pay after losing a defamation case brought by Liberal MP Moira Deeming .\par \par \uc2 Federal court registrar Alison Legge will rule on costs during a short hearing this morning, which begins at 10.30am.\par \par \uc2 It comes after the court in December found Pesutto defamed Deeming by falsely implying she sympathised with neo-Nazis and white supremacists, and ordered he pay $300,000 in damages. According to several senior Liberal sources, he is also yet to pay hundreds of thousands to his legal team.\par \par \uc2 In an earlier hearing, Deeming\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers had said they were seeking $2.4m from Pesutto, mostly to pay back a loan that the New South Wales property developer Hilton Grugeon offered the MP to cover her legal expenses.\par \par \uc2 Both parties hired top defamation barristers for the weeks-long trial, with Sue Chrysanthou SC representing Deeming and Dr Matthew Collins KC representing Pesutto.\par \par \uc2 Liberal sources say the ruling could force Pesutto to declare bankruptcy, which would disqualify him from being a member of parliament and trigger a byelection in his seat of Hawthorn, held by a slim margin of 1.74%.\par \par \uc2 They say Liberal figures have been exploring the possibility of the party or donors covering Pesutto\u8217\'20\'19s costs in an effort to stave off a byelection.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T23:05:02.509Z">at 12.05am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:25:56.736Z">11.25pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 As few as \u8216\'20\'1830, 40, 50\u8217\'20\'19 votes still to come in by midnight in some seats \u8211\'20\'13 AEC\par \par \uc2 Australian Electoral Commissioner Jeff Pope was speaking on ABC Radio National this morning.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks out from the federal election, there are only a few thousand votes still expected to come in by midnight tonight \u8211\'20\'13 that is only as few as \u8220\'20\'1C30, 40, 50\u8221\'20\'1D in some seats.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the number of informal votes, Pope said \u8220\'20\'1Cit is looking pretty similar to previous elections for the House of Representatives\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s coming in nationally I think around about sort of 5%. You\u8217\'20\'19ll get some divisions a bit below that, some a bit above, a few well above, regrettably. But it\u8217\'20\'19s nationally coming in around about the same as what it has for the last few federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:31:55.699Z">at 11.31pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:10:29.765Z">11.10pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Ley says she\u8217\'20\'19ll bring \u8216\'20\'18different approach\u8217\'20\'19 than Dutton to Liberal leadership\par \par \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  says she and former opposition leader \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  have different leadership styles and personalities.\par \par \uc2 The new opposition leader appeared on Today a short while ago and was asked if she is \u8220\'20\'1Cpartly responsible\u8221\'20\'1D for the Liberal\u8217\'20\'19s recent election loss as Dutton\u8217\'20\'19s deputy.\par \par \uc2 Ley responded:\par \par \uc2 We all have to accept responsibility and I don\u8217\'20\'19t step back from that, and after every election we look at what went wrong, at what we didn\u8217\'20\'19t do that we should have done, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll do that in an open and transparent way \u8211\'20\'13 and that will of course happen.\par \par \uc2 But now is about the future, it is about the next election, it is about the team that I lead, and it is about optimism and a sense of purpose, and always remembering that we are elected \u8230\'20\'26 I\u8217\'20\'19m elected here in my seat representing my community. My colleagues and their own individual life experiences bring tremendous passion and tremendous determination for that future that we want to provide for the Australian people. As I said, to get up every day to work for them.\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  and I have different styles, we\u8217\'20\'19re different personalities, and I will bring a different approach to my leadership.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-16T00:41:50.526Z">at 1.41am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:02:55.371Z">11.02pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Ley\u8217\'20\'19s elevation to leadership about \u8216\'20\'18so much more\u8217\'20\'19 than being female, she says\par \par \uc2 Asked what took the Liberal party \u8220\'20\'1Cso long to elect a female leader,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  said her appointment \u8220\'20\'1Cis about so much more than that\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader was speaking on Today earlier this morning:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m incredibly humbled to have the endorsement of my party room just a few days ago to be the leader of the Liberal party, it\u8217\'20\'19s an incredible honour. Now, Karl, people reflect on the female aspect and I understand that and I want to say it\u8217\'20\'19s significant, but my appointment is about much more than that. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t meet the expectations of the Australian people at the last election. We have to change, we have to step up, we have to have a fresh approach.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:09:02.651Z">at 11.09pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:51:16.814Z">10.51pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 D-day for \uc2 Ben Roberts-Smith\uc2 's appeal\par \par \uc2 After a marathon legal battle spanning seven years, disgraced special forces veteran \uc2 Ben Roberts-Smith\uc2  will learn this morning whether he has cleared his name of war crime allegations, \uc2 Australian Associated Press\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The Victoria Cross recipient sued Nine newspapers and journalists Nick McKenzie and Chris Masters for defamation over their reports in 2018 which claimed he had committed war crimes.\par \par \uc2 But Justice Anthony Besanko found the claims that Roberts-Smith {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jun/01/ben-roberts-smith-loses-defamation-case-with-judge-saying-newspapers-established-truth-of-some-murders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was responsible }}}\uc2 for the murder of four unarmed civilians when deployed in Afghanistan were substantially true.\par \par \uc2 But Roberts-Smith launched an appeal against findings, which was heard in the federal court over 10 days in February 2024.\par \par \uc2 More than a year on, three justices will hand down their decision on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 If Roberts-Smith is unsuccessful, only the high court could overturn the war criminal findings.\par \par \uc2 The 46-year-old steadfastly denies any wrongdoing and has not been criminally charged.\par \par \uc2 If his appeal is dismissed, it could clear a path for a criminal investigation by the Australian federal police and the Office of the Special Investigator.\par \par \uc2 Roberts-Smith could be forced to pay out tens of millions of dollars over the legal saga after the cost of the defamation proceedings was tipped to exceed $25m back in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Taking into account the subsequent appeal and interlocutory issues, the final bill could be far higher.\par \par \uc2 Today the full court will also rule on Roberts-Smith\u8217\'20\'19s push to reopen the appeal over a secret recording he claims reveals an alleged miscarriage of justice.\par \par \uc2 In a taped call, McKenzie can be heard telling Roberts-Smith\u8217\'20\'19s ex-lover that two crucial witnesses were \u8220\'20\'1Cactively briefing us on his legal strategy\u8221\'20\'1D during the initial trial.\par \par \uc2 But the journalist has denied claims he obtained privileged information while investigating Roberts-Smith, whose reputation was tarnished by his reports in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Roberts-Smith rose to prominence in 2011 after he was awarded Australia\u8217\'20\'19s highest military honour, the Victoria Cross, for single-handedly taking out machine-gun posts to protect pinned-down colleagues in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:57:05.803Z">at 10.57pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:43:27.804Z">10.43pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Fresh delay for Australian-made rocket launch\par \par \uc2 The first Australian-made rocket was set to be test launched into orbit from north Queensland today after being pushed back by a system issue, but has been delayed again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDuring final launch preparations last night, an electrical fault triggered the system that opens the rocket\u8217\'20\'19s nose cone,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gspace.com/missions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gilmour Space Technologies}}}\uc2  said in a statement this morning. \u8220\'20\'1CThis happened before any fuel was loaded into the vehicle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 No one was injured, and there has been no damage to the rocket or launch pad. An investigation was under way into the cause of the issue, they said.\par \par \uc2 As a result, we\u8217\'20\'19ll be postponing this test launch campaign to fully understand what happened and make any necessary updates.\par \par \uc2 \u8203\'20\'0BThe Queensland-based company got the green light from the Australian Space Agency to launch the Eris TestFlight1 from 7:30am yesterday morning, but they had to halt plans after the team \u8220\'20\'1Cidentified an issue in the ground support system during overnight checks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There is no new test flight date yet.\par \par \uc2 Its launch would be the first orbital launch from Australia in more than 50 years and the first Australian-made rocket to attempt orbit.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:51:30.708Z">at 10.51pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:30:07.067Z">10.30pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Only 43 votes between Bradfield candidates as count continues\par \par \uc2 An update on the narrowing race in electorates yet to be called.\par \par \uc2 In Bradfield, Liberal Gisele Kapterian leads independent Nicolette Boele by just 43 votes, with 303 left to be counted.\par \par \uc2 And Liberal Tim Wilson continues with a tight lead of 292 over independent Zoe Daniel in Goldstein. There are 717 votes still to be counted.\par \par \uc2 Today is the last day for postals to be received, so there may be a handful still to come.\par \par \uc2 We will keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:01:39.586Z">at 11.01pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:23:48.075Z">10.23pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Greens leader \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  was asked whether she could bring independent senator \uc2 Lidia Thorpe\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cback into the fold\u8221\'20\'1D on ABC News Breakfast a short while ago.\par \par \uc2 Waters said:\par \par \uc2 Look, I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure that\u8217\'20\'19s what Lydia is after. There was a bit of a media circus about that yesterday \u8230\'20\'26 Lydia is a really strong person and I respect the work she does. But I don\u8217\'20\'19t think there\u8217\'20\'19s any truth to the suggestion she wants back in.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s doing strong work in the way she\u8217\'20\'19s doing it now.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:19:11.865Z">10.19pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  decries poll campaign\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18tokenistic\u8217\'20\'19 treatment of violence against women\par \par \uc2 Greens leader \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cdismayed\u8221\'20\'1D that violence against women was addressed as \u8220\'20\'1Can afterthought\u8221\'20\'1D in recent election campaigns.\par \par \uc2 She is speaking on ABC News Breakfast. Asked about whether there will be a focus on preventing violence against women under her leadership, Waters says:\par \par \uc2 I was really dismayed that it didn\u8217\'20\'19t get a look-in, until, as you say, the last week of the campaign with what felt to me like some fairly tokenistic announcements. They were really small financial commitments and it just felt like a bit of an afterthought. It\u8217\'20\'19s not an afterthought to ordinary people. It\u8217\'20\'19s not an afterthought to the vast legions of women who are facing violence.\par \par \uc2 We need to make sure there\u8217\'20\'19s no women\u8217\'20\'19s refuge that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the funding that it needs to help everyone that needs help. We need to make sure those women\u8217\'20\'19s legal services are fully funded, we got to work on prevention, we got to change the attitudes that\u8217\'20\'19s leading to this crisis. That\u8217\'20\'19s a job for all of us. But government funding has a lot to do there.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:10:40.073Z">at 11.10pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:13:17.264Z">10.13pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  says climate and nature at core of Greens\par \par \uc2 New Greens leader \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  is speaking on ABC News Breakfast this morning.\par \par \uc2 Asked if the party will \u8220\'20\'1Creturn to its roots\u8221\'20\'1D under her leadership after its campaign made \u8220\'20\'1Cmore noise about renters, about dental\u8221\'20\'1D in the recent election, Waters says:\par \par \uc2 We have always had a broad policy platform \u8230\'20\'26 We have always had a number of priorities. Climate and nature is at our core.\par \par \uc2 My personal priorities have always been acting on the climate crisis and protecting biodiversity. That\u8217\'20\'19s why I was an environmental lawyer, that\u8217\'20\'19s what drives me personally. I\u8217\'20\'19m a strong feminist and gender equality and ending gender violence against women. But making sure that everyone can have the things they need. And have good healthcare, have good-funded schools, and you know make sure we\u8217\'20\'19re just a bit of a kinder and a less unified \u8230\'20\'26 community that\u8217\'20\'19s not struggling so much.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:37:34.100Z">at 10.37pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:05:20.791Z">10.05pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thanks to Martin Farrer for kicking off the blog. I\u8217\'20\'19ll be keeping you posted from here \u8211\'20\'13 let\u8217\'20\'19s go.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T20:53:34.516Z">9.53pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Woman allegedly splashed with chemicals in Sydney attack\par \par \uc2 A young woman has been injured in an alleged unprovoked chemical attack in Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s north, New South Wales police have said.\par \par \uc2 They said an unknown woman had approached a 27-year-old woman at the intersection of Norfolk Road and Callistemon Close in North Epping about 6.15pm on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The stranger allegedly splashed an unknown liquid from a cup she was holding at the 27-year-old, which landed on her face, clothing and headphones, police said in a statement yesterday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 They said the 27-year-old contacted them and then attended Ryde hospital, where she was treated for a chemical burn.\par \par \uc2 Police said they established a crime scene and patrolled the area but were unable to find the alleged attacker.\par \par \uc2 As part of their inquiries, police have said they would like to speak to a woman who may be able to assist. Police have described the woman as being of Asian appearance and said she was seen wearing a light-coloured cap, a N95 white face mask, black jumper and dark long pants.\par \par \uc2 She was holding a silver-coloured metal cup in her hand, police said.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T22:12:39.899Z">at 11.12pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T20:40:16.232Z">9.40pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Energy bill hardship targeted in proposed changes\par \par \uc2 In Victoria, the Essential Services Commission is expected to propose a suite of reforms today aimed at easing energy hardship.\par \par \uc2 The proposed changes will include obligations for retailers to move customers receiving assistance under the payment difficulty framework on to their cheapest plan, and address the \u8220\'20\'1Cloyalty penalty\u8221\'20\'1D where consumers on expensive contracts more than four years old must be switched to a reasonable price.\par \par \uc2 In response, the \uc2 Consumer Action Law Centre\uc2  said it strongly supported the draft decision, which would help the increasing number of Victorians struggling with energy costs and growing debt.\par \par \uc2 Consumer Action senior policy officer Eirene Tsolidis Noyce said:\par \par \uc2 People who call the national debt helpline tell us they often forego other expenses in order to meet their electricity costs \u8211\'20\'13 one of the proposals is that people experiencing energy poverty are identified and supported to find a more competitive offer and prevented from accruing larger debts.\par \par \uc2 The ESC\u8217\'20\'19s draft decision estimates Victorians could save up to $730 per year on electricity, and up to $1,890 per year on gas bills, by requiring retailers to automatically switch consumers in payment difficulty to their best offer. For those stuck on bad deals, the dollar amount is likely to be much higher.\par \par \uc2 These reforms will address some longstanding issues in the energy market, making it easier for Victorians to access an affordable energy price.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:46:23.384Z">at 10.46pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-15T20:35:03.791Z">9.35pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our live news blog. I\u8217\'20\'19m Martin Farrer with the top overnight stories and then my colleague Rafqa Touma will take over.\par \par \uc2 Our top story this morning concerns the meeting yesterday between the new Liberal leader, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/sussan-ley"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sussan Ley}}}\uc2  , and her Nationals counterpart, \uc2 David Littleproud\uc2  . Ley said progress on a new cooperation agreement with the Nationals would take time, but the problem areas promise to be a Nationals push to ditch net zero policies and even end the longstanding coalition with the Liberals. More coming up.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a huge day in the courts for disgraced special forces veteran \uc2 Ben Roberts-Smith\uc2 , who will learn today whether he has cleared his name of war crime allegations. The ruling by three justices is expected in Sydney this morning and we\u8217\'20\'19ll have their verdict as soon as it\u8217\'20\'19s out.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-15T21:20:12.941Z">at 10.20pm BST</time> \par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250515el5f0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
