\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 People interviewed by AI for jobs face discrimination risks, Australian study warns\b0\par\par\uc2 Josh Taylor Technology reporter \par \uc2 894 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2025\par 09:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Data used to train artificial intelligence may not \u8216\'20\'18reflect the demographic groups we have in Australia\u8217\'20\'19, says researcher\par \par \uc2 * <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/14/australia-news-live-anthony-albanese-jakarta-subianto-liberal-coalition-sussan-ley-energy-trade-tariffs-russia-defence-ntwnfb">Australia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2025/may/14/australia-news-live-anthony-albanese-jakarta-subianto-liberal-coalition-sussan-ley-energy-trade-tariffs-russia-defence-ntwnfb">Australia}}}\uc2  news live: latest politics updates</a>\par \par \uc2 * Get our <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl">breaking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl">breaking}}}\uc2  news email</a>, <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3">free"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3">free}}}\uc2  app</a> or <a href="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl">daily"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl">daily}}}\uc2  news podcast</a>\par \par \uc2 Job candidates being interviewed by AI recruiters risk being discriminated against if they speak with accents, or are living with a disability, a new study has warned.\par \par \uc2 This month, videos of job candidates interacting with at-times faulty AI video interviewers as part of the recruitment process have been widely shared on \uc2 TikTok\uc2 . <figure class="element element-embed" data-alt="AI video"> </figure>\par \par \uc2 The use of AI video recruitment has grown in recent years. \uc2 HireVue\uc2 , an AI recruitment software company used by many employers, reported in February that, among 4,000 employers surveyed worldwide, AI use in hiring had risen from 58% in 2024 to 72% in 2025.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up for Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s breaking news email}}}\par \par \uc2 Australian research {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1111/jols.12535"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published this month}}}\uc2  estimates the use is significantly lower \u8211\'20\'13 about 30% in Australian organisations \u8211\'20\'13 but expected to grow in the next five years.\par \par \uc2 However, the paper, by Dr Natalie Sheard, a \uc2 University of Melbourne\uc2  law school researcher, warns the use of AI hiring systems to screen and shortlist candidates risks discriminating against applicants, due to biases introduced by the limited datasets the AI models were trained on. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/may/09/crowdstrike-to-cut-jobs-and-use-ai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Tone deaf\u8217\'20\'19: US tech company responsible for global IT outage to cut jobs and use AI}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 In her research, Sheard interviewed 18 human resources professionals in Australia on their use of AI in recruitment. Of these, 13 had used AI recruitment systems in their companies, with the most common tool being CV analysis systems, followed by video interviewing systems.\par \par \uc2 Datasets based on limited information that often favours American data over international data presents a risk of bias in those AI systems, Sheard said. One AI systems company featured in Sheard\u8217\'20\'19s research, for example, has said only 6% of its job applicant training data came from Australia or New Zealand, and 36% of the job applicants in the training data were white.\par \par \uc2 The same company has said, according to the paper, that its word error rate for transcription of English-language speakers in the US is less than 10%, on average. However, when testing non-native English speakers with accents from other countries, that error rate increases to between 12 and 22%. The latter error rate is for non-native English speakers from China. <figure class="element element-embed" data-alt="AI video"> </figure>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe training data will come from the country where they\u8217\'20\'19re built \u8211\'20\'13 a lot of them are built in the US, so they don\u8217\'20\'19t reflect the demographic groups we have in Australia,\u8221\'20\'1D Sheard said.\par \par \uc2 Research participants told Sheard that non-native English speakers or those with a disability affecting their speech could find their words not being transcribed correctly, and would then not be rated highly by the recruitment algorithm.\par \par \uc2 This prompted two of the participants to seek reassurance from their software vendor that it did not disadvantage candidates with accents. Sheard said they were given reassurances that the AI was \u8220\'20\'1Creally good at understanding accents\u8221\'20\'1D but no evidence was provided to support this.\par \par \uc2 Sheard said there was little to no transparency about the AI interview systems used, for potential recruits, the recruiter, or the employer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the problem. In a human process, you can go back to the recruiter and ask for feedback, but what I found is recruiters don\u8217\'20\'19t even know why the decisions have been made, so they can\u8217\'20\'19t give feedback,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a problem for job seekers \u8230\'20\'26 It\u8217\'20\'19s really hard to pick where liability lies, but absolutely vendors and employers are legally liable for any discrimination by these systems.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 No case of AI discrimination had yet reached the courts in Australia, Sheard said, with any instances of discrimination needing to first go to the \uc2 Australian Human Rights Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 2022, the federal merit protection commissioner {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mpc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2022-10/Annual%20Report%202021-22.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revealed}}}\uc2  11 promotion decisions in Services Australia in the previous year had been overturned, after the agency outsourced the process to a recruitment specialist that used AI automated selection techniques, including psychometric testing, questionnaires and self-recorded video responses.\par \par \uc2 It was found that the selection process \u8220\'20\'1Cdid not always meet the key objective of selecting the most meritorious candidates\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sheard said the returned Albanese Labor government should consider a specific AI act to regulate the use of AI, and potentially strengthen existing discrimination laws to guard against AI-based discrimination.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 14 May 2025. A previous version incorrectly stated job candidates risk being discriminated against if they don\u8217\'20\'19t have American accents. The previous version also incorrectly stated 23 human resources professionals were interviewed and 33% of the job applicants in the training data were white.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250513el5d0028o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
