\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18So many are devastated\u8217\'20\'19: Trump\u8217\'20\'19s federal firings and their ripple effect\b0\par\par\uc2 Heather Stewart Economics editor \par \uc2 1,730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2025\par 19:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 From Beltway Virginia to Pittsburgh, it is not just sacked workers feeling the impact but whole local economies\par \par \uc2 Naomi Anderson was on leave looking after her young baby when she was told her \uc2 US Department of Agriculture\uc2  job helping farmers in developing countries was being cut. A former volunteer with the Peace Corps, which sends young Americans overseas to projects in emerging economies, Anderson had expected to spend her whole career in international development.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had taken this job two years ago expecting to stay here for at least 10 years, and you know, we had started to make a community and build up our life here. In January, we had started looking at buying a home,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Now Anderson is having to consider giving up the apartment in the Washington DC commuter town of Reston, Virginia, that she shares with her husband and their four-month-old baby and almost two-year-old toddler.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFinancially, it\u8217\'20\'19s a little bit precarious, and honestly we\u8217\'20\'19re not sure what we\u8217\'20\'19re going to do,\u8221\'20\'1D says Anderson, who is also an activist with the local branch of the \uc2 AFSCME\uc2  union and dabbles in selling political merchandise. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re thinking about moving back to Ohio, where I\u8217\'20\'19m from, where my family is. You know, it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot cheaper there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anderson is far from alone. \u8220\'20\'1CIn our apartment complex, there\u8217\'20\'19s been lots of yard sales, people selling things and moving away. It really does seem like people are just picking up and leaving, because it\u8217\'20\'19s too expensive to live here without a job,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Tough life-decisions like these have been forced on hundreds of thousands of former federal employees in the past couple of months, as the so-called department of government efficiency (Doge), which is headed up by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s favourite tech billionaire, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/03/mass-resignations-at-labor-department-threaten-workers-in-us-and-overseas-warn-staff-as-more-cuts-loom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slashed jobs in a cost-cutting spree}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data from the latest monthly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/april-2025-job-cuts-plunge-but-doge-drives-2025-layoffs-to-pandemic-era-highs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Challenger jobs report}}}\uc2  suggests Doge has been responsible for 281,452 job cuts so far \u8211\'20\'13 almost eight times the number of workers the government let go in the entire year to April 2024.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Demich is among those to be dismissed, losing his job as an engineer at the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2  (\uc2 NIOSH\uc2 ) in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. All his colleagues working on mine safety, as well as those in their sister laboratory testing equipment such as respirators, are also leaving \u8211\'20\'13 more than 200 in total \u8211\'20\'13 as part of a wave of cuts initiated by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s health secretary, Robert F Kennedy Jr.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo many people are devastated,\u8221\'20\'1D says Demich, chief steward of the local \uc2 AFGE\uc2  union branch. He says so many workers have been removed at once that their colleagues have barely been able to give them any kind of send-off. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just unceremoniously leaving, because they had their package processed and they had to walk out the door.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Each of these cuts has its own human impact, but experts are warning of a growing risk that they combine to trigger an economic retrenchment \u8211\'20\'13 particularly in areas with a heavy concentration of government jobs.\par \par \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  federation of 63 trade unions, which together represent more than 15 million US workers, is trying to build a nationwide campaign to highlight the devastating impact.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe trick is connecting the dots because there\u8217\'20\'19s already a national narrative around what\u8217\'20\'19s happening but it\u8217\'20\'19s not quite being felt yet,\u8221\'20\'1D Shuler says. \u8220\'20\'1CElon [Musk] has his \u8216\'20\'18department of government efficiency\u8217\'20\'19. We established the \u8216\'20\'18department of people who work for a living\u8217\'20\'19. That\u8217\'20\'19s kind of cheeky, but it\u8217\'20\'19s kind of serious, because we\u8217\'20\'19re saying we\u8217\'20\'19re the ones working on the frontlines. We know what\u8217\'20\'19s efficient and what\u8217\'20\'19s not.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CObviously as the labour federation we\u8217\'20\'19re worried about jobs and people\u8217\'20\'19s livelihoods, but it\u8217\'20\'19s also connected to community, and the fact the economy is being impacted in such a stark way, that ripples out across all of the industries that we represent,\u8221\'20\'1D she adds.\par \par \uc2 These ripples are being felt especially strongly in the towns and counties around Washington DC, where job losses and government cuts crop up constantly in conversation.\par \par \uc2 Kate Bates is the president of the chamber of commerce in affluent Arlington, Virginia, across the Potomac River from the US capital. She compares the current uncertainty to the pandemic, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut during Covid, the federal government was the backstop, whereas right now it\u8217\'20\'19s the federal government that\u8217\'20\'19s causing a lot of this,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Bates reports that her members are warning of a slowdown across real estate and hospitality, as well as among government contractors, with several reporting they have already had to make job cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we hear from a lot of people is that if they could plan for the cuts, they would be in an OK position, right? But because things are changing, going back and forth, that\u8217\'20\'19s causing a lot of stress,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Businesses that rely on government workers for custom are also feeling the chill. Saamir Nizam, the general manager of Arlington\u8217\'20\'19s Barley Mac restaurant, which is part of a small family-owned chain, has noticed trade declining in just about every one of their usual customer groups.\par \par \uc2 The nearby hotels are less occupied; bookings for \u8220\'20\'1Chappy hour parties\u8221\'20\'1D by the accountants and consultants who serve the federal government are down by two-thirds; and many older local residents have been spooked by market volatility.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can only do so much to turn things around: we can\u8217\'20\'19t pull people to Washington, or convince companies to go out and do things,\u8221\'20\'1D Nizam says. \u8220\'20\'1CBarley Mac serves great food, it gives great service, but it exists, like many restaurants, on the financial margin. And if the whole year is on the margin then restaurants like ours will go under, because we\u8217\'20\'19re not part of a huge national chain which has deep pockets.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jess Miller, who set up Rock Spring Real Estate Solutions a couple of years ago, has been hosting a breakfast roundtable for clients, on the top floor of an as yet unoccupied new office block in Arlington.\par \par \uc2 She has noticed clients responding to the shifting climate, pulling out of deals and hoping to negotiate unusually short leases. The owners of this building are splitting the floors instead of looking for one anchor tenant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust how they\u8217\'20\'19re making decisions is different \u8211\'20\'13 the cutbacks we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing,\u8221\'20\'1D Miller says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt hit the NGOs first and then it hit, you know, the corporations and the contractors, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of the senior management.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Katherine D\u8217\'20\'19Zmura Friedman is a co-founder and the chief executive of Thumbprint, an Arlington-based startup offering an artificial intelligence platform for designing office layouts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no universe in which something like the last few months happens and there\u8217\'20\'19s not serious consequences,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve had family friends, we\u8217\'20\'19ve had neighbours lose their jobs, and these are not people who would normally be subject to layoffs. These are people who are at the peak of their career, and hyper-specialised.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As far as the business effects are concerned, D\u8217\'20\'19Zmura Friedman says: \u8220\'20\'1CCertainly on the commercial side, there\u8217\'20\'19s been hesitancy about launching into things.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At her office nearby, Renata Briggman, a residential estate agent, plays down the idea that the housing market in Arlington could be hit, pointing to the many local employers broadly unaffected by federal spending \u8211\'20\'13 such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which is headquartered here.\par \par \uc2 However, she does acknowledge signs of change. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s definitely shifting. We\u8217\'20\'19re not seeing any fire sales, it\u8217\'20\'19s too soon for that. It\u8217\'20\'19s very, very slow, and we\u8217\'20\'19re just going to start seeing it, it\u8217\'20\'19s just on the cusp \u8230\'20\'26 end of June, we\u8217\'20\'19ll have a better idea.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Such challenges are being replicated across the region. \uc2 Jimmy Olevson\uc2 , the president and chief executive of National Capital Bank, which serves Washington DC and the surrounding areas, says the bank is not yet seeing signs of financial distress, such as rising arrears, but the mood is \u8220\'20\'1Cuneasy\u8221\'20\'1D. Many customers who have had a recent mortgage application approved seem to have put house-hunting on hold.\par \par \uc2 Some experts fear this widespread mood of unease bodes badly for the coming months. Analysis by Dr Jo\u227\'00\'E3o Ferreira, an expert in regional economics at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 , suggests more than 320,000 people in the state are employed directly by the federal government \u8211\'20\'13 and another 441,000 jobs depend on taxpayer-funded contracts, of the kind that are being cut.\par \par \uc2 In some sectors \u8211\'20\'13 construction, for example \u8211\'20\'13 the same firms fretting about whether their contract will be cut are also contending with the rising price of materials, as a result of tariffs. Although some of the border taxes have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/12/china-us-agree-pause-trade-war-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paused or reduced}}}\uc2 , those restrictions that remain mean costs are still far higher than at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 In theory, the Trump administration could lift the gloom by drawing a line under budget cuts as Musk heads {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/may/05/elon-musk-doge-federal-government"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 back to his day job}}}\uc2  running the electric carmaker \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . But key members of the cabinet, including Kennedy and the defence secretary, \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 , have boasted of how much they plan to slash from their budgets \u8211\'20\'13 and White House trade policy continues to see-saw.\par \par \uc2 Ferreira says: \u8220\'20\'1CI think, as an economist, I\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen so many things happening at the same time. But they all lead to the same direction, and that\u8217\'20\'19s a recession.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He says Virginia has often been cushioned from economic downturns in the past by federal funding, but in this cycle he expects the state to lead the way. \u8220\'20\'1CWe definitely might see that Virginia, and other regions like Maryland, will be the frontrunners in this recession period,\u8221\'20\'1D Ferreira says.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, for many of the affected individuals, the future looks highly uncertain \u8211\'20\'13 despite the US treasury secretary Scott Bessent\u8217\'20\'19s suggestion they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newrepublic.com/post/193695/treasury-secretary-says-fired-federal-workers-can-work-factory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 should go work in manufacturing}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CFor us on our team, we work in international development,\u8221\'20\'1D says Anderson. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a background in humanitarian work, and the Trump administration is trying to cut international foreign aid. So where do you go from there?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250514el5e00237\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
