\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  CEO says crisis \u8216\'20\'18decades in the making\u8217\'20\'19; US inflation hits four-year low \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 7,927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2025\par 14:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial news, as MPs quiz \uc2 Thames Water\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T14:36:16.033Z">3.36pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to recap\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The chair of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/thames-water"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thames Water}}}\uc2  has admitted its finances were \u8220\'20\'1Chair raising\u8221\'20\'1D, as he said bosses were in line for \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantial\u8221\'20\'1D bonuses linked to an emergency \u163\'00\'A33bn loan.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s biggest water company came within just five weeks of running out of money, \uc2 Adrian Montague\uc2  told MPs on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThames in the last year has come very close to running out of money entirely,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. He added there were times where it only had weeks\u8217\'20\'19 worth of cash left.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRunning a \u163\'00\'A320bn corporation on five weeks\u8217\'20\'19 liquidity, honestly, it\u8217\'20\'19s hair raising,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Montague also said some executives at the struggling water company were in line for payouts amounting to \u8220\'20\'1C50% of salary, very substantial bonuses\u8221\'20\'1D as part of the high-interest emergency debt package, which was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/18/thames-water-wins-court-backing-for-debt-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved by the high court in February}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During the hearing, chief executive \uc2 Chris Weston\uc2  also warned that he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t guarantee there wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be restrictions on water use this summer, but was{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/may/13/uk-wage-growth-slows-payrolls-vacancies-drop-thames-water-mps-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68231ad58f0824efe73e35bd#block-68231ad58f0824efe73e35bd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  confident that Thames wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t run out of water}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Weston said the crisis at Thames had been \u8216\'20\'18decades in the making\u8217\'20\'19, due in part to{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/may/13/uk-wage-growth-slows-payrolls-vacancies-drop-thames-water-mps-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-68230b7c8f0894079b084916#block-68230b7c8f0894079b084916"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a lack of leadership}}}\uc2  and turnover in the boardroom.\par \par \uc2 Montague insisted that the new team was committed to{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/may/13/uk-wage-growth-slows-payrolls-vacancies-drop-thames-water-mps-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6823231f8f089320ecd283c8#block-6823231f8f089320ecd283c8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  fixing the problems at Thames}}}\uc2 . <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/13/thames-water-chair-admits-to-mps-that-its-finances-were-hair-raising"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thames Water chair admits to MPs that its finances were \u8216\'20\'18hair raising\u8217\'20\'19}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere today\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Inflation in the US has dropped to a four-year low in April, the month that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  announced his sweeping \u8220\'20\'1Cliberation day\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tariffs}}}\uc2  on the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest trading partners.\par \par \uc2 The annual inflation rate was 2.3% in April, down from an annual rate of 2.4% March, according to a new inflation report from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2  ). <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/may/13/council-leader-accepted-spurs-tickets-felling-ancient-oak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Council leader accepted Spurs tickets days before felling of ancient oak}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in the UK has risen to its highest level in almost four years, according to official figures, as the jobs market continues to slow.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/office-for-national-statistics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Office for National Statistics}}}\uc2  (ONS) said the rate was 4.5% in the first three months of this year, up 0.2% on the previous quarter and the highest reading since summer 2021.\par \par \uc2 The ONS also reported that UK wage growth slowed, as firms reduced their vacancies and cut the number of staff on their payrolls. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/13/uk-unemployment-rises-ons-job-vacancies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK unemployment rises to highest in nearly four years}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T14:18:18.358Z">3.18pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened higher, as investors welcome the drop in US inflation today.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 share index gained 0.64%, up 37 points at 5,881 points.\par \par \uc2 The tech-focused Nasdaq has risen by over 1%.\par \par \uc2 The drop in inflation may be reassuring traders that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will have space to cut interest rates this, if it needs to stimulate the US econonomy.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average is in the red, though, down 119 points at 42,290.86 points. It\u8217\'20\'19s been dragged down by \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  (-13%), which today announced that CEO \uc2 Andrew Witty\uc2  was quitting immediately for \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal reasons\u8221\'20\'1D as it suspended its annual outlook.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T13:58:00.773Z">2.58pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Starling Bank\uc2  probed over reporting failings\par \par \uc2 Starling Bank\uc2  has been ordered to launch an external review and boost compliance training after CMA said it had concerns about an \u8220\'20\'1Cunderlying weakness\u8221\'20\'1D in the online lender\u8217\'20\'19s procedures.\par \par \uc2 The challenger bank\u8217\'20\'19s breaches related to data it fed into customer satisfaction surveys,{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/latest-banking-satisfaction-survey-results-revealed-by-cma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  which it regularly tops}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was found to have excluded 17% of personal account customers from data handed to a market research company in both 2023 and 2024, and also overstated the total number of accounts held by customers to the competitions regulator between 2020-2023.\par \par \uc2 It comes months after the bank was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/02/starling-bank-fined-29m-for-shockingly-lax-financial-controls"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hit with a \u163\'00\'A329m fine}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Financial Conduct Authority\uc2  in October 2024, over \u8220\'20\'1Cshockingly lax\u8221\'20\'1D controls that the \uc2 FCA\uc2  said left the financial system \u8220\'20\'1Cwide open to criminals\u8221\'20\'1D. That included failures in its automated screening system for individuals facing government sanctions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6821c8c1db6463b14cd818bd/Directions_to_Starling_Bank_Limited_issued_under_the_Retail_Banking_Market_Investigation_Order.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6821c8c1db6463b14cd818bd/Directions_to_Starling_Bank_Limited_issued_under_the_Retail_Banking_Market_Investigation_Order.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CMA}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6821c8c1db6463b14cd818bd/Directions_to_Starling_Bank_Limited_issued_under_the_Retail_Banking_Market_Investigation_Order.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  said on Tuesday}}}\uc2  that excluding a large number of customers from the data was a \u8220\'20\'1Cmaterial breach\u8221\'20\'1D, and while the impact on the rankings was likely \u8220\'20\'1Cnegligible\u8221\'20\'1D, it risked undermining consumer confidence in the survey results.\par \par \uc2 The CMA said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerned that there may be an underlying weakness in \uc2 Starling Bank\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s procedures.\u8221\'20\'1D It added:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe CMA has concerns that formal action may be necessary to prevent a recurrence. This is because \uc2 Starling Bank\uc2  has now breached the requirements to provide full data on current account holders twice and we are not convinced that Starling Bank is capable of preventing further breaches through the initiatives mentioned above.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starling said that the exclusions were due to a lack of appropriate oversight, and that the over-reporting was a misunderstanding of the regulatory requirement. Starling itself notified the CMA of the breaches last year and was called into the regulator in November to discuss the matter.\par \par \uc2 The bank has now been issued with directions that include hiring an external firm to carry out a review of its processes and procedures, and will now have to provide extra compliance training for staff.\par \par \uc2 Starling said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLast year we identified some reporting errors related to the CMA\u8217\'20\'19s customer satisfaction surveys. We informed the CMA of our findings, we apologised, and we changed our processes to prevent a recurrence. We are pleased that the CMA has now determined that the impact on customers was \u8216\'20\'18negligible\u8217\'20\'19 and that the bank has overpaid rather than underpaid its dues.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to work constructively with the CMA because we share their goal of ensuring that people can see which banks are listening to their customers and meeting their needs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T13:53:47.801Z">2.53pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 MPs also heard today that Thames Water \u8217\'20\'19s senior executives will receive large \u8220\'20\'1Cretention incentives\u8221\'20\'1D funded through the \u163\'00\'A33bn emergency loan it agreed recently.\par \par \uc2 Chair Sir Adrian Montague told the EFRA committee that the deal \u8220\'20\'1Crequired\u8221\'20\'1D Thames to create a retention plan for its senior management team, which he called \u8220\'20\'1Cour most precious resource\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That plan will pay out in three installments \u8211\'20\'13 when the first restructuring plan is agreed, then when the second restructuring is agreed, and then a larger amount at the end of the process.\par \par \uc2 The first two installments are worth 50% of salary, which Montague agrees is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery substantial\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He reveals its the first time he\u8217\'20\'19s encountered this sort of deal.\par \par \uc2 And he denied that Thames was swayed to choose the funding offer from its A-class creditors, rather than a rival offer, at a lower interest rate but no such retention scheme, from B-class shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Customers should take into account that they are not paying these bonuses, Montague insists:\par \par \uc2 This amount will be funded by the lenders, and at the end of the day borne by the lenders.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T13:28:53.163Z">2.28pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s hard to see much impact from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs in today\u8217\'20\'19s inflation report, says Heather Long , the Washington Post \u8217\'20\'19s economics columnist. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1922270764994331072"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1922270764994331072}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 You can see the 0.2% inflation increase in April.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's a jump from March, but April was in line with recent trends.\par \par \uc2 Commodity prices up just 0.1% in April\par \par \uc2 New car prices flat\par \par \uc2 Used car prices down -0.5%\par \par \uc2 Apparel prices down -0.2%\par \par \uc2 It's hard to see much impact from Trump's\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Ny0k3bLvHW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Ny0k3bLvHW}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1922270764994331072?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure> <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="https://twitter.com/ernietedeschi/status/1922269979354792035">\par \par \uc2 CPI inflation was 0.22% MM in April. Core CPI grew 0.24% MM. Apparel prices (-0.2%) & new car prices (-0.01%) both fell in April. Core commodity prices grew more slowly than overall CPI, such that relative core commodity prices ticked down slightly. Not much sign of tariffs here {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Ghqyo2gmT4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Ghqyo2gmT4}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ernie Tedeschi (@ernietedeschi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ernietedeschi/status/1922269979354792035?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T13:03:53.175Z">2.03pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The drop in US inflation in April suggests that the tariffs are yet to feed through to inflation, says Seema Shah , chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management , adding:\par \par \uc2 Yet, it is questionable whether or not today\u8217\'20\'19s CPI print really moves the needle after the rollercoaster ride of the past month. After all, not only is the April CPI report unlikely to have fully captured the tariff impact post-Liberation Day, but inflation numbers will now be further whipsawed by the US/China trade truce announcement.\par \par \uc2 An inflation impulse will likely come through during late Q2, but may be partially and quickly eroded if container traffic rapidly resumes in light of the drop in US/China tariffs. The implication is that a clear read on the inflation trend won\u8217\'20\'19t be visible for several months yet. This prolonged inflation uncertainty likely implies a prolonged Fed pause. \u8220\'20\'1C\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T12:56:28.976Z">1.56pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump will be pleased to see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/may/13/uk-wage-growth-slows-payrolls-vacancies-drop-thames-water-mps-us-inflation-business-live-news?page=with%3Ablock-68233c558f089320ecd284ea#block-68233c558f089320ecd284ea"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inflation has managed to ease slightly in the US}}}\uc2 , says Lindsay James, investment strategist at Quilter .\par \par \uc2 But the US is \u8220\'20\'1Cfar from out of the woods\u8221\'20\'1D when it comes to inflation, James adds:\par \par \uc2 The first quarter of the year saw businesses ramp up their inventories as they looked to stockpile ahead of \u8216\'20\'18Liberation Day\u8217\'20\'19. As such, price rises are very much delayed, and we can still expect inflation in the US to spike because of these policies. This is, of course, why the Federal Reserve is reluctant to cut interest rates at this point.\par \par \uc2 The reprieve for Chinese goods will likely lead to some more re-stocking before the end of the 90-day period. But while tariffs are much reduced since yesterday, they remain markedly higher than before the 2nd April. The \u8216\'20\'18Art of the Deal\u8217\'20\'19 means no-one knows how permanent these tariffs may become, if at all, or if they will be reintroduced in a less harsh form. However, businesses will become increasingly wary of the shifting sands beneath their feet as these deadlines approach. Given we are already almost at the halfway point of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 90 day pause for the \u8216\'20\'18reciprocal tariffs\u8217\'20\'19 on other nations, we may soon see them building in more margin through price rises to protect themselves from sudden changes in policy making.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs are not the only thing weighing on inflation either, however, and there remain some other underlying factors that aren\u8217\'20\'19t changing as quickly. Food prices in the US remain elevated despite Trump\u8217\'20\'19s campaigning to lower those pressures for consumers.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s inflation figure may paint somewhat of a sanguine picture, but scratch lightly under the surface and it is clear the US faces a number of risks. With economic growth slowing at the same time, the Fed is left in a bind of where to go from here. Risk of policy misstep, therefore, is growing and as such market volatility remains very much on the table.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T12:44:56.174Z">1.44pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 US inflation drops to four-year low\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: US inflation was lower than expected last month, despite fears that tariffs will push up costs.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, US consumer price inflation fell to 2.3% in April, down from 2.4% in March, the lowest reading since February 2021. Economists had expected the rate would be unchanged. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1922268929780584636"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1922268929780584636}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 US CPI inflation 2.6% - the lowest since Feb 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KMXqlOQjn2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/KMXqlOQjn2}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mike Zaccardi, CFA, CMT (@MikeZaccardi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1922268929780584636?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 This might cheer Donald Trump , and bolster his claim that he is easing the price pressures hitting US households.\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS ) reports that food prices rose by 2.8% over the year, while energy prices were 3.7% cheaper than a year ago. Shelter (housing costs) rose by 4% year-on-year.\par \par \uc2 During the month, prices rise by 0.2% in April, having dropped by 0.1% in March.\par \par \uc2 The BLS says:\par \par \uc2 Indexes that increased over the month include household furnishings and operations, medical care, motor vehicle insurance, education, and personal care. The indexes for airline fares, used cars and trucks, communication, and apparel were among the major indexes that decreased in April.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T12:49:41.266Z">at 1.49pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T12:06:23.394Z">1.06pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England \u8217\'20\'19s chief economist, Huw Pill , has underlined his concerns about wage growth, suggesting it might mean rates have to stay higher for longer.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a conference at the London School of Economics today, he said he was concerned about the upside risks to inflation, which might get stuck above the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target.\par \par \uc2 Pill said that could, \u8220\'20\'1Cmean that the response of monetary policy, in order to ensure that we get back to our target within a reasonable cycle, needs to be somewhat more aggressive or more persistent in itself.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T12:05:04.172Z">1.05pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Jaguar Land Rover has welcomed the US-UK trade deal announced last week, after reporting its highest annual profit in 10 years.\par \par \uc2 JLR posted a \u163\'00\'A32.5bn pre-tax profit in the year to 31 March, its highest profit in a decade.\par \par \uc2 The company is pleased that the UK has negotiated down tariffs on cars exported to the US, saying:\par \par \uc2 In April, at the start of the new financial year, we implemented a series of short-term actions to address the immediate impact of trade tariffs introduced by the US Administration on the global automotive sector. On 8 May 2025 we welcomed the positive announcement of a US-UK trade deal. This reduces US trade tariffs on UK auto exports to the US from 27.5% to 10%, within a quota of 100,000 vehicles. This deal brings greater certainty for our sector and stakeholders.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to engage with the UK Government on the detail of the trade deal. Our priority is to ensure we deliver for our global clients and protect EBIT through delivery of transformation and efficiency initiatives.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T11:55:12.401Z">12.55pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames Water \u8217\'20\'19 s CEO concedes that the company has run up \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely high\u8221\'20\'1D costs on advisors, which total around \u163\'00\'A3159m.\par \par \uc2 That bill reflects the fact Thames is the \u8220\'20\'1Clargest restructuring of a balance sheet in British history\u8221\'20\'1D, Chris Weston says.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T11:35:28.546Z">12.35pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Life would be \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely difficult for everyone\u8221\'20\'1D if Thames Water falls into a special administration regime (effectively nationalisation), its chair told MPs.\par \par \uc2 Adrian Montague suggested that suppliers might wonder how much longer they wanted to work for Thames; senior management might also consider their position, and there would be a considerable burden on government , who could face \u163\'00\'A35bn of extra costs, and would need to eventually find a buyer for the business. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/03/treasury-threatens-defra-with-4bn-bill-if-thames-water-nationalised"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Treasury threatens Defra with \u163\'00\'A34bn bill if Thames Water nationalised}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T11:30:40.237Z">12.30pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Touching again on Thames\u8217\'20\'19s cash squeeze, Sir Adrian Montague says the company\u8217\'20\'19s liquidity was very tight when it was negotiating with lenders recently.\par \par \uc2 Having only five weeks liquidity \u8220\'20\'1Cis a nightmare\u8221\'20\'1D for a large company such as Thames, Montague says.\par \par \uc2 But, he insists, Thames was in a \u8220\'20\'1Cbeggers can\u8217\'20\'19t be choosers situation\u8221\'20\'1D when it was negotiating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/25/struggling-thames-water-pushes-for-lifeline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the deal to provide a \u163\'00\'A33bn lifeline}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Montague adds:\par \par \uc2 We were running along the edge of the precipice, and we had to secure the financial future of the company.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T11:09:11.089Z">12.09pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 MPs have also quizzed Thames Water \u8217\'20\'19s top brass about its future ownership.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive Chris Weston told the committee it was still a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery fluid situation\u8221\'20\'1D, despite investment firm KKR having been chosen as the sole bid to invest in the company.\par \par \uc2 He says there is still a possibility that Thames could go back to other interest parties if \u8220\'20\'1Cif the current process fails\u8221\'20\'1D. Other options are that creditors step in, and execute a debt-for-equity swap.\par \par \uc2 Or, there is a possibility the company could still fall into public ownership.\par \par \uc2 Weston says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very fluid situation but those both are possibilities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no guarantee we would not stay on a market-led solution as opposed to a special administration, but it is a very fluid situation and those are all possibilities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:59:30.129Z">11.59am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Montague: We're here to put things right\par \par \uc2 Sir Adrian Montague then refuses to give a commitment to Helena Dollimore MP that the Thames Water top team at the committee hearing (him, CEO Chris Weston , and CFO Steve Buck) will remain in place through this parliament.\par \par \uc2 We live in a competitive marketplace, Montague points out, adding:\par \par \uc2 We have to live in the real world. No-one will give you a commitment that will be no change in the leadership team over five years. It is simply not possible.\par \par \uc2 Q: So how can we have any faith in the commitments you are making?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are the new kids on the block,\u8221\'20\'1D Montague declares, adding \u8220\'20\'1CWe are making progress. We are here to try to put things right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We won\u8217\'20\'19t quit until the job is done, but it is not realistic to say there will be no changes in the team over 5 years.\par \par \uc2 Q: Why would headhunters recruit from a failing company?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a failing company\u8221\'20\'1D, Montague fires back, \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s a company in recovery\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We are making progress. This is a good team. You can see how the progress is starting to become apparent.\par \par \uc2 Committee chair Alistair Carmichael seems unimpressed, asking:\par \par \uc2 Q: If a 40% rise in pollution incidents is a sign of success, what does failure look like?\par \par \uc2 Montague says \u8220\'20\'1CWe must go back into those pollution incidents\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T11:42:16.019Z">at 12.42pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:46:41.405Z">11.46am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames Water chair Sir Adrian Montague then defends the company\u8217\'20\'19s controversial decision to pay dividends, rather than investing in improving its infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Montague says Thames decided to pay the \u8216\'20\'18intergroup\u8217\'20\'19 dividend, of \u163\'00\'A3157m, in October 2023 so it could keep discussions about future capital injections with then-shareholders alive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the event, that failed\u8221\'20\'1D, he admits. But at the time, Thames didn\u8217\'20\'19t know it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t work.\par \par \uc2 Helena Dollimore MP points out that a better use of that would have been to mend pipes, or to fund the 100 infrastructure projects that were delayed in summer 2023.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: Montague may have his dates mixed up here. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/28/thames-water-board-150m-dividend-payout-funding-u-turn?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Guardian reported last June that this \u163\'00\'A3150m dividend was agreed in March 2024}}}\uc2 , having already faced investigation for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/dec/05/ofwat-investigates-whether-thames-water-dividend-is-licensing-breach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paying a \u163\'00\'A337.5m dividend in October 2023}}}\uc2 , for which it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/19/thames-water-to-pay-18m-penalty-after-breaking-dividend-rules"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fined}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T11:47:26.261Z">at 12.47pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:39:36.584Z">11.39am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames CEO Chris Weston then feels more heat over his bonus payments.\par \par \uc2 He reveals that Thames\u8217\'20\'19s front-line staff can qualify for bonuses worth 3%-6% of their salary, while Weston can earn 156% of his salary in a bonus.\par \par \uc2 Weston concedes that \u8220\'20\'1Cit is a lot of money\u8221\'20\'1D, but argues that Thames must offer competitive packages to attract and retain the staff it needs.\par \par \uc2 Labour MP Helena Dollimore says there is a \u8220\'20\'1Cclear discrepancy\u8221\'20\'1D here, and that if Thames was truly committed to improving its infrastructure it would have more focus on the morale of front-line staff, and support them better.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:32:23.610Z">11.32am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames chair: we got hair-raisingly close to running out of money\par \par \uc2 Thames Water \u8217\'20\'19s chair has admitted to MP\u8217\'20\'19s that the company came worryingly close to running out of money in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Tackled over Thames\u8217\'20\'19s decision to delay 100 vital infrastructure projects, to prevent storm overflows and pollution incidents, in August 2023, Sir Adrian Montague reveals\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 Thames in the last year has come very close to running out of money entirely.\par \par \uc2 There were times in the last year when we had five weeks of liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Running a \u163\'00\'A320bn corporation on five week\u8217\'20\'19s liquidity is \u8220\'20\'1Chair-raising\u8221\'20\'1D, Montague says.\par \par \uc2 He explains that the decision to delay those projects was the start of a process to fight a \u8220\'20\'1Cfunding crunch\u8221\'20\'1D, as \u8220\'20\'1Cwe felt we needed to conserve cash\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Montague denies that the money was used on bonuses, which he says are necessary to retain staff. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AArmstrong_says/status/1922225501550076278"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/AArmstrong_says/status/1922225501550076278}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 Thames Water bosses grilling by MPs pretty startling so far.\par \par \uc2 As chair Sir Andrew Montague just said \u8220\'20\'1Crunning a \u163\'00\'A322 billion company on just five weeks cash - is pretty hair raising\u8230\'20\'26 the cash box was nearly empty\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Justifies paying bonuses to bosses to \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep talent\u8221\'20\'1D\u8212\'20\'14 Ashley Armstrong (@AArmstrong_says) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AArmstrong_says/status/1922225501550076278?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:33:16.174Z">at 11.33am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:21:22.704Z">11.21am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames CEO: Confident we won't run out of water this summer, but...\par \par \uc2 Thames Water is taking steps to prepare for a possible drought this summer, its CEO insists (even though {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/may/13/uk-wage-growth-slows-payrolls-vacancies-drop-thames-water-mps-us-inflation-business-live-news?page=with%3Ablock-682319658f089320ecd28332#block-682319658f089320ecd28332"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its desalination plant at Beckton is still out of action)}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chris Weston tells MPs on the EFRA committee that Thames has four levels of drought awareness, and has just raised that level from zero to 1.\par \par \uc2 That means:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Thames is checking all its reservoirs are as full as can be \u8211\'20\'13 they are currently 94% full now, and the company is trying to top them up.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 it is going through all its plants to ensure all critical maintainance is done before the summer\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 It will also start to communicate the importance of conserving water to customers.\par \par \uc2 Weston says:\par \par \uc2 At the moment, from what I can see, we have learned the lessons from the situation in 2022 and we are doing all that we need to at the moment to prepare for water shortage.\par \par \uc2 I hope that is not necessary.\par \par \uc2 Q: How confident are you that you won\u8217\'20\'19t run out of water this summer?\par \par \uc2 Weston replies that he is confident Thames won\u8217\'20\'19t run out of water. But, he\u8217\'20\'19s not confident that it won\u8217\'20\'19t have to restrict usage, as that will depend on weather and rainfall.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:52:16.397Z">at 11.52am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:09:41.011Z">11.09am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 MPs then tackle Thames Water about the problems with its water desalination plant in Beckton, east London.\par \par \uc2 CEO Chris Weston confirms that the plant, which is designed to turn salty seawater into fresh drinking water, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2025/05/13/britain-only-desalination-plant-still-offline-drought/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will be out of action again this year}}}\uc2 , despite fears there could be a drought.\par \par \uc2 Weston says the desalination plant, which cost \u163\'00\'A3250m, is a big problem, and reveals he wonders why it was built (!).\par \par \uc2 He tells the EFRA committee:\par \par \uc2 It is not a good story, it was not a good investment. There are no excuses about it. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/aug/04/emergency-water-plant-london-unusable-despite-drought-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Emergency water plant in London unusable despite drought risk}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T13:21:21.741Z">at 2.21pm BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T10:03:50.732Z">11.03am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames chair: We should apologise for not serving customers well enough\par \par \uc2 Thames Water \u8217\'20\'19s chairman, Sir Adrian Montague , tells MPs that there is a \u8220\'20\'1Cvast amount to be done to bring performance up to scratch\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 City grandee Montague , who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jun/29/uk-privatisation-thames-water-crisis-nationalisation-cost-of-living-economics-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appointed in summer 2023}}}\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 We know we are letting customers down. We know that pollution, spills, supply interruptions cause inconvenience and sometimes real hardship.\par \par \uc2 He adds that a discussion about Thames\u8217\'20\'19s turnaround plan should start by achnowledging that the company \u8220\'20\'1Chaven\u8217\'20\'19t always served our customers as well as we should, and through the committee apologising to them\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That turnaround is starting to yield \u8220\'20\'1Creal progress\u8221\'20\'1D, Montague insists, saying that green shoots are there \u8211\'20\'13 citing improvements on supply interruptions, and water quality.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s such a big business, you can\u8217\'20\'19t hope to put everything right straight away. You\u8217\'20\'19ve got to choose your targets, and little-by-little improve it overall.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T09:53:56.269Z">10.53am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Q: Staff are worried about the future, because of the uncertainty over Thames\u8217\'20\'19 ownership. Can you guarantee that their jobs, pay, terms and conditions will be maintained?\par \par \uc2 CEO Chris Weston swerves away from the word \u8220\'20\'1Cguarantee\u8221\'20\'1D, as he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know how things will change.\par \par \uc2 But, he insists, he sees a growing employee base, over the last year and over the next five years. Staff should take comfort from that, he argues.\par \par \uc2 However, it would be \u8220\'20\'1Cmore difficult\u8221\'20\'1D if Thames Water falls into a special administration regime \u8211\'20\'13 but even then, jobs should be protected as Thames must keep providing services.\par \par \uc2 Weston goes on to criticise short-term decisions to cut staff in the past, which undermined its performance.\par \par \uc2 He promises to fight for jobs, if Thames\u8217\'20\'19s new owner pushes for efficiency savings, saying:\par \par \uc2 I will fight for every job in Thames.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T09:41:44.550Z">10.41am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames CEO: Crisis is decades in the making\par \par \uc2 MPs then turn to the BBC\u8217\'20\'19s recent (revealing) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/16/thames-water-on-tv-pity-the-staff-this-place-is-decrepit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documentary into the Thames Water crisis}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Q: Why did CEO Chris Weston tell the BBC that \u8220\'20\'1CI won\u8217\'20\'19t know how it got this way\u8221\'20\'1D, after 10 months at the company? What\u8217\'20\'19s his analysis today?\par \par \uc2 Weston replies that he has a pretty clear idea now, and was also pretty clear then, but questions the value of \u8220\'20\'1Ctalking about it publicly and pointing the finger\u8221\'20\'1D [in which case, why allow TV cameras into the company?!].\par \par \uc2 Weston tells the EFRA committee there are \u8220\'20\'1Cmany authors\u8221\'20\'1D responsible.\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m clear how we got here. This has been decades in the making, the crisis we face at Thames.\par \par \uc2 I think all actors had a role to play in this.\par \par \uc2 Absolutely, the company and management has got something wrong. Five chief executives in five years is not a recipe for success, and I would argue consistency in leadership is a very important part of what we need to do now.\par \par \uc2 But, Weston also takes aim at the UK\u8217\'20\'19s regulatory regime, saying it is flawed.\par \par \uc2 Regulators need to attract investment into the sector, and allow companies who are in trouble to turn around and improve \u8211\'20\'13 Weston argues that it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t do that at the moment.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T09:30:23.725Z">10.30am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames CEO defends \u163\'00\'A3190,000 bonus\par \par \uc2 Thames Water CEO Chris Weston is then tackled about his decision to accept a \u163\'00\'A3190,000 bonus after three months at the company.\par \par \uc2 Q: Was that a wise thing to do, given your focus on providing leadership?\par \par \uc2 Weston says his remuneration was agreed with the board before he joined.\par \par \uc2 By and large, he says, people do recognise the magnitute of his job.\par \par \uc2 And he insists that he did \u8220\'20\'1Cmake a difference\u8221\'20\'1D in his first three months.\par \par \uc2 Weston says he started to put a new organisation structure in place, and started to give Thames staff \u8220\'20\'1Cconfidence and reassurance\u8221\'20\'1D that they could take pride in their job.\par \par \uc2 That helped to stabilise the company, and I think that was important.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T09:23:25.757Z">10.23am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Thames Water CEO: We lost direction\par \par \uc2 Thames Water chief executive Chris Weston has blamed \u8220\'20\'1Cconfusion\u8221\'20\'1D and a lack of direction from previous bosses for the problems that have beset the company.\par \par \uc2 Testifying to the Environment, Food and Rural Affairs Committee this morning, Weston says he has been making progress since becoming CEO 16 months ago.\par \par \uc2 He explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere it had gone wrong is that Thames has lost direction a bit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Weston tells the committee he was the 5th CEO appointed in five years, and that 26 members of the executive team had changed in that time.\par \par \uc2 That churn has created \u8220\'20\'1Ca confusion for people in the company,\u8221\'20\'1D Weston explains, insisting that Thames staff weren\u8217\'20\'19t unwilling to do their job or take on accountability.\par \par \uc2 He says he has provided clearer direction at the company, created a better organisation structure and a governance structure to allow him and the board to understand performance at the company,.\par \par \uc2 Weston says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI want the people in the company to feel that they have clear direction, they have the right resources and they are supported in what they do.\par \par \uc2 My job is to make them feel supported and confident in what they are doing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Weston also tells MPs he was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery impressed\u8221\'20\'1D with Thames\u8217\'20\'19s performance when he joined the company:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C2.6bn litres of clean water every day, 5bn litres of waste dealt with every day. I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t help but be impressed, coming from outside the industry\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And he adds that he is \u8220\'20\'1Cquite pleased with the progress we are making\u8221\'20\'1D at the company.\par \par \uc2 (one which became notorious for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/18/thames-water-data-reveals-raw-sewage-discharges-rivers-2024#:~:text=1%20month%20old-,Thames%20Water%20data%20reveals%20raw%20sewage,rivers%20rose%2050%25%20in%202024&text=A%20record%2050%25%20more%20raw,seen%20by%20the%20Guardian%20reveals."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sewage spills}}}\uc2 , a push for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/14/thames-water-launches-appeal-for-permission-to-raise-bills-even-higher"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 higher bills}}}\uc2 , and a debt pile that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/18/thames-water-wins-court-backing-for-debt-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatens its future}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Weston cautions, though:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s going to take a long time to turn Thames around.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T09:24:16.628Z">at 10.24am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T09:05:06.099Z">10.05am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Over in parliament, MPs are digging into the crisis at Thames Water .\par \par \uc2 The Environment, Food and Rural Affairs Select Committee (EFRA) are quizzing top brass at the water company \u8211\'20\'13 CEO Chris Weston , chair Sir Adrian Montague CBE , and CFO Steve Buck .\par \par \uc2 You can watch it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.parliamentlive.tv/Event/Index/e0f0d376-8063-4b11-8585-2f33047a0a92"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 . <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="https://twitter.com/CommonsEFRA/status/1922215421723263371">\par \par \uc2 Our session with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/thameswater?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @thameswater}}}\uc2  has started.\par \par \uc2 Watch it live: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/qxmtOxNOFX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/qxmtOxNOFX}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/q0LTSBLynY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/q0LTSBLynY}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 EFRA Committee (@CommonsEFRA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CommonsEFRA/status/1922215421723263371?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 MPs are planning to cover the following issues\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Leadership and culture at the company\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Recent court case and appeal\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Current ownership\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The future of Thames Water\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Thames Water \u8217\'20\'19s selection of KKR as its preferred bidder\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Price review, performance, and penalties\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Returns to investors and profitability\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Executive pay and bonuses\par \par \uc2 <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/nils-pratley-on-finance/2025/may/12/mps-should-not-accept-any-murky-answers-from-thames-water-chair-on-potential-sale"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MPs should not accept any murky answers from Thames Water chair on potential sale | Nils Pratley}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T08:45:31.122Z">9.45am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 UK pub chain Marston\u8217\'20\'19s has swung back to a profit in the first half of its financial year, thanks to a busy Christmas and Mother\u8217\'20\'19s Day, as well as a tighter focus on costs.\par \par \uc2 The pub owner reported a pre-tax profit of \u163\'00\'A319m in the 26 weeks ended on 29 March, compared with a loss of \u163\'00\'A30.2m in the comparable period in 2024.\par \par \uc2 That was largely driven by a sharper focus on costs, as revenue was broadly flat at \u163\'00\'A3427.4m. But more recent sales growth has been much stronger thanks to a busy Easter season: like-for-like sales in the 5 weeks since 29 March grew by 10.5%. Chief executive Justin Platt has said he is \u8220\'20\'1Cexcited for the summer months ahead\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Marston\u8217\'20\'19s bounced by as much as 6.4% in early trading on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T08:58:39.277Z">at 9.58am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T08:34:15.992Z">9.34am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs : US recession less likely as tariff rates fall\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs has lowered its estimate for the likelihood that the US economy falls into recession in the next year, following an easing in trade war tensions.\par \par \uc2 Economists at Goldman have cut the chances of a US recession in the next 12 months to 35%, down from 45%.\par \par \uc2 The move follows {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/12/china-us-agree-pause-trade-war-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the agreement reached by the US and China yesterday}}}\uc2  to sharply reduce tariffs for the next 90 days. That deal means US importers will only pay a 30% tariff on imports from China, down from 145%.\par \par \uc2 Goldman now forecast the US economy will grow by 1% during 2025, twice as fast as the 0.5% previously forecast.\par \par \uc2 The bank says:\par \par \uc2 Their revised forecast reflects a combination of a lower effective tariff rate and the recent easing of financial conditions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/14/goldman-sachs-boss-says-chances-of-us-recession-has-increased-after-trumps-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A month ago, }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/14/goldman-sachs-boss-says-chances-of-us-recession-has-increased-after-trumps-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Goldman}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/14/goldman-sachs-boss-says-chances-of-us-recession-has-increased-after-trumps-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  raised the odds of a US recession to 45% from 35%}}}\uc2 , after Donald Trump announced a swathe of tariffs on trading partners \u8211\'20\'13 which have all now been paused while trade negotiations take place.\par \par \uc2 Goldman has also lifted its forecast for gains on the US stock market this year, saying:\par \par \uc2 We raise our S&P 500 return and earnings forecasts to incorporate lower tariff rates, better economic growth, and less recession risk than we previously expected. Our new 3- and 12-month returns forecasts are +1% and +11%, indicating levels of 5900 and 6500 (previously 5700 and 6200).\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T08:00:00.258Z">9.00am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Warm weather in the UK has helped drive demand for DIY and gardening equipment, retailer Wickes has reported.\par \par \uc2 Wickes reported a 9.6% rise in revenues in the 17 weeks to 26 April, which it partly credits to decent weather in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Wickes tells the City:\par \par \uc2 We have been well positioned to benefit from the warmer weather at the start of this year, which has supported the strong sales performance in Retail. For example, in the week of the early May bank holiday we had our biggest ever week for sales of compost and top soil.\par \par \uc2 Wickes strikes a cautious note, though, adding that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe consumer outlook remains uncertain and the business faces significant cost headwinds\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T07:32:02.446Z">8.32am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 M&S reveals some customer data taken in cyber attack\par \par \uc2 Marks & Spencer has revealed that \u8220\'20\'1Csome personal customer information\u8221\'20\'1D was taken during the cyber-attack which hit the retailer last month.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/company/marks-and-spencer/posts/?feedView=all"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In a statement on LinkedIn}}}\uc2 , M&S adds that there is \u8220\'20\'1Cno need for customers to take any action\u8221\'20\'1D, as the information does not include usable card or payment details.\par \par \uc2 The company has told the City:\par \par \uc2 Today, we are writing to customers informing them that due to the sophisticated nature of the incident, some of their personal customer data has been taken.\par \par \uc2 M&S says it will prompt online customers to reset their passwords when they next log in, adding:\par \par \uc2 Everyone at M&S is working around the clock to get things back to normal for customers as quickly as possible, and we are very sorry for any inconvenience they have experienced. Our stores remain open as they have throughout.\par \par \uc2 M&S has been battling the disruption caused its cyber-incident for more than three weeks. It was forced to halt all orders through its website and apps at the end of April.\par \par \uc2 At the start of May, the Information Commissioner\u8217\'20\'19s Office said it had received reports from Marks and Spencer , and also the Co-op Group, which was also hit by a cyber attack. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/03/inside-the-marks-and-spencer-cyber-attack-chaos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clothing shortages, food waste and millions lost each day: inside the M&S cyber-attack chaos}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T07:33:37.454Z">at 8.33am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T07:07:09.102Z">8.07am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The largest employment falls were seen in UK retail and hospitality in the last quarter, reports Stephen Evans, chief executive of Learning and Work Institute (L&W).\par \par \uc2 Evans adds:\par \par \uc2 Pay growth is fastest in those sectors as the rise in the minimum wage takes effect. That, along with rises in national insurance and other changes, may show the pressures employers are facing. Time will tell whether this is an indicator of a broader slowdown or a temporary effect.\u8221\'20\'1D <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LearnWorkUK/status/1922182827640053786"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/LearnWorkUK/status/1922182827640053786}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTime will tell whether this is an indicator of broader slowdown or a temporary effect.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u9997\'27\'0D L&W\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Stephen_EvansUK?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Stephen_EvansUK}}}\uc2  comments on employment falls in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/retail?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #retail}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/hospitality?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #hospitality}}}\uc2  sectors shown in today\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/labourmarket?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #labourmarket}}}\uc2  stats from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ONS}}}\uc2 .{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gfWc3BpqDz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/gfWc3BpqDz}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Learning and Work Institute (@LearnWorkUK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LearnWorkUK/status/1922182827640053786?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 According to the ONS, the number of employees in the accommodation and food services sector has fallen by 107,000 in the year to April, while there was an 87,000 rise in employees in the health and social work sector.\par \par \uc2 Updated <time datetime="2025-05-13T07:14:29.572Z">at 8.14am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T06:50:00.548Z">7.50am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 The increased cost of hiring workers in the UK is continuing to dampen the job market, according to the Institute of Directors.\par \par \uc2 Alex Hall-Chen , Principal Policy Advisor for Employment at the Institute of Directors , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s figures indicate declining employer demand for labour in the UK job market, with the number of payrolled employees decreasing on the month by 0.1% and vacancies falling by 42,000 on the quarter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe business case for hiring has been weakened by a perfect storm of last month\u8217\'20\'19s increased employer National Insurance Contributions and above-inflation increases to the minimum wage, alongside a wave of measures in the Employment Rights Bill which will make hiring staff riskier and costlier.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the government is to achieve its aim of an 80% employment rate, it must take urgent action to restore business confidence in hiring. We urge the government to support targeted changes to the Employment Rights Bill which would ensure that the Bill works for both businesses and employees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T06:48:32.664Z">7.48am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Economists: cracks appearing in UK jobs market\par \par \uc2 Economists are concerned that the UK\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market appears to have cooled in recent months, judging by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/may/13/uk-wage-growth-slows-payrolls-vacancies-drop-thames-water-mps-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-6822dc558f0894079b084796#block-6822dc558f0894079b084796"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s labour statistics}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Suren Thiru , ICAEW economics director, fears the employment market is cooling, saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CApril\u8217\'20\'19s drop in payrolled employment and unemployment rate increase suggests that cracks in the labour market are widening as the double whammy of rising National Insurance and National Living Wage hit employers, exacerbated by elevated global uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile wage growth remains uncomfortably high, the downward pressure from a struggling economy, softening jobs market and rising employment costs should help put pay settlements on a firmer downward path.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough more difficult times lie ahead for the jobs market with sinking business confidence and rising employment costs likely to drive a modest rise in unemployment, any increase should be limited by longstanding employer concerns over skills shortages.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis growing softness in the labour market is unlikely to be sufficient to persuade rate setters to depart from their current approach to gradually cutting interest rates, given lingering concerns over inflation and heightened global uncertainty.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Paige Tao , economist at PwC UK , suggests the weakening jobs market could spur the Bank of England towards further interest rate cuts:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf last month\u8217\'20\'19s labour market data hinted at an early response to upcoming employer tax rises, this month\u8217\'20\'19s figures confirm a clearer weakening. Unemployment ticked up for the first time in four months by 0.1 percentage point, payrolled employment fell by 33,000, and vacancies dropped further \u8212\'20\'14 staying below pre-pandemic levels for a second straight month. Heightened uncertainty around U.S. tariffs have likely added to the drag on business confidence.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWage growth has cooled, with annual earnings including bonuses easing by 0.2 percentage points in March \u8212\'20\'14 suggesting fading inflationary pressures from pay in advance of April\u8217\'20\'19s tax changes. As the Bank of England continues to balance inflation risks with growing weakness in the UK growth outlook, today\u8217\'20\'19s figures may indicate a green light for another rate cut at next month\u8217\'20\'19s MPC meeting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lindsay James , investment strategist at Quilter , fear today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report may be \u8220\'20\'1Cthe start of the expected slowdown\u8221\'20\'1D, adding:\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked up slightly to 4.5% in January to March 2025, while payrolled employee numbers fell by 47,000 between February and March 2025 and by 63,000 between March 2024 and March 2025. Job vacancies also dropped by 42,000 on the quarter to 761,000 in February to April 2025, marking the 34th consecutive quarterly decline.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGrowth in regular pay, excluding bonuses, fell to 5.6% between January and March 2024, as did total pay, including bonuses, which dipped marginally to 5.5%. Despite the slight fall, with inflation currently at 2.6%, wage growth is still outpacing inflation at more than double the rate.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T06:43:26.540Z">7.43am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 UK unemployment rate highest since summer 2021\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate has risen to its highest in almost four years, today\u8217\'20\'19s labour market data shows.\par \par \uc2 At 4.5% in January-March, the jobless rate for those aged 16 and over was the highest since June-August 2021, when it was also recorded at 4.5%. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/VPatelFX/status/1922180018777522338"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/VPatelFX/status/1922180018777522338}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 \u9888\'26\'A0 The single-month UK unemployment rates for Feb & Mar have been the highest since the pandemic. Even if we stay around these single month rates, the official UK unemployment rate is heading to at least 4.6%. Risks are skewed towards 5% earlier than the BoE forecasts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24GBP&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $GBP}}}\uc2  forecasts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/v7US8o0sU8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/v7US8o0sU8}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Viraj Patel (@VPatelFX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/VPatelFX/status/1922180018777522338?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Hyett , investment manager at Wealth Club, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK Labour market is painting a bit of a mixed picture. On the one hand unemployment is a touch higher than last month and vacancies are also shrinking. On the other, wages continue to grow well ahead of inflation, with sectors like retail, hotels and restaurants showing particular wage strength.\par \par \uc2 That doesn\u8217\'20\'19t bode well for businesses in the labour intensive hospitality and retail sectors - with higher wages and looming tax change both likely to push up costs. Fortunately a broad rise in wages suggests the consumer will be able to stomach some extra costs from higher prices.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T06:29:48.881Z">7.29am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 Here are more key facts from this morning\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The UK employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 years was estimated at 75.0% in January to March 2025. This is above estimates of a year ago, but largely unchanged in the latest quarter.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The UK unemployment rate for people aged 16 years and over was estimated at 4.5% in January to March 2025. This is above estimates of a year ago, and up in the latest quarter.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The UK economic inactivity rate for people aged 16 to 64 years was estimated at 21.4% in January to March 2025. This is below estimates of a year ago, and down in the latest quarter.\par \par \uc2 <time datetime="2025-05-13T06:26:04.881Z">7.26am BST</time>\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK jobs report shows wage growth slowdown, as vacancies drop\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Wage growth across the UK has slowed, and the number of people on payrolls has fallen, as Britain\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market continues to cool.\par \par \uc2 The latest UK labour market data, just released, shows that average regular earnings (excluding bonuses) rose by 5.6% in January to March 2025, down from 5.9% in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Growth in pay including bonuses also slowed \u8211\'20\'13 it rose by 5.5% in January-March, down from 5.7% in the final three months of 2024. <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1922170984401871087"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1922170984401871087}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 In January to March 2025, average weekly earnings were up 5.6% on the year excluding bonuses and 5.5% including bonuses. Regular pay grew fastest in the retail and hospitality sector.\par \par \uc2 Read the release \u10145\'27\'A1 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/fGCmGWT23L"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/fGCmGWT23L}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nubYNHu2bY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nubYNHu2bY}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Office for National Statistics (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1922170984401871087?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 Although wages growth slowed, earnings are still rising faster than prices in the shops. Once you adjust for inflation, pay (both regular and total) rose by 2.6% per year on both measures.\par \par \uc2 Minister for Employment, Alison McGovern has said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CReal wages are growing with around 200,000 more people into work since the publication of our Get Britain Working plan.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut we know that the Government\u8217\'20\'19s Plan for Change needs more workers \u8211\'20\'13 in every part of our country. That\u8217\'20\'19s why we will continue to change Jobcentres, invest in British industry, and get help to those who need it until everyone who can work has got a decent job and a good income.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But, today\u8217\'20\'19s report also shows a drop in demand for workers, as UK companies adjust to the increase in the minimum wage, and higher national insurance contributions, which kicked in at the start of April.\par \par \uc2 The Office for National Statistics reports that the number of payrolled employees felled by 33,000 in April, following a 47,000 drop in March.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, there were 106,000 fewer payrolled employees in April than a year ago, the ONS estimates.\par \par \uc2 In another sign that firms are being cautious, the number of vacancies in the UK fell by 42,000 in the January-March quarter, the 34th consecutive quarterly decline in a row. The biggest fall came in the construction sector <figure class="element element-tweet" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1922170987476304045"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1922170987476304045}}}\uc2 ">\par \par \uc2 There were 761,000 job vacancies in February to April 2025, down 42,000 on the quarter, with the biggest fall coming from construction.\par \par \uc2 Read the release \u10145\'27\'A1 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/OcuWFq6v95"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/OcuWFq6v95}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/mpc98cz5fP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/mpc98cz5fP}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Office for National Statistics (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1922170987476304045?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 13, 2025}}}\uc2  </figure>\par \par \uc2 ONS director of economic statistics Liz McKeown says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWage growth slowed slightly in the latest period but remains relatively strong, with public and private sectors now showing little difference.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe broader picture continues to be of the labour market cooling, with the number of employees on payroll falling in the first quarter of the year. The number of job vacancies has also fallen again, with the rate of decline increasing in the last few months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am BST: UK labour market report\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: ZEW survey of eurozone economic confidence\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: Environment, Food and Rural Affairs Committee (EFRA) to quiz the CEO, CFO and Chair of Thames Water\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US inflation report for April\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250513el5d000p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
