\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 I was secretary of labor. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s bizarre tariff scheme won\u8217\'20\'19t revive American jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 814 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2025\par 16:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Manufacturing and coalmining aren\u8217\'20\'19t the way to make America great again. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what would actually restore good-paying jobs\par \par \uc2 On Sunday night, the US announced that it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/12/china-us-agree-pause-trade-war-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cutting tariffs on Chinese imports}}}\uc2  from 145% to 30%, for 90 days, and the Chinese are dropping tariffs on US goods from 125% to 10%, also for 90 days.\par \par \uc2 The stock market soared on the news. (Anyone with inside knowledge of the deal could have made a killing.)\par \par \uc2 But what\u8217\'20\'19s the ultimate goal here? What happens over the next 90 days?\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to know what\u8217\'20\'19s in Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s mind (other than an insatiable thirst for power and money), but his trade and manufacturing adviser, \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , says the \u8220\'20\'1Cbigger picture is restoring the American manufacturing base\u8221\'20\'1D. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/may/09/americans-democracy-healthcare-union-taxes-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Protecting democracy is not enough: five things Americans must fight for | Huck Gutman}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 In 1970, more than a quarter of US workers held jobs in the manufacturing sector. Today, it\u8217\'20\'19s only about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/empsit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 8%}}}\uc2 . The Trump regime says sweeping tariffs will reverse this decades-long decline.\par \par \uc2 The president also promises to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/08/trump-executiver-order-coal-power-plants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revive the US coal industry}}}\uc2 . Last month he issued an executive order to rescind regulations limiting coal development. Last week, he even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/05/10/climate/social-cost-carbon-trump.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered federal agencies}}}\uc2  to stop considering the economic damage caused by coal and other carbon emissions when writing regulations. And he\u8217\'20\'19s doing whatever he can to destroy green energy \u8211\'20\'13 solar and wind.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing? Coalmining?\par \par \uc2 These sorts of jobs won\u8217\'20\'19t make America great again. They\u8217\'20\'19ll make American workers sickened, injured and killed on the job again.\par \par \uc2 I was secretary of labor. I saw close up how awful most of these jobs are. I met workers who had lost limbs in manufacturing jobs because machines they were cleaning or repairing accidentally started up. Others I met suffered carpal tunnel injuries from repetitive motions on assembly lines.\par \par \uc2 Child labor was \u8211\'20\'13 and still is \u8211\'20\'13 a pervasive problem. So, too, with dangerous sweatshops crammed with people working for subminimum wages (if they\u8217\'20\'19re lucky enough to be paid at all).\par \par \uc2 And mining? I met mine workers with black lung disease from exposure to coal dust. Despite being on the decline after the 1970s, black lung has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://publichealth.uic.edu/news-stories/what-is-causing-the-rise-in-black-lung-disease/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 been on the rise}}}\uc2  for two decades. Changes in mining technology have let mine owners dig deeper, exposing miners to even more highly toxic silica.\par \par \uc2 The reason many working-class Americans want manufacturing and mining to return is not because they loved the work. In most cases it was grueling and dangerous or mind-numbingly boring.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s because that work paid far better than the service jobs in fast-food and retail outlets, hospitals and hotels that many have been forced to take in their absence.\par \par \uc2 And the reason those manufacturing and mining jobs paid far better was because they were unionized. Unions gave workers in manufacturing and mining the bargaining leverage they needed to force employers to offer better pay (along with more job security and safer working conditions). <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/apr/01/trump-workers-unions-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump claimed he was pro-worker. His new order shows how absurd that was | Steven Greenhouse}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 But since the Reagan years, corporations have been busting unions. In the 1960s, a third of all workers in the private sector were unionized. Today, just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/265958/percentage-workers-union-members.aspx#:~:text=An%20analysis%20of%20Gallup's%202023,of%20all%20private%2Dsector%20employees."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 6% are}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if there\u8217\'20\'19s one thing Trump and his cronies don\u8217\'20\'19t want, it\u8217\'20\'19s unions giving workers more bargaining power. He\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/apr/28/doge-nlrb-cuts-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gutted the National Labor Relations Board}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2019/apr/25/trump-safety-cuts-workplace-deaths-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eviscerated Osha}}}\uc2 , and is filling the Department of Labor with corporate stooges.\par \par \uc2 So even if it were possible to restore manufacturing and mining to the US \u8211\'20\'13 even if we stopped foreign trade and forced US consumers to pay enormous sums for stuff made here, even if we filled the atmosphere with carbon, even if we prevented new technologies (including artificial intelligence) from doing the manufacturing and mining jobs that returned \u8211\'20\'13 the American working class would be no better off than they are now, if their pay is still in the cellar.\par \par \uc2 A return of manufacturing and mining jobs won\u8217\'20\'19t make Americans great again. Good-paying jobs will.\par \par \uc2 The easiest and best way to raise the pay of working-class Americans is to 1 strengthen unions, 2 raise the minimum wage (which hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been increased since 2009), 3 expand the earned income tax credit (a wage subsidy) for lower-income workers and 4 institute a universal basic income.\par \par \uc2 Will Trump do any of these things? No chance in hell.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich, a former US secretary of labor, is a professor of public policy emeritus at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 . He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250513el5d00237\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
