\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 For Silicon Valley, AI isn\u8217\'20\'19t just about replacing some jobs. It\u8217\'20\'19s about replacing all of them\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Newton-Rex \par \uc2 991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2025\par 17:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 AI will do the thinking, robots will do the doing. What place do humans have in this arrangement \u8211\'20\'13 and do tech CEOs care?\par \par \uc2 I recently found myself at a dinner in an upstairs room at a restaurant in San Francisco hosted by a venture capital firm. The after-dinner speaker was a tech veteran who, having sold his AI company for hundreds of millions of dollars, has now turned his hand to investing. He had a simple message for the assembled startup founders: the money you can make in AI isn\u8217\'20\'19t limited to the paltry market sizes of previous technology waves. You can replace the world\u8217\'20\'19s workers \u8211\'20\'13 which means you can capture their salaries. All of them.\par \par \uc2 Replacing all human labour with AI sounds like the stuff of science fiction. But it is the explicit aim of a growing number of the tech elite \u8211\'20\'13 and these are people who lack neither drive nor resources, who have deep pockets and even deeper determination. If they say they want to automate all labour, we should take them at their word.\par \par \uc2 This is generally an aim that\u8217\'20\'19s only admitted to behind closed doors, for obvious reasons. There\u8217\'20\'19s little that will summon the pitchforks quicker than telling people you\u8217\'20\'19re trying to take away their jobs. But a company called Mechanize last month bucked the trend and said the quiet part out loud. Their vision is \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/MechanizeWork/status/1912904151874625928"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the full automation of the economy}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, a vision they\u8217\'20\'19ve convinced some of the biggest names in Silicon Valley to fund, including \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief scientist, \uc2 Jeff Dean\uc2 , and popular podcaster Dwarkesh Patel.\par \par \uc2 Is automating all jobs really feasible? \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  certainly thinks so. The rise of AI and robotics will mean \u8220\'20\'1Cprobably {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2024/05/23/tech/elon-musk-ai-your-job/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 none of us will have a job}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, he said last year. \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  thinks humans soon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://people.com/bill-gates-ai-will-replace-doctors-teachers-in-next-10-years-11705615"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won\u8217\'20\'19t be needed for \u8220\'20\'1Cmost things\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 . Massive labour replacement has also been predicted by godfather of AI {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tech.yahoo.com/ai/articles/ai-godfather-geoffrey-hinton-says-175605931.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Geoffrey Hinton}}}\uc2  and billionaire investor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/2024/09/24/silicon-valley-billionaire-vinod-khosla-universal-basic-income-ai-80-jobs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vinod Khosla}}}\uc2 . These are hardly fringe voices that have no idea what they\u8217\'20\'19re talking about.\par \par \uc2 Some careers are obviously safe from robot takeover. Taylor Swift is not in danger. Nor is Harry Kane. Nor, for that matter, is Keir Starmer, or the as-yet-unnamed next archbishop of Canterbury. Famous artist, sportsperson, politician, priest \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps the four jobs that are the most resistant to automation. Unfortunately they\u8217\'20\'19re not open to all of us.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s technology cannot replace all human labour. AI makes mistakes. Robots lack coordination, dexterity, versatility. So that\u8217\'20\'19s something. But there is lots that cutting-edge technology can already do. And there are good reasons to think it will continue to improve \u8211\'20\'13 fast.\par \par \uc2 GPT-4, one of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s large language models, was already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdn.openai.com/papers/gpt-4.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scoring in the top 10%}}}\uc2  on the bar exam back in 2023. Their more recent models are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/12/20/technology/openai-new-ai-math-science.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 better at coding}}}\uc2  than their own chief scientist. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.techradar.com/pro/chatgpt-has-caused-a-massive-drop-in-demand-for-online-digital-freelancers-here-is-what-you-can-do-to-protect-yourself"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Freelance writing jobs plummeted}}}\uc2  when ChatGPT was released; the same happened to graphic design jobs with the arrival of AI image generators. Driverless cars are everywhere in San Francisco. As \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  himself said: \u8220\'20\'1CJobs are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2023/09/sam-altman-openai-chatgpt-gpt-4/674764/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 definitely going to go away}}}\uc2 , full stop.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While AI grabs most of the headlines, robots are advancing rapidly too. And where AI threatens white-collar jobs, robots target physical labour. One type of humanoid robot is already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.press.bmwgroup.com/global/article/detail/T0444265EN/successful-test-of-humanoid-robots-at-bmw-group-plant-spartanburg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 being tested}}}\uc2  in \uc2 BMW\uc2  factories; another managed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.therobotreport.com/sanctuary-ai-latest-phoenix-humanoid-can-learn-tasks-in-24-hours/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 master more than 100 tasks}}}\uc2  that would usually be done by human store workers. Companies plan to start testing robots in the home {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/27/figure-will-start-alpha-testing-its-humanoid-robot-in-the-home-in-2025/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this year}}}\uc2 . The Silicon Valley vision for the labour market is remarkably simple: AI does the thinking, robots do the doing. What place do humans have in this arrangement?\par \par \uc2 Up until very recently, AI researchers thought that artificial general intelligence (AGI) \u8211\'20\'13 that is, AI that can perform essentially all cognitive tasks at human level \u8211\'20\'13 was a long way off. Not any more. Demis Hassabis, the head of \uc2 Google DeepMind\uc2 , now thinks \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/vitrupo/status/1915006240134234608"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it\u8217\'20\'19s coming very soon}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 less than five to 10 years wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t surprise him.\par \par \uc2 Of course these predictions may be wrong. Perhaps we\u8217\'20\'19re headed for another AI winter; perhaps the chatbots will stop improving, the robots will keep falling over, the funding will move on to the next big thing in tech. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think so, but it\u8217\'20\'19s possible. But that\u8217\'20\'19s not the point. The question here isn\u8217\'20\'19t whether the legions of tech CEOs and billions of dollars of funding being poured into near-total labour automation will achieve what they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to achieve. The question is why they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to achieve it at all, and how the rest of us feel about it. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2025/may/04/the-big-idea-can-we-stop-ai-making-humans-obsolete"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Better at everything: how AI could make human beings irrelevant}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 The generous answer is that they genuinely believe a post-labour economy will mean huge economic growth and vastly improved global living standards. The obvious question is what, historically speaking, suggests that the benefits of this growth would be distributed evenly.\par \par \uc2 The less generous answer is that it\u8217\'20\'19s about what it\u8217\'20\'19s always about: money. Venture capitalist \uc2 Marc Andreessen\uc2  once famously said: \u8220\'20\'1CSoftware is eating the world.\u8221\'20\'1D Up until now there\u8217\'20\'19s only been so much it could eat. Whatever software you built, you still needed people to do most of the world\u8217\'20\'19s work, with the labour market itself tantalisingly out of reach for ambitious tech execs. But now Silicon Valley sees an opening. A chance to own the entire means of production. And it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be Silicon Valley if it didn\u8217\'20\'19t try to seize that chance.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ed Newton-Rex is the founder of Fairly Trained, a non-profit that certifies generative AI companies that respect creators\u8217\'20\'19 rights, and a visiting scholar at \uc2 Stanford University\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250512el5c000rw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
