\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Tone deaf\u8217\'20\'19: US tech company responsible for global IT outage to cut jobs and use AI\b0\par\par\uc2 Josh Taylor Technology reporter \par \uc2 786 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2025\par 07:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2  CEO announces 5% of workforce to be slashed globally, citing artificial intelligence efficiencies created in the business\par \par \uc2 The cybersecurity company that became a household name after causing a massive global IT outage last year has announced it will cut 5% of its workforce in part due to \u8220\'20\'1CAI efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a note to staff earlier this week, released in stock market filings in the US, \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 George Kurtz\uc2 , announced that 500 positions, or 5% of its workforce, would be cut globally, citing AI efficiencies created in the business.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re operating in a market and technology inflection point, with AI reshaping every industry, accelerating threats, and evolving customer needs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Kurtz said AI \u8220\'20\'1Cflattens our hiring curve, and helps us innovate from idea to product faster\u8221\'20\'1D, adding it \u8220\'20\'1Cdrives efficiencies across both the front and back office\u8221\'20\'1D. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/sep/24/crowdstrike-outage-microsoft-apology"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CrowdStrike apologizes for global IT outage in congressional testimony}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAI is a force multiplier throughout the business,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Other reasons for the cuts included market demand for sustained growth and expanding the product offering.\par \par \uc2 The company expects to incur up to US$53m in costs as a result of the job cuts.\par \par \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2  reported in March revenue of US$1bn for the fourth financial quarter of 2025, up 25% on the same quarter in 2024, with a loss of US$92m.\par \par \uc2 In July last year, \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2  pushed out a faulty update to its software designed to detect cybersecurity threats that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/sep/24/crowdstrike-outage-microsoft-apology"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought down 8.5m Windows systems worldwide}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The outage caused chaos at airports, and took down computers in hospitals, TV networks, payment systems and people\u8217\'20\'19s personal computers.\par \par \uc2 Aaron McEwan, vice-president of research and advisory at consultancy \uc2 Gartner\uc2 , said he was sceptical when companies announced AI efficiencies close to reduced revenue forecasts, as \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2  had in March.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think particularly in the tech sector \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s a way of justifying a reduction in the workforce because [of] a financial issue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo either they\u8217\'20\'19re not tracking well financially, or they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to send a message to investors that good times are around the corner. So I\u8217\'20\'19m immediately sceptical.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McEwan said companies were facing pressure to deliver on the big investments made in AI.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe productivity gains that we expect to see from AI just aren\u8217\'20\'19t flowing through.\u8221\'20\'1D <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2025/may/04/the-big-idea-can-we-stop-ai-making-humans-obsolete"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Better at everything: how AI could make human beings irrelevant}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Gartner\uc2  research showed across workforces less than 50% of employees are using AI in their job, and only 8% of employees are using AI tools to improve productivity.\par \par \uc2 Toby Walsh, professor of artificial intelligence at the \uc2 University of New South Wales\uc2 , said \uc2 CrowdStrike\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s announcement was \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty tone deaf\u8221\'20\'1D after the outage last year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey would have been better redeploying this 5% of people to emergency response and bug fixing,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Walsh said the market should expect more of these announcements in future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty simple: more profits for companies, less work for workers. But we should learn from the first Industrial Revolution. If we stand up in solidarity, we can use these savings to improve quality and quantity of work for all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Niusha Shafiabady, associate professor in computational intelligence at the \uc2 Australian Catholic University\uc2 , said AI job replacements were an \u8220\'20\'1Cunavoidable reality\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo matter what we believe is moral and right, this change will happen. Unfortunately, a lot of people will lose their traditional jobs to AI and technology,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf [companies] see that they are saving money by using AI and technology and enhancing their services, they will ask their employees to leave. This is the reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  report in 2023 found nearly 23% of all jobs globally will change in the next five years due to AI and other macroeconomic trends. While 69m jobs are expected to be created, 83m jobs could be eliminated, leading to a net decrease of 2%, Shafiabady said.\par \par \uc2 McEwan said companies \u8211\'20\'13 tech companies in particular \u8211\'20\'13 would be looking for ways to use AI to reduce workforces over time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have no doubt that there will be the emergence of companies that are able to reduce their workforce and substantially because of AI,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19ll depend on the type of product that they\u8217\'20\'19re selling. But at the moment most companies would be wise to look at how they can use AI to augment their workforce rather than replace.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Has your job been lost to AI? Get in touch \u8211\'20\'13 josh.taylor@theguardian.com\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250509el590015t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
