\par\par \uc2 Music\par \b \uc2 Drug runs for the Stones, a love triangle with Joni Mitchell: has Chris O\u8217\'20\'19Dell had the music industry\u8217\'20\'19s wildest career?\b0\par\par\uc2 Christian Kriticos \par \uc2 1,548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2025\par 08:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 She managed tours for Queen and Led Zeppelin and was at George Harrison\u8217\'20\'19s house when the Beatles split \u8211\'20\'13 but O\u8217\'20\'19Dell is most proud of her legacy as a woman in a macho industry\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18When you start with the Beatles, your r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 looks pretty good,\u8221\'20\'1D Chris O\u8217\'20\'19Dell says. A new documentary charts the extent of that CV, as a music manager with bands including Fleetwood Mac, Genesis and Santana, but it all began with a chance encounter in Los Angeles in 1968.\par \par \uc2 Derek Taylor was heading publicity at the Beatles\u8217\'20\'19 company, \uc2 Apple Corps\uc2 , and O\u8217\'20\'19Dell was a low-level assistant in radio promotion. When Taylor suggested she come work at the \uc2 Apple\uc2  office in London, she dropped everything and moved halfway across the world. \u8220\'20\'1CPaul [McCartney] was there every day organising everything,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, on the phone from her home in Arizona. \u8220\'20\'1COne day he came into my office and said, \u8216\'20\'18Chris, should we use paper towels or cloth towels in the bathroom?\u8217\'20\'19 That\u8217\'20\'19s how detailed he was.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Dell didn\u8217\'20\'19t just stay at her desk: she joined the Beatles in the studio to sing on the epic climax to Hey Jude, was on the roof of \uc2 Apple\uc2  headquarters for their last ever live show, and even moved in with George Harrison and his wife \u8211\'20\'13 she was at their house when McCartney announced he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be playing in the Beatles any more. \u8220\'20\'1CI came down for breakfast and every newspaper was on the table, saying: \u8216\'20\'18Paul quits.\u8217\'20\'19 It felt so abrupt and so unexpected. George went out in the garden, because that\u8217\'20\'19s where he went for his peace. That afternoon, John [Lennon] came over and he and George spent quite a long time walking together.\u8221\'20\'1D <figure class="element element-video" data-canonical-url="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2XhgTs7lvVY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2XhgTs7lvVY}}}\uc2 " > </figure>\par \par \uc2 Even after the band split, O\u8217\'20\'19Dell remained in their orbit. \u8220\'20\'1CI got to know John better when he was with May Pang,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, referring to Lennon\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clost weekend\u8221\'20\'1D in the mid-1970s. \u8220\'20\'1CWe all lived in this beach house in Santa Monica. That was the closest I ever got to John. The time before, when he was with Yoko [Ono], they were really focused on what they were doing. So I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know him as intimately \u8211\'20\'13 if I can use that word.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Dell might hesitate over that term because her relationship with another Beatle became a lot more intimate: in her memoir, published in 2009, she wrote candidly about her turbulent affair with Ringo Starr in the 1970s. But now she prefers to focus on how they\u8217\'20\'19ve remained on good terms: \u8220\'20\'1CI could honestly say he\u8217\'20\'19s a friend.\u8221\'20\'1D Like all her remarkable anecdotes, this is told with an easy-going charm. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve stayed in touch up until probably the last couple of years. He\u8217\'20\'19s the godfather to my son.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Harrison, though, is the Beatle she is most closely associated with among music fans, thanks to the song he wrote about her: Miss O\u8217\'20\'19Dell, which gives the new documentary its name. The song came about in 1971, when they were both in Los Angeles. \u8220\'20\'1CGeorge kept calling me,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Dell says. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to go over. I thought there were going to be all these hangers-on.\u8221\'20\'1D Inspired by her absence, Harrison wrote the song, with its pleading refrain, \u8220\'20\'1CWhy don\u8217\'20\'19t you call me, Miss O\u8217\'20\'19Dell?\u8221\'20\'1D It was the B-side to Give Me Love (Give Me Peace on Earth), which hit No 1 in the US. \u8220\'20\'1CI was really happy every time I heard the A-side because I thought, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m spinning around as the B-side right now,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Dell expands on this story (and many more) in the new documentary, which sees her return to key sites such as the \uc2 Apple\uc2  offices on London\u8217\'20\'19s Wigmore Street and Saville Row. The film also reunites her with Harrison\u8217\'20\'19s then-wife, Pattie Boyd, and \uc2 Apple\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s head of A&R, Peter Asher. But the documentary goes beyond her time with the Beatles, showing how she built on that remarkable first experience to develop an even more extraordinary CV.\par \par \uc2 Soon after, she was working as an assistant to the Rolling Stones during the Exile on Main St sessions \u8211\'20\'13 she even appears on the artwork for the album. O\u8217\'20\'19Dell once joked that her job description should have included: \u8220\'20\'1CSleep with Mick [Jagger] whenever he asks.\u8221\'20\'1D But her memories of him now are more innocent. \u8220\'20\'1CHe was like a brother,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 That fraternal loyalty might have been on display when Jagger got O\u8217\'20\'19Dell her first job on the road, working on the Rolling Stones\u8217\'20\'19 tour in 1972. But some of her duties were a bit unconventional. \u8220\'20\'1CKeith [Richards] sent me on a drug run,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalls. \u8220\'20\'1CBack then, it was just called being an assistant.\u8221\'20\'1D She joined in with the debauchery, and one comment from Richards sticks in her mind all these years later. \u8220\'20\'1CHe said, \u8216\'20\'18You know, Chris, you can keep up with the guys.\u8217\'20\'19 I thought that was a compliment then. Today I don\u8217\'20\'19t see it quite that way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gradually, O\u8217\'20\'19Dell built a career as one of rock\u8217\'20\'19s first female tour managers. With Crosby, Stills, Nash & Young, she gained her first experience managing travel logistics. \u8220\'20\'1CThat was a nightmare,\u8221\'20\'1D she remembers wryly. But working for Linda Ronstadt in 1975 was a breakthrough, allowing her to be \u8220\'20\'1Cin charge of everything\u8221\'20\'1D for the first time. She also brought some much-needed sensitivity to the often boorish and brutal world of tour management. \u8220\'20\'1COne time before Mother\u8217\'20\'19s Day I went to Linda and asked, \u8216\'20\'18Do you want me to send flowers to your mom?\u8217\'20\'19 She said, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ve never had a tour manager who asked me that question.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another highlight was working on Bob Dylan\u8217\'20\'19s mid-1970s Rolling Thunder Revue. \u8220\'20\'1CI found Bob extremely fun to work with,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI remember, he and I were sitting at a table in a restaurant one time. We just talked about all kinds of different things. But never did his eyes leave mine. Ever. He is highly focused.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During the Revue, O\u8217\'20\'19Dell became the subject of another song. \u8220\'20\'1CJoni Mitchell came on the tour and we got caught up in a bit of a love triangle with Sam Shepard. I\u8217\'20\'19d already been sort of seeing him, and then she had a thing with him. This dude was married and had a wife back in California! So what was wrong with us?\u8221\'20\'1D she says, disbelievingly. \u8220\'20\'1CFrom that came the song Coyote about \u8216\'20\'18a woman at home\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18another woman down the hall\u8217\'20\'19. I am that woman down the hall.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As O\u8217\'20\'19Dell remembers, the drama unfolded over just a few days, with Mitchell debuting the song at Gordon Lightfoot\u8217\'20\'19s home during a stop on the tour (an incident captured in Martin Scorsese\u8217\'20\'19s 2019 film Rolling Thunder Revue: A Bob Dylan Story). \u8220\'20\'1CI had heard she\u8217\'20\'19d written this song and I thought, \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t know if I want to hear this!\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Dell recalls. But in her typical easy-going way, O\u8217\'20\'19Dell seems to have been able to patch things up: \u8220\'20\'1CShe and I became really good friends after that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She spent the latter part of her career as a tour manager in Germany in the 80s, adding more bands to her r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 including Led Zeppelin and Queen, but Echo and the Bunnymen proved to be her last job. \u8220\'20\'1CI was just like: I\u8217\'20\'19m done. I don\u8217\'20\'19t know who these people are and they want me to get them a towel and I probably am not gonna do that any more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Drugs and alcohol had also taken their toll. \u8220\'20\'1CI had consumed quite a lot\u8221\'20\'1D, O\u8217\'20\'19Dell says. She quit touring to get a master\u8217\'20\'19s degree and became a therapist working with people with addictions: \u8220\'20\'1CI had been in recovery myself, so it was a natural transition.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She gradually lost contact with many of the male rock stars she worked with, but is still \u8220\'20\'1Cvery much in touch with the women\u8221\'20\'1D. She recently launched{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/@chrisodell7373/videos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a YouTube series}}}\uc2  chronicling her conversations with the likes of Suzi Ronson (David Bowie\u8217\'20\'19s hair stylist) and her old friend May Pang. She plans to release some older interviews too, which she did in the 1980s with Linda McCartney and several other women, possibly as a documentary or book: \u8220\'20\'1CIt was so amazing because they talked about how they really felt,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Dell says.\par \par \uc2 No matter what she does next, the Beatles will probably continue to dominate Chris O\u8217\'20\'19Dell\u8217\'20\'19s story. But, she says, \u8220\'20\'1CI worked with over 21 bands. That is something to be quite proud of \u8211\'20\'13 being the first woman to really operate in the music world on the road. I can look back and say, \u8216\'20\'18That was a good legacy.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Miss O\u8217\'20\'19Dell is streaming from 13 May on Peacock, Prime Video, Tubi and other platforms. It will also be available on DVD and Blu-ray.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250507el57001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
