\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Federal workers in limbo amid whiplash White House firings and court-ordered rehirings\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino and Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2025\par 14:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump and Musk\u8217\'20\'19s Doge agency have spread confusion and uncertainty among government employees\par \par \uc2 In the 100 days since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  returned to power, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new administration}}}\uc2  has driven tens of thousands of federal workers from a civil service it has denigrated as \u8220\'20\'1Cbloated\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrupt\u8221\'20\'1D. Among its first targets: probationary workers like Cindi Hron.\par \par \uc2 Hron, a landscape architect with the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2 , was one of thousands abruptly fired across the agency on 14 February, in an action some now refer to as the Valentine\u8217\'20\'19s Day massacre, executed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s newly empowered \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D, or Doge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs much as I could see it coming, it was still a gut punch,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Hron was given just two hours to return her equipment before losing access to her government email.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks that followed, similar scenes played out across the government \u8211\'20\'13 nurses at the Department of Veterans Affairs, food safety regulators at the Department of Agriculture, scientists at the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 . Employees, along with unions and legal groups, challenged the legality of the widespread terminations. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/18/trump-administration-reinstating-fired-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 At least 24,000}}}\uc2  of those dismissals led to court-ordered reinstatements, which have since been paused.\par \par \uc2 Whiplashing court orders have left probationary workers in a fragile state of limbo. Some have been rehired, while others remain on administrative leave. Some have received backpay and benefits, while others have not. Some were returned to work, put on administrative leave and then fired again, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/08/trump-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an intervention by the supreme court}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a state of confusion as to anybody\u8217\'20\'19s status,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jeffrey Grant, a former deputy director at the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hron had already moved back to Pennsylvania, from where she had relocated to accept the Forest Service job in Utah, when she learned that she would be reinstated, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usda.gov/about-usda/news/press-releases/2025/03/11/usda-status-update-probationary-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 compliance}}}\uc2  with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://democracyforward.org/updates/mspb030525/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 , an independent agency responsible for protecting federal employees. The reversal was welcome but the return to work felt precarious. When her department extended a second \u8220\'20\'1Cdeferred resignation\u8221\'20\'1D offer, she decided to take it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDoge has just taken a baseball bat and it\u8217\'20\'19s just breaking things,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s certainly not the way I ever anticipated leaving a job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Probationary workers are employees who generally have less than a year in their role after being hired or promoted, though they are often highly skilled and have years of prior experience in the federal government. While they lack the full job protections of longer-tenured civil servants during this period, probationary workers can only be removed for poor performance or conduct.\par \par \uc2 In late April, a federal judge in California said the administration\u8217\'20\'19s suggestion that tens of thousands of probationary workers were dismissed because of their performance was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/trump-administration-ordered-retract-sham-rationale-firing-workers-2025-04-21/#:~:text=U.S.%20District%20Judge%20William%20Alsup,that%20could%20hobble%20their%20careers."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ca total sham\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  and order several agencies to provide a retraction in writing.\par \par \uc2 But in a blow to efforts to challenge their removals, the office of special counsel, an independent watchdog agency, announced it was dropping its inquiry into a torrent of complaints that the Trump administration had unlawfully terminated probationary workers. The decision was a reversal of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2025/02/24/hampton-dellinger-special-counsel-filing-probationary-employees/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conclusion}}}\uc2  reached by the previous head of the agency, Hampton Dellinger, who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/02/trumps-firing-of-watchdog-agency-chief-and-would-give-license-to-bully-officials-judge-rules"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired by Trump}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Craig Becker, senior counsel for the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , which is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/district-courts/california/candce/3:2025cv01780/444883/45/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 challenging the administration\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  firings of probationary employees, said a legal defense network established to provide assistance to federal workers has fielded many calls from employees struggling with questions over benefits and job status.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been a hellish way of treating people,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tom Di Liberto, a public affairs specialist and climate scientist at the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  (Noaa) who was among the probationary employees dismissed in February, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/13/federal-court-orders-fired-workers-doge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rehired and put on administrative leave in March}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/10/noaa-firings-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dismissed again on 10 April.}}}\par \par \uc2 Di Liberto\u8217\'20\'19s probationary period would have ended before he was refired on 10 April, but the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/10/noaa-firings-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 termination was backdated to 27 February}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey didn\u8217\'20\'19t want us basically to be able to say that we had employment,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sure that\u8217\'20\'19s not legal, but I\u8217\'20\'19m sure that\u8217\'20\'19s what the courts will decide.\u8221\'20\'1D <aside class="element element-pullquote element--inline">\par \par \uc2 This is happening to us first, but the ripple effects of not having us will be absolutely enormous. <footer> A reinstated probationary employee with the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2  </footer> </aside>\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 It is not clear how many federal employees were in a probationary status when Doge began its mass firings earlier this year. An analysis by the \uc2 Partnership for Public Service{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bit.ly/ProbationaryPeriods"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimates}}}\uc2  the number was roughly 250,000. At the time Trump won the election in November, the federal workforce employed{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/01/07/what-the-data-says-about-federal-workers/#how-many-federal-workers-are-there"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  just over 3 million people}}}\uc2 , excluding the roughly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dwp.dmdc.osd.mil/dwp/app/dod-data-reports/workforce-reports"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.3 million active-duty military personnel}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Musk initially promised Doge would slash $1tn \u8211\'20\'13 a sum so large he and Trump suggested some of the savings could be distributed to taxpayers. This month, Musk revised his target to $150bn \u8211\'20\'13 just 2% of the federal budget. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doge.gov/savings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tracker}}}\uc2  of the savings Doge claims to have achieved is riddled with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/13/us/politics/doge-contracts-savings.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 well-documented}}}\uc2  errors, duplications and inaccuracies.\par \par \uc2 The Doge team\u8217\'20\'19s efforts have also cost billions of dollars. The \uc2 Partnership for Public Service{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20250426003035/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/24/us/politics/musk-cuts.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  Doge\u8217\'20\'19s firings, rehirings, lost productivity and paid leave will cost upwards of $135bn this fiscal year, not including costs associated with court battles their actions have incited.\par \par \uc2 Americans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/22/polls/doge-elon-musk.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 broadly support}}}\uc2  Doge\u8217\'20\'19s stated mission \u8211\'20\'13 tackling waste and inefficiency in government. And experts on the federal bureaucracy as well as workers themselves acknowledge that reforms are needed.\par \par \uc2 But the stories of fired workers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/21/democrats-attorneys-general-tour-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shared at town halls}}}\uc2  and protests, have helped rally raucous \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox10phoenix.com/news/creative-arts-therapist-phoenix-va-let-go-amid-federal-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and even poignant}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 opposition to Musk\u8217\'20\'19s government-slashing initiative. Polls show Musk and Doge are increasingly unpopular. According to a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1behUPOujHZSkw37RTdnt_2pCJESMDrglY_uYUeoWdvg/edit?gid=0#gid=0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post/ABC News/Ipsos poll}}}\uc2 , 56% of respondents agreed that Trump was \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing too far\u8221\'20\'1D in laying off government employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEfficiency is a cloak for a Project 2025 operation to gut the federal workforce and replace career civil servants with Trump loyalists,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rob Shriver, the former director of the office of personnel management, referring to the far-right blueprint for overhauling the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Shriver, who now serves as managing director of the Civil Service Strong initiative at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://democracyforward.org/action/donate/?gad_source=1&gad_campaignid=22232973762&gbraid=0AAAAAqhhMDzo1lHPO4psc1PS_n_kncgDd&gclid=Cj0KCQjw2tHABhCiARIsANZzDWqw5LU909Op_gTMih2bUW3-UVpnnTHXpWobuKwkxO58IA8njqK2CFIaAgqKEALw_wcB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democracy Forward}}}\uc2 , a legal group contesting the administration\u8217\'20\'19s sweeping dismissals of government employees, expressed concern that Doge\u8217\'20\'19s brute-force approach to government cost-cutting could have lasting repercussions on the civil service.\par \par \uc2 The administration\u8217\'20\'19s treatment of federal workers risked eroding longstanding expectations of job security and confidence in its public service mission \u8211\'20\'13 advantages that help the government recruit new talent.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that the stability has been completely undermined by these antics,\u8221\'20\'1D Shriver said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I think folks have a lot of questions about what they would be asked to do were they to come in and work for a federal agency \u8211\'20\'13 whether what they would be asked to do would be in line with their values as somebody who wants to serve the public and takes an oath to the constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even after Musk said recently he would step back from leading Doge, the administration has made clear its government-shrinking crusade is far from complete. And probationary workers say they feel more vulnerable than ever. Last week, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/04/strengthening-probationary-periods-in-the-federal-service/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed an executive order}}}\uc2  making it easier to fire probationary employees by expanding agency discretion over whether they attain full status.\par \par \uc2 Doge did not reply to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump is the chief executive of the executive branch and reserves the right to fire anyone he wants,\u8221\'20\'1D the White House press secretary, Karoline Leavitt, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The day-to-day stress brought about by the administration\u8217\'20\'19s demonization of federal workers as \u8220\'20\'1Cdeep state\u8221\'20\'1D bureaucrats working to thwart the president\u8217\'20\'19s agenda has left the workforce deeply demoralized.\par \par \uc2 One reinstated probationary employee with the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2  in Alaska, who spoke on condition of anonymity for fear of professional reprisal, said some days she feels so \u8220\'20\'1Cdowntrodden\u8221\'20\'1D that she can\u8217\'20\'19t stop crying. At the same time she feels a deep sense of resolve to \u8220\'20\'1Chold the line\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cnot comply in advance\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are the indicator species in this situation,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is happening to us first, but the ripple effects of not having us \u8211\'20\'13 and not having all of those protections that the federal government provides for the resources that people care so much about, and the lands that they subsist on \u8211\'20\'13 will be absolutely enormous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The anxiety and fatigue is by design. A destabilized civil service was central to the vision laid out by Russell Vought, head of the office of management and budget and chief architect of Project 2025, who once {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/10/who-is-russell-vought-trump-office-of-management-and-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CWe want the bureaucrats to be traumatically affected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Multiple probationary employees told the Guardian they expect to be targeted in future rounds of layoffs. Several said they were pursuing other opportunities or had accepted their agency\u8217\'20\'19s buyout offer, while some remained hopeful they would prevail in court.\par \par \uc2 The mass exodus of probationary workers has already disrupted government functions and services \u8211\'20\'13 and is raising alarms about the loss of scientific and technical expertise.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US has been a leader in hurricane science and in weather forecasting,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andy Hazelton, a physical scientist who worked on hurricane modeling at Noaa. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have so much talent, so much knowledge and data. But everything that\u8217\'20\'19s happened, it\u8217\'20\'19s kind of threatening that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hazelton, who was among the probationary employees caught in the chaotic cycle of being fired, reinstated and dismissed again, said Noaa staff were already stretched thin. Staff reductions, coupled with proposed budget cuts, could affect work on the agency\u8217\'20\'19s lifesaving forecasts just as hurricane season approaches, he warned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI worry that the forecasts, perhaps the improvements we\u8217\'20\'19ve come to rely on, may not be as reliable going forward,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 As Hron prepares to leave the Forest Service, she has been thinking about the oath she took nearly one year ago.\par \par \uc2 During a security training as part of her onboarding, Hron remembers incorrectly answering a question about the type of threat facing the agency. She assumed the answer was \u8220\'20\'1Cforeign\u8221\'20\'1D, but the correct response was \u8220\'20\'1Cdomestic\u8221\'20\'1D. At the time, she found it surprising.\par \par \uc2 Now she has come to believe that the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s war on federal workers is the very kind of domestic threat the agency warned of: \u8220\'20\'1CWe just departed from the ethics, the guidelines and policies that shape what I understood the Forest Service to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250504el54001us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
