\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Why you shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t trust everything you read about the US\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18fed up\u8217\'20\'19 workforce\b0\par\par\uc2 Gene Marks \par \uc2 550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2025\par 14:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Despite rumors of \u8216\'20\'18quiet quitting\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18coffee badging\u8217\'20\'19, people know work is work and the perfect job doesn\u8217\'20\'19t exist\par \par \uc2 If you read the media, you\u8217\'20\'19ll walk away with this impression: American employees are fed up. They\u8217\'20\'19re doing the \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bare_minimum_Monday"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bare minimum}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D on Mondays, \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/councils/forbeshumanresourcescouncil/2024/03/07/career-cushioning-a-preventative-measure-in-uncertain-times/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cushioning}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D their careers with side gigs, \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/when-work-gets-too-frustrating-some-employees-turn-to-rage-applying/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rage applying}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D to vent their frustrations and \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/coffee-badging-workplace-trend-where-173038103.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 coffee badging}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D to protest return-to-office policies.\par \par \uc2 US workers are \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/jackkelly/2022/08/04/how-many-of-your-co-workers-are-showing-signs-of-ghost-quitting/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ghost quitting}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D (checking out without actually leaving their jobs) and pretending to work when actually practicing \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.worklife.news/culture/wtf-is-productivity-theater/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 productivity theater}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. They\u8217\'20\'19re taking paid time off without authorization in the form of \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2023/06/20/hush-trips-heres-how-workers-are-hiding-them-from-their-employers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hush trips}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://money.usnews.com/money/blogs/outside-voices-careers/articles/what-is-quiet-vacationing-as-a-workplace-trend"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quiet vacations}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. They\u8217\'20\'19re suffering from \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afr.com/technology/zoom-doom-is-a-real-thing-20200603-p54z2w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Zoom doom}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and paying an \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/imperfect-spirituality/202306/how-emotional-labor-taxes-relationships"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emotional labor tax}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. According to the latest polls, things are so bad that they\u8217\'20\'19re \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hrdive.com/news/what-is-quiet-cracking-worker-trend/745384/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quietly cracking}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 They triggered the \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Resignation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 great resignation}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D but are now practicing the \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2024/12/23/why-the-great-resignation-became-the-great-stay-labor-economists.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 great stay}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. Meanwhile, employers are \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gallup.com/workplace/404996/quiet-firing-stop-doing.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quietly firing}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D some of them while \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.worklife.news/culture/quiet-promotions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quietly promoting}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D others by giving them more work with less recognition or just \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/bryanrobinson/2023/11/12/the-quiet-cutting-trend-is-a-controversial-leadership-strategy-new-study-shows/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quietly cutting}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D them, which is reassigning employees to less important roles. Don\u8217\'20\'19t complain. Just be quiet.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s next? \u8220\'20\'1CMute commuting\u8221\'20\'1D? \u8220\'20\'1CCalendar cluttering\u8221\'20\'1D? \u8220\'20\'1CDeadline doodling\u8221\'20\'1D?\par \par \uc2 One thing\u8217\'20\'19s for certain: these reports aren\u8217\'20\'19t going to end anytime soon. They\u8217\'20\'19re catchy and they get clicks. They\u8217\'20\'19re fun to talk about at parties and on late-night TV programs. We can all expect lots more pithy phrases and clever terms describing how unhappy our workforce is for the foreseeable future. But we should all take these reports with a grain of salt. Why?\par \par \uc2 For starters \u8211\'20\'13 and I won\u8217\'20\'19t name names but you can read the links above \u8211\'20\'13 most of these eye-catching, clickbait-y terms are defined by surveys conducted by HR firms, HR media sites, HR software providers and polling companies looking for media attention. They tend to survey hundreds of employees at a time out of a workforce of hundreds of millions using a questionable definition of \u8220\'20\'1Cstatistical sampling\u8221\'20\'1D. And they all have an agenda. Want your employees to be happier? Buy our software! Hire our firm! Read more of our research! When it comes to most of these reports, you\u8217\'20\'19ll find what I\u8217\'20\'19ve found: science is not a priority.\par \par \uc2 Next, it\u8217\'20\'19s easy to make employers the bad guys. Any survey that sticks it to the man is going to get eyeballs. The reality? People I know are mostly happy with their jobs and they understand the pressures their employers face. They\u8217\'20\'19re grown-ups who know that work is work and the perfect job doesn\u8217\'20\'19t exist.\par \par \uc2 Then again, isn\u8217\'20\'19t being dissatisfied with your job a good thing? It says that we want to make things better \u8211\'20\'13 we are driven by ambition. I\u8217\'20\'19m not judging people who are happy clocking in and clocking out of their jobs. Good for them. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not happy at your job, maybe that\u8217\'20\'19s because you want more out of your life, and that\u8217\'20\'19s normal. See if you can change the job you do, change how you work. Failing that, change your employer, maybe start your own business. But in the meantime, you still need to do your job.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250504el54001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
