\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Wall Street and European markets finish week on a high after US jobs report\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Partridge and Michael Sainato \par \uc2 483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2025\par 04:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 records its longest winning streak as Washington releases better-than-expected hiring figures\par \par \uc2 Markets on both sides of the Atlantic rose on Friday after hiring in the US slowed less than expected in April, offering a glimmer of hope that the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy was in a better-than-feared position to withstand the fallout from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tariffs.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street the S&P 500 was up 1.5% and the Dow Jones rose 1.3% by early afternoon on Friday, while European markets closed sharply higher after official figures showed the US workforce grew by 177,000 last month.\par \par \uc2 It was a slowdown compared with March \u8211\'20\'13 when 185,000 jobs were added \u8211\'20\'13 but was better than the 130,000 expected by economists. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/may/02/us-april-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US jobs market better than expected even as hiring slowed in April}}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 In the UK, the FTSE 100 closed 1.2% up at 8,596, marking its longest-ever winning streak and the 15th day in a row of gains. Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax rose 2.5% and France\u8217\'20\'19s Cac by 2.3%, building on earlier gains after reports that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/02/china-evaluating-us-offer-to-engage-in-trade-negotiations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Beijing was considering trade negotiations with Washington}}}\uc2 , raising hopes of easing tensions.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE has now recovered almost all of the losses from early last month, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s announcement of sweeping tariffs sent global markets plummeting over fears of a trade war.\par \par \uc2 Susannah Streeter, the head of money and markets at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe FTSE has surged higher, racing into a record winning streak, as fresh optimism pulses through markets.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said the jobs report \u8220\'20\'1Cadded to hopes that the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy is in a more resilient position to withstand the fallout from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs. Expectations for a further easing in the standoff between the US and China have been high with a feelgood factor dominating Friday trade\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As the White House pressed ahead with sweeping tariffs on overseas imports, claiming this would revitalise the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2 , employers across the country continued to add jobs at a steady pace in April and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 However, while April\u8217\'20\'19s hiring was stronger than predicted, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ) shaved 58,000 off its previous tallies for February and March\u8217\'20\'19s gains. April\u8217\'20\'19s largest hiring gains were in healthcare, and transportation and warehousing.\par \par \uc2 Federal government employment declined by 9,000 in April as the \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 -led \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D continued to cut government workers. Federal employment has fallen by 26,000 since January. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  noted that the number undercounted how many jobs had been lost as they did not include people on paid leave or those receiving severance payments.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250502el5200337\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
