\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Exploitative\u8217\'20\'19 contracts and hazardous conditions: life for some of the immigrants cleaning up wildfire-stricken LA\b0\par\par\uc2 Hilary Beaumont in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2025\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Advocates say workers risk their health and fear speaking out about conditions amid Trump\u8217\'20\'19s immigration crackdown\par \par \uc2 On a sunny day in February three workers swept up the piles of ash left behind on an Altadena driveway from when the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/california-wildfires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eaton fire }}}\uc2  raged through the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/los-angeles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Angeles }}}\uc2  neighborhood the month before.\par \par \uc2 The flames of the blaze had consumed nearly every home on the street, leaving only brick chimneys and charred vehicles. Red signs at the entrances of properties warned in English: \u8220\'20\'1CUnsafe, do not enter or occupy \u8230\'20\'26 entry may result in death or injury.\u8221\'20\'1D Hazards such as lead paint, asbestos and batteries were strewn amongst the ashes, but few workers cleaning the neighborhood that day wore masks or other personal protective equipment (PPE).\par \par \uc2 Across the street, in the ashes of a home that had burned to the ground, Pedro Ramos, wore a thin blue medical mask as he picked up charred branches and placed them in a wheelbarrow. \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth is that it\u8217\'20\'19s toxic,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramos, who is from Guatemala, said in Spanish about the debris. \u8220\'20\'1CBut it\u8217\'20\'19s necessary to work to pay the rent and bills for my family.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the US, after climate disasters such as fires, floods and hurricanes, it is often {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/29/trump-immigration-disaster-recovery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immigrant workers who clean up and rebuild communities }}}\uc2 . In Los Angeles, it\u8217\'20\'19s no different. In the burn zones of Altadena and the Palisades, many of the thousands of workers involved in clearing the debris from the megafires that killed 30 people and destroyed about 16,000 are from an immigrant background. Immigrants make up about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://eyeonhousing.org/2024/03/states-and-construction-trades-most-reliant-on-immigrant-workers-2022/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40% }}}\uc2  of the construction workforce in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California }}}\uc2 , including hazardous materials removal. Those workers are particularly vulnerable while working in potentially hazardous conditions, say advocates.\par \par \uc2 On several visits to the Altadena burn zone in past months, it was clear that some workers involved in the cleanup had received training and PPE, but many others had not. Advocates say workers are not only risking their health, but they fear speaking out about conditions over concerns of wage theft and potential deportation amid stepped up immigration enforcement by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 As climate disasters become more common, immigrant workers are serving as \u8220\'20\'1Csecond responders\u8221\'20\'1D, said Nadia Marin-Molina, co-director of the National Day Laborer Organizing Network (NDLON), which supports immigrant day laborers across the US. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s impossible for me to exaggerate how much disaster response and recovery in this country relies on immigrant labor in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/16/berkeley--california-gardens-wildfires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cut back your roses? Berkeley sets new rules for its most wildfire-prone areas }}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 Exploitative contractors\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles is under immense pressure to rebuild. LA was already experiencing a housing crisis before the fires, and rental and housing prices have skyrocketed since the disaster. The city is also in a hurry to recover before hosting a series of mega-events: the World Cup in 2026, the Super Bowl in 2027 and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2025/mar/20/los-angeles-olympics-2028"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Olympics in 2028 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Government officials are highlighting the speed of the cleanup. In February, the California governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  , and the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  (\uc2 EPA\uc2  ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.gov/newsreleases/trump-administration-finishes-phase-1-epas-largest-ever-wildfire-cleanup-record-28"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced }}}\uc2 EPA\uc2  workers had cleared more than 9,000 properties in just 28 days \u8211\'20\'13 the largest wildfire waste cleanup in the agency\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 Cleanup efforts following wildfires take place in different phases. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  is in charge of the first phase, in which dangerous materials like paint, cleaners and solvents, oils, batteries, pesticides, propane tanks and asbestos are removed. In the second phase, property owners have the choice to either allow the army corps of engineers to finish clearing their properties, or hire private contractors. As of 24 April, more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tt-emi.maps.arcgis.com/apps/dashboards/874a690d69694d2685d83d07a320e6dd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1,700 property owners }}}\uc2  of about 12,000 eligible properties had opted out of the federal offer.\par \par \uc2 For homes left standing in burn zones, property owners hire private contractors to clean smoke damage and replace insulation in homes that survived the fires. For all types of work in the burn zones, employers are required to provide PPE and training. That\u8217\'20\'19s because the debris can be highly toxic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a really wide variety of materials [in the burn zones],\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachael Jones, an exposure scientist and professor in the UCLA Fielding School of Public Health\u8217\'20\'19s environmental health sciences department. She said workers may be exposed to carcinogens like Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs), formaldehyde, benzene, asbestos and harmful metals such as lead, arsenic, cadmium and chromium.\par \par \uc2 Soil testing has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://publichealth.lacounty.gov/media/wildfire/docs/Roux_LA_Fires_Soil_Prelim_Results.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found }}}\uc2  dangerous levels of lead in and around the Eaton fire burn zone, likely from lead-based paint in the older housing stock in Altadena, according to the Los Angeles department of public health.\par \par \uc2 Soot and ash {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.publichealth.lacounty.gov/media/wildfire/faq.htm#faq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contain }}}\uc2  fine particles and toxic substances that can cause skin and lung irritation, shortness of breath, and exposure to carcinogens.\par \par \uc2 Without proper protection, workers risk long term breathing issues, lung scarring and cancer, said Jones. Their risk level depends on how long they are working in the burn zones, and what type of PPE they are wearing.\par \par \uc2 There is a spectrum of employers at the fire sites, and conditions for workers vary. The federal government has hired large contractors to carry out debris removal; those workers are properly trained and wear Tyvek suits and industrial-grade respirators.\par \par \uc2 But there is also a secondary industry that is bypassing the formal process; contractors are picking up immigrant day laborers from the corner near \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and taking them to burn zones. They are less likely to receive training and PPE, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re the ones that I\u8217\'20\'19m most worried about, because it\u8217\'20\'19s just completely uncontrolled. They\u8217\'20\'19re really at the mercy of the people who bring them to the site.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[Workers] don\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily have a formal employment arrangement, and that creates a lot of gray area about whose responsibility it is to ensure health and safety.\u8221\'20\'1D Jones said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou end up with exploitative contractors.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nancy Zuniga, the director of the Workers Health Program at the Institute of Popular Education of Southern California, said workers at the LA burn sites told her their employers have not provided training or PPE. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/10/la-wildfire-victims-trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 After losing homes and businesses, LA wildfire victims face a hurdle to rebuilding: Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs }}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 One worker said she was taken with a group of people to a burn zone, Zuniga recalled. They weren\u8217\'20\'19t given PPE and were asked to pick up debris and put it in trash bags. After she finished the job, her eyes became irritated and red from exposure to the debris. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd then she didn\u8217\'20\'19t get paid,\u8221\'20\'1D Zuniga said.\par \par \uc2 Zuniga led a study on the experiences of hundreds of workers who were affected by the Woolsey fire, which raged in northern Los Angeles county in 2018. The study found workers developed rashes and breathing problems, faced wage theft, and felt pressure to stay on the job. What happened to workers during the Woolsey fire is now repeating at a larger scale, Zuniga said.\par \par \uc2 Lack of enforcement\par \par \uc2 Cal-Osha is the state agency responsible for regulating worker safety, but Jones, the exposure scientist at UCLA, said she was puzzled by the agency\u8217\'20\'19s apparent lack of enforcement at the burn sites.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese sites aren\u8217\'20\'19t that big,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not like you can\u8217\'20\'19t drive through them in an afternoon and stop and look at what\u8217\'20\'19s happening. Many of us are wondering why there hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been a more aggressive approach to this?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 California\u8217\'20\'19s department of industrial relations, which includes Cal-Osha, said it has participated in hundreds of outreach events and deployed teams to fire-impacted areas to reach homeowners and small-business contractors. The department said it is supporting worker rights and safety by providing $6m in funding to 21 community groups in Los Angeles through the California Workplace Outreach Project.\par \par \uc2 Employers are required to conduct worksite risk assessments, implement necessary safety measures, train workers to prevent exposure to hazards, and provide PPE, department spokesperson Denisse Gomez said. Employers engaged in fire debris removal must comply with title 8 of the California code of regulations, which outlines legal standards for workplace safety.\par \par \uc2 If a homeowner declines the offer of a no-cost, government-led cleanup, the homeowner or contractor is accountable for any issues that arise, such as permitting, environmental protections and worker safety, Gomez added. <aside class="element element-rich-link element--thumbnail">\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/15/budget-los-angeles-california-county"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 LA county facing financial turmoil after wildfires and $4bn abuse settlement }}}\uc2  </aside>\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Department of Industrial Relations (DIR) helps protect workers on the frontlines of disaster recovery \u8211\'20\'13 regardless of immigration status,\u8221\'20\'1D Gomez wrote in an email.\par \par \uc2 Some workers have received Osha training. On 13 March in Altadena, four men in Tyvek suits, goggles, masks and gloves cleaned a smoke-damaged apartment. The building was surrounded by the blackened debris of homes that had burned to the ground. But the workers knew how to protect themselves; they had received PPE and Osha training through the Pasadena Community Job Center.\par \par \uc2 Taking a break from cleaning, Cesar Saucedo said he knew workers who were picked up from a corner near \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and taken to a burn zone but provided no training or PPE. \u8220\'20\'1COn the weekend, we went to a corner, where a group of workers were hired by a company for $17 an hour. They had no Osha training and the company gave them nothing,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in Spanish.\par \par \uc2 Marin-Molina, of NDLON, said that in the rush to recover, few employers take the time to train workers on the hazards they will encounter when cleaning up after climate disasters.\par \par \uc2 NDLON {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ndlon.org/report-immigrant-day-laborers-as-second-responders-in-climate-disasters/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surveyed }}}\uc2  workers who cleaned up after Hurricane Ida hit New Orleans in 2021 and found that only 15% of day laborers working in hurricane-affected areas in New Orleans received required training. More than 90% reported wage theft while doing post-disaster recovery work. One study found that one in four workers who cleaned up after Hurricane Katrina were undocumented.\par \par \uc2 Marin-Molina explained that often it\u8217\'20\'19s large national companies that hire day laborers through layers of subcontractors. The climate crisis has become lucrative for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://newyorker.com/magazine/2021/11/08/the-migrant-workers-who-follow-climate-disasters?utm_source=pocket_shared"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 large disaster cleanup companies }}}\uc2 , who shield themselves from accountability by using subcontractors. \u8220\'20\'1CUnder those layers, it\u8217\'20\'19s the undocumented workforce that\u8217\'20\'19s holding this all up,\u8221\'20\'1D Marin-Molina said.\par \par \uc2 Jones said the vulnerability of immigrant workers and lack of enforcement is nothing new. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a longstanding uphill battle, and I think the fires are just making it more visible,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250428el4s00234\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
