\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Doge cuts spark questions as employees supporting Musk space launches spared\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Perkins \par \uc2 980 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2025\par 11:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Department of Transportation employees who provide support for Starlink and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  launches safe amid job cuts\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge) and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  have spared the jobs of \uc2 US Department of Transportation\uc2  employees who provide support services for spacecraft launches by Musk\u8217\'20\'19s companies, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/science/spacex"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SpaceX}}}\uc2  and Starlink \u8211\'20\'13 a revelation that raises a new round of conflict of interest questions around Doge.\par \par \uc2 In its most recent buyout announcement, the transportation department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2025/04/01/transportation-dept-buyout/82756419007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 did not note}}}\uc2  that the positions spared supported Musk\u8217\'20\'19s and others\u8217\'20\'19 space operations.\par \par \uc2 But the fiscal year 2025 transportation department budget reviewed by the Guardian details funding for positions in pipeline management, transportation management, air traffic control and cybersecurity that the document states are critical for commercial space operations, including \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , Starlink and other entities.\par \par \uc2 The decision to keep launch support staff employed while broadly cutting potentially thousands of other positions at the agency has raised fresh ethical questions about Musk and Doge\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive assault on the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 While the administration and Doge have targeted hundreds of thousands of federal employees, critics say the decision shows Musk is willing to allow federal workers to remain employed if their work benefits him.\par \par \uc2 Keeping these employees on the payroll is \u8220\'20\'1Cshowing Musk\u8217\'20\'19s bias because cutting these people would create so many hindrances to him launching\u8221\'20\'1D, said a former congressional staffer and current space industry employee who requested anonymity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really weird those people were spared. If I had to read between the lines, I would say this is on purpose because Musk knows these positions support the space industry.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The conflict of interest controversy is the latest in a series for Musk. In his quest to slash the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s workforce of 2.3m people by 75%, he has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/elon-musk/elon-musk-starlink-growing-footprint-federal-government-rcna195400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allegedly steered}}}\uc2  lucrative contracts to his companies, or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/mar/25/doge-musk-spacex-starlink-contracts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hobbled}}}\uc2  agencies or departments that he could potentially take over and profit from.\par \par \uc2 In other cases, he has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2025/02/12/nx-s1-5293382/x-elon-musk-doge-cfpb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attempted to kill}}}\uc2  departments or agencies that regulate his businesses, like the Consumer Finance Protection Bureau.\par \par \uc2 The latest iteration of a Doge conflict of interest is \u8220\'20\'1Ccompletely unsurprising\u8221\'20\'1D, said Lisa Gilbert, co-president of \uc2 Public Citizen\uc2 , a government transparency advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCertainly giving someone who\u8217\'20\'19s that interconnected in government a huge amount of carte blanche power to change the way government is structured is problematic, damaging, bad for American taxpayers, and beneficial to Musk\u8217\'20\'19s bottom line,\u8221\'20\'1D Gilbert added.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the transportation department did not respond to a comment request.\par \par \uc2 The transportation department has a workforce of about 57,000, and about 800 probationary employees were reportedly fired in March. On 1 April, the administration sent buyout letters urging more to quit in exchange for pay and benefits through 30 September. It is unclear how many were asked to leave, and \u8220\'20\'1Cinvoluntary layoffs\u8221\'20\'1D are expected for many who stay.\par \par \uc2 Among the fiscal year 2025 transportation department budget line items that show support for \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and other companies were $21m and 38 full-time positions for the office of commercial space transportation. That in part includes air traffic control in the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cto manage increased airspace complexities associated with enhanced space operations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and Starlink {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launch multiple spacecraft weekly}}}\uc2 , and the air traffic controllers ensure that commercial aircraft and spacecraft don\u8217\'20\'19t collide.\par \par \uc2 The former congressional staffer noted that many previous \uc2 FAA\uc2  budget increase requests were made because of pressure to keep up with the higher number of \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  launches \u8211\'20\'13 about 80% of agency overtime has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-09-11/spacex-accounts-for-80-of-faa-s-space-workers-overtime?utm_source=website&utm_medium=share&utm_campaign=copy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attributed to}}}\uc2  supporting \uc2 SpaceX.\par \par \uc2 The budget items detailed don\u8217\'20\'19t just support Musk or other private space companies \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Nasa\uc2  and the military are served by them, as well. However, Musk has called for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2025/mar/18/nasa-cuts-elon-musk-spacex"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cuts to space programs at Nasa}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/04/doge-noaa-headquarters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration}}}\uc2  that don\u8217\'20\'19t serve his needs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is true that we need air traffic controllers, and it is true that we have a shortage of them, but they are also critical for space launches,\u8221\'20\'1D the former congressional staffer and space industry employee said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth is hiding under the truth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the budget shows $400m to fund the \uc2 US Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration\uc2 , which manages the movement of fuel and other supplies for launching.\par \par \uc2 It supports the administration\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgrowing oversight responsibilities to ensure the safe transport of hazardous materials, including a significant increase in the market for transporting energy products, high value equipment that contains hazardous materials such as spacecraft, and other items that are essential to the US economy\u8221\'20\'1D, the document states.\par \par \uc2 The budget continues to say the department will \u8220\'20\'1Cset safety standards and continue to perform safety inspections \u8230\'20\'26 for the transport of space equipment and products via truck and air\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The former congressional staffer and space industry employee said the department may shut down highways when spacecraft or highly flammable material is being transported.\par \par \uc2 An $81m line item within the \uc2 PHMSA\uc2  budget also notes funding for adding staff to support the growing space industry.\par \par \uc2 The new staff would \u8220\'20\'1Cmanage a growing special permits and approvals workload including the transportation of high value hazardous materials containing products bound for outer space, and a rapidly growing outer space economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The DOT also stressed that it is sparing those employees who work on cybersecurity. Though this is a broad category that applies to all facets of the agency, Musk\u8217\'20\'19s systems used by the \uc2 US government\uc2  are protected by this.\par \par \uc2 Gilbert said \uc2 Public Citizen\uc2  is supporting legislation introduced by Senator Elizabeth Warren that would strengthen rules around conflicts of interest for people like Musk who are classified by an administration as a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial government employee\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a challenging moment and not what anyone wants to see \u8211\'20\'13 this individual amassing power without the checks put upon people who serve in government,\u8221\'20\'1D Gilbert said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250418el4i001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
