\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US labor groups sue over \u8216\'20\'18ignorant\u8217\'20\'19 cuts of programs fighting child labor abroad\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,069 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2025\par 18:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Musk\u8217\'20\'19s Doge team announced in March it had canceled about $577m in grants for programs it labeled \u8216\'20\'18America last\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Labor groups have filed a lawsuit challenging the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s abrupt termination of international labor rights programs aimed at ending child labor and other abuses.\par \par \uc2 The Solidarity Center, Global March Against Child Labour, and the \uc2 American Institutes for Research\uc2  (AIR), filed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.solidaritycenter.org/stopping-the-race-to-the-bottom/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawsuit }}}\uc2 on Tuesday seeking to stop the cuts, enacted by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge), and arguing the programs were authorized by Congress and that the secretary of labor has no authority to cancel the funds.\par \par \uc2 Several groups supporting workers and corporations have criticized the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s decision to abruptly cancel all ongoing grants and contracts for programs with the \uc2 Bureau of International Labor Affairs\uc2  (ILAB) at the Department of Labor which works to improve labor conditions outside the US.\par \par \uc2 In March Doge announced it had canceled about $577m in grants for programs it labeled \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica last\u8221\'20\'1D. Doge cited programs including \u8220\'20\'1Cworker empowerment in South America\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Cimproving respect for Worker\u8217\'20\'19s rights in agricultural supply chains\u8221\'20\'1D in Honduras, Guatemala and El Salvador and \u8220\'20\'1Cassisting foreign migrant workers\u8221\'20\'1D in Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/press/releases/afl-cio-president-responds-doge-cuts-department-labor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AFL-CIO}}}\uc2 , the largest federation of labor unions in the US, and the{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aafaglobal.org/AAFA/AAFA_News/2025_Press_Releases/AAFA_Reacts_DOL_Action_Cancel_All_ILAB_Contracts.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  American Apparel & Footwear Association (AAFA)}}}\uc2 , whose members include corporations such as Adidas and Ralph Lauren, criticized the cuts and argued the decision \u8220\'20\'1Cputs American workers and American businesses last\u8221\'20\'1D by enabling the degradation of labor and business standards abroad.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just so frustrating to read the shallow and useless justifications that are being put out on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  by the secretary of labor and the Doge crowd,\u8221\'20\'1D said Thea Lee, who served as deputy undersecretary for international labor affairs at the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  from 2021 to 2025. \u8220\'20\'1CThe abrupt termination of all of ILAB grants is a destruction of decades of consensus that is bipartisan, that is business and labor agreeing together that these are important things. It\u8217\'20\'19s ignorant. It\u8217\'20\'19s self-defeating, and it\u8217\'20\'19s wasteful and inefficient.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lee explained the cuts demonstrate ignorance of how the global economy works, of the long-term sourcing and investment decisions made by corporations, and the negative impacts on American workers, businesses and consumers in competing and relying on supply chains where forced labor, child labor and other human rights abuses are ignored.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was a completely indiscriminate meat ax that was taken to these projects and workers will suffer, businesses will suffer and American workers will suffer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lee.\par \par \uc2 Lee cited programs and research required to enforce trade agreements between the US and other nations, such as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ilo.org/resource/other/national-child-labour-survey-ncls-reporting-templates#:~:text=The%20National%20Child%20Labour%20Survey%20(NCLS)%20reporting%20templates%20provide%20a,interventions%20to%20combat%20child%20labour."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Child Labor survey}}}\uc2 , and around enforcing the bipartisan Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act, which was co-sponsored by the secretary of state, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , when he was a senator as examples of programs where the work already completed is likely to be wasted due to the cuts.\par \par \uc2 Shawna Bader-Blau, the executive director of the Solidarity Center, a non-profit working in more than 90 countries to improve worker standards and conditions, said the cuts reduced the organization\u8217\'20\'19s budget by 20%, in addition to 30% cuts through \uc2 USAID\uc2  cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a devastating, huge impact. The Solidarity Center is very often in countries where they are the only external support for trade union organizing and the advancement of worker rights. If we have to leave, we\u8217\'20\'19re not replaced,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bader-Blau. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s critical to the American economy that American workers not be forced to compete with extremely exploited workers in other countries, up to and including forced and child labor in supply chains.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She cited programs involved in enforcing labor aspects of the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA), signed under Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first term, to improve labor conditions in Mexico. The programs directly affect US workers whose jobs have often been outsourced to Mexico by corporations to exploit cheaper labor.\par \par \uc2 The cuts, noted Bader-Blau, made it more likely that workers will be affected by offshoring and consumers will be purchasing goods where labor abuses are rampant in the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 The cuts follow cancellations of previous grants, including a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/SecretaryLCD/status/1903136157493244078"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 program}}}\uc2  that began in 2022 and was slated to continue until 2026 to provide support for Uzbekistan, the sixth largest producer of cotton in the world, after the country banned forced labor and child labor in its cotton production industry.\par \par \uc2 The program was created to affirm and support the ban on forced labor and child labor so American corporations that had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/ilo-welcomes-lifting-cotton-campaign-boycott-uzbekistan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 boycotted}}}\uc2  cotton from the region could begin sourcing from the country.\par \par \uc2 The program\u8217\'20\'19s cancellation was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/SecretaryLCD/status/1903136157493244078"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 touted}}}\uc2  by the US labor secretary, Lori Chavez-DeRemer, last month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CState-imposed forced labor was used in the cotton harvest for decades,\u8221\'20\'1D said Raluca Dumitrescu, coordinator for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.uzbekforum.org/uzbekistan-increasing-farmers-autonomy-critical-to-address-forced-labor-risks-and-attract-responsible-sourcing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cotton Campaign.}}}\par \par \uc2 Umida Niyazova, director of the Uzbek Forum for Human Rights, explained Uzbekistan had moved in recent years from producing cotton and exporting the entire crop, to developing a textile industry to process it. Though the country has eliminated forced labor and child labor in harvesting, problems and abuses are still rampant throughout the industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince 2021, under enormous pressure, the state has changed the coercive practice of mass mobilization for cotton harvesting, however, the risks of forced labor remain high since structural reforms have not been implemented,\u8221\'20\'1D said Niyazova.\par \par \uc2 Niyazova said the country still needed programs to establish decent labor standards and enforce them, such as the cancelled ILAB program.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs Uzbek textile products are aimed at the foreign market, this concerns other countries and people of goodwill who would not want to become potential participants in a production chain based on worker exploitation,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  did not provide information on how the funds will be reallocated, and did not comment on criticisms of the cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe American people resoundingly elected President Trump with a clear mandate to reduce federal government bloat and root out waste. Americans don\u8217\'20\'19t want their hard-earned tax dollars bankrolling foreign handouts that put America last,\u8221\'20\'1D said Courtney Parella, \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  spokesperson, in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why we\u8217\'20\'19re focused on improving oversight and accountability within this program \u8211\'20\'13 and across the entire department \u8211\'20\'13 while prioritizing investments in the American workforce.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250429el4f0031x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
