\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US firm in \u163\'00\'A3263m takeover bid for \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  banknote printer\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Partridge \par \uc2 637 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2025\par 13:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Board has recommended all-cash deal with \uc2 Atlas Holdings\uc2 , but Edi Truell may be considering a higher offer\par \par \uc2 A bidding war could be heating up for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/delaruebusiness"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 De La Rue}}}\uc2 , the 200-year-old British firm that prints banknotes for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , after its board recommended an all-cash offer from the US buyout firm \uc2 Atlas Holdings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 De La Rue\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s shares climbed by as much as 16% on Tuesday morning after the company released a statement to investors saying that its board had recommended shareholders accept \uc2 Atlas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s offer of 130p a share, which valued the company at \u163\'00\'A3263m.\par \par \uc2 The all-cash takeover bid was at a 16% premium to \uc2 De La Rue\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s closing price on Monday, which the company board described as \u8220\'20\'1Cfair and reasonable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The City financier Edi Truell, however, is reportedly considering a higher offer of 132.17p a share, which could trigger a bidding war.\par \par \uc2 Investment funds controlled by Truell, a pensions and private equity entrepreneur, have made the proposal to De La Rue, as first reported by Sky News.\par \par \uc2 The company said it had not received a firm offer from Truell, and released a further statement later on Tuesday reaffirming its recommendation of the \uc2 Atlas\uc2  offer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would take something very significant for the board to make any other decision right now, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t see that happening,\u8221\'20\'1D said De La Rue\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 Clive Vacher\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Atlas\uc2  offer would give \uc2 De La Rue\uc2  the benefit of backing from a \u8220\'20\'1Cfinancially strong company\u8221\'20\'1D, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are expecting continuity of strategy, continuity of management, continuity of employees, continuity of location and continuity of customers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The sale to \uc2 Atlas\uc2 , which would mean \uc2 De La Rue\uc2  delisting from the \uc2 London Stock Exchange\uc2 , would be subject to shareholder approval and regulatory clearance. Vacher said he hoped the deal could be completed by early summer.\par \par \uc2 The company, which is headquartered in Basingstoke, first confirmed in January {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/09/shares-in-banknote-printer-de-la-rue-soar-after-it-confirms-takeover-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 that it was in takeover talks}}}\uc2  with Disruptive Capital GP and Pension SuperFund Capital (PSFC), founded by Truell.\par \par \uc2 The deal with PSFC was conditional on the completion of \uc2 De La Rue\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A3300m sale of its authentication division to \uc2 Crane NXT\uc2 , which was announced in October 2024. The company expects that sale to complete on 1 May.\par \par \uc2 The long-running takeover process took another turn in February, when \uc2 De La Rue\uc2  launched a formal sale process after it said it had received \u8220\'20\'1Cpreliminary approaches\u8221\'20\'1D from more than one potential bidder.\par \par \uc2 The company has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/apr/12/de-la-rue-banknotes-paper-money-printing-profit-warning"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced a string of challenges}}}\uc2 , including a widespread fall in the use of cash, which accelerated during the pandemic, and it has issued a number of profit warnings in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The company stopped production of banknotes and UK passports at its plant in Gateshead in 2020 after it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2019/may/30/de-la-rue-boss-to-step-down-after-losing-uk-passport-print-contract"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost out on the contract to print post-Brexit blue passports}}}\uc2  to the Franco-Dutch company \uc2 Gemalto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the completion of the sale of its authentication business, \uc2 De La Rue\uc2  will employ about 1,250 people, of whom just over half are based in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Atlas\uc2  has said that as part of its offer it would cut about 50 jobs at the company, or 4% of the workforce, mainly in positions related to being a listed business.\par \par \uc2 The company can trace its history back to 1813, when Thomas de la Rue founded it as a printing business in Guernsey, before setting up in London a decade later selling straw hats and stationery.\par \par \uc2 It has a contract with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/bankofenglandgovernor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bank of England}}}\uc2  to run the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s printing facility in Debden, Essex, and was involved in printing millions of new banknotes featuring the image of King Charles before his coronation.\par \par \uc2 Truell did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250415el4f001s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
