\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ditches gender and ethnicity targets in US\b0\par\par\uc2 Kalyeena Makortoff Banking correspondent \par \uc2 615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2025\par 16:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 34\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bank says it still values inclusivity after bowing to Trump agenda by changing hiring and promotion policy\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  has ditched gender and ethnicity targets for US staff, making it the latest UK company to bow to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s anti-diversity drive.\par \par \uc2 Managers at the US arm of the British lender will no long have to consider how new hires and promotions advance the careers of women and people from minority ethnic backgrounds, who have traditionally been overlooked in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 The bank\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, CS Venkatakrishnan, made the decision after a review, which started late last year, into its approach to diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI).\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Barclays\uc2  spokesperson said that the review was \u8220\'20\'1Cstill under way\u8221\'20\'1D and that \u8220\'20\'1Cin the meantime, we have taken the decision to no longer have specific US representation ambitions. Our representation ambitions for the rest of the group will continue, and are being considered as part of the review.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe remain committed to a culture which values inclusiveness and equality of opportunity across \uc2 Barclays\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson clarified that the review, run by its head of DEI, Natalie Hattrell, began before Trump won the presidential election in November.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Barclays\uc2  had not set US-specific targets, its 11,600 US staff, including those in offices in New York, Nevada, New Jersey and Delaware, were expected to adhere to global targets. Those included the goal of 33% of director and managing director roles being held by women by the end of 2025. Staff from under-represented ethnicities were also due to make up 50% of those senior positions.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  is maintaining targets for the rest of its roughly 91,260 global staff, including the 43,180 it employs in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Venkatakrishnan said in the memo, first reported by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CThe group executive committee has been carefully considering the changed environment in the US. As a global organisation, we have always complied with the local requirements in the jurisdictions in which we have operated. In doing so, we have remained faithful to our workplace culture.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The US rollback comes two months after Venkatakrishnan, who was born in India, told journalists that \uc2 Barclays\uc2  had an \u8220\'20\'1Cenduring commitment to a culture of inclusivity in the bank. It\u8217\'20\'19s enduring and unwavering\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you want to get the very best people, by necessity, you get a very diverse workforce,\u8221\'20\'1D he said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/13/barclays-profit-rise-ceo-cs-venkatakrishnan-more-pay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 during a full-year earnings call in February}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to provide equality of opportunity, and we want to create that inclusive environment in which people really can shine, excel and contribute to this bank. So that\u8217\'20\'19s our approach, and we will continue to run the business and run the company in that way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump used {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/26/trump-executive-orders-dei"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive orders after returning to the White House in January}}}\uc2  to reverse DEI policies in the federal government. While the orders did not directly apply to the private sector, he called on companies to follow suit, and asked the US attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office to consider how the rules could be more widely enforced.\par \par \uc2 It started a wide-ranging reversal, with several large US corporations, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/15/walmart-dei-diversity-equity-inclusion-rollback"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Walmart}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/06/mcdonalds-diversity-programs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2 , Ford and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jan/10/amazon-ending-dei-programs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2 , announcing plans to scrap or scale back their DEI schemes.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  is the latest UK company to drop some of its DEI targets, with others, such as the pharmaceutical company \uc2 GSK\uc2 , going as far as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/28/uk-based-british-pharma-gsk-pauses-diversity-work-citing-trump-orders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pausing diversity activities for UK workers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last month City regulators at the Bank of England and Financial Conduct Authority controversially announced they were not planning to introduce new diversity and inclusion rules for financial companies and would instead support \u8220\'20\'1Cvoluntary industry initiatives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250408el480028o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
