\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US workers feel effects of Trump cuts: \u8216\'20\'18I am seeing my work dry up\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2025\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President\u8217\'20\'19s effort to rapidly shrink federal government is already reaching private sector as recession fears loom\par \par \uc2 Americans are grappling with climbing costs, falling sales and dwindling work as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  moves to overhaul the federal government and economy.\par \par \uc2 As the US president pushes forward with an array of controversial policies, from sweeping cuts to blanket tariffs, the Guardian asked US workers how they have been affected. Some requested anonymity for fear of retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Knock-on effects from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to rapidly shrink the federal government by firing workers, slashing funding and cancelling grants are already reaching the private sector, with workers reporting layoffs, price hikes and supply issues.\par \par \uc2 Consumer confidence in the US has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/25/trump-tariffs-us-consumer-confidence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped }}}\uc2  in March to the lowest level in four years. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanprogress.org/article/thousands-of-workers-in-each-congressional-district-could-lose-their-jobs-to-doge/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 At least 60,000 federal civil servants}}}\uc2  have been fired in recent months, in addition to about 25,000 probationary employees who have been ordered by courts to be reinstated.\par \par \uc2 The federal layoffs have also created challenges in the private sector, with contractors and businesses reliant on federal workers or agencies as customers hit hard.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Revard of Seattle, Washington, worked at a non-profit which provided support and referrals for mental health, substance abuse and problem gambling, he said \u8211\'20\'13 until his position was eliminated due to grant withdrawals from the state health department in response to federal cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe work with mostly individuals who are on the state Medicaid program,\u8221\'20\'1D said Revard, 67. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are disenfranchised individuals for a large part, who mean nothing to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another worker, at a copper and brass manufacturing facility in Michigan, said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive tariff strategy had already caused layoffs and price hikes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have had to raise our prices in order to stay profitable,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve had a round of layoffs and firings, and we\u8217\'20\'19re expecting more as the economy worsens.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Leo Robertson, a metal worker in New Orleans, Louisiana, said many of his suppliers had already increased prices due to uncertainty caused by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariffs, forcing him to raise prices, too. He has already lost international customers, he said, including a repeat customer in Canada.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe increase in steel and aluminum tariffs has caused my tools and replacement parts to get even more expensive than they already were,\u8221\'20\'1D Robertson said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy domestic sales have dropped noticeably also, which is understandable, as I\u8217\'20\'19ve had to almost double my prices to keep up with the unstable metals market.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI lost one particularly large commission for some architectural copper panels because the client could no longer afford the materials cost.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A worker at a defense contractor in Alabama, meanwhile, said a number of colleagues had opted for early retirement due to the federal funding cuts. \u8220\'20\'1CThat leaves a backlog of responsibility,\u8221\'20\'1D they said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut with the hiring freeze it just creates more work for the already overworked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Amy Wachspress, 70, a semi-retired grant writer based in Portland, Oregon, said their work had dried up through a contractor in Washington DC due to federal funding and grant cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have continued to work to make enough money to supplement our income and meet our basic living costs,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe do nothing extravagant. No travel. Rarely eat out. Modest living. But I am seeing my work dry up. I need to make about $15,000 a year to cover our expenses that go beyond what we can pay using my husband\u8217\'20\'19s pension and our social security.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wachspress and her husband are particularly concerned about cuts to social security benefits. Without them, she said, they would lose their house. Her husband relies on Medicare for treating diabetes and several chronic health conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we lose Medicare, he will die. So will many, many other people,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CI am personally more sad than anxious. I am grieving for all that we have lost and all that we stand to lose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250408el48001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
