\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They\u8217\'20\'19re killing you\u8217\'20\'19: US poultry workers fear faster lines will lead to more injury\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,201 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2025\par 10:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers say fast-paced conditions compound injury risks, while \uc2 USDA\uc2  will no longer require reports on safety data\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration will speed up processing lines for poultry and pork meatpacking plants while halting reports on worker safety, the \uc2 US Department of Agriculture (USDA)\uc2  announced recently, in a move that workers and advocates say will lead to more injuries.\par \par \uc2 Some poultry and pork plants already receive waivers to speed up production lines, and the \uc2 USDA\uc2  plans to update its rules to make the changes permanent and applicable to all poultry and pork plants, the department said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usda.gov/about-usda/news/press-releases/2025/03/17/secretary-rollins-takes-action-streamline-us-pork-and-poultry-processing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 press release}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the \uc2 USDA\uc2  will no longer require reports on worker safety data, calling the information \u8220\'20\'1Credundant\u8221\'20\'1D and pointing to research that the agency says \u8220\'20\'1Cconfirmed no direct link between processing speeds and workplace injuries\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Four people working at different poultry processing plants described to the Guardian fast-paced working conditions that compound the risks for injury. They asked that their names and locations be withheld for fear the Trump administration would revoke their visas.\par \par \uc2 One young man had only worked at the chicken processing plant for two weeks, and he was still scrambling to learn the job and keep up with his expected workload.\par \par \uc2 After sustaining one workplace injury, he said, he kept working \u8211\'20\'13 until he fell from a 13ft ladder and broke his back.\par \par \uc2 He hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been able to return to work as the fracture slowly heals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI could\u8217\'20\'19ve been paralyzed for the rest of my life,\u8221\'20\'1D the man said. Now, he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cliving with remorse and regret\u8221\'20\'1D, he said, unable to work or pay bills on his own.\par \par \uc2 A January {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fsis.usda.gov/sites/default/files/media_file/documents/PULSE_PoultryStudy_250109_Final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 USDA\uc2  found that faster line speeds were not the leading reason for injuries \u8211\'20\'13 but a higher \u8220\'20\'1Cpiece rate\u8221\'20\'1D, or a different way of measuring speed, did correlate with injuries.\par \par \uc2 The report cautioned that the injury rate among poultry workers was already high at speeds of both 140 and 175 birds per minute, with 81% of workers at high risk for musculoskeletal disorders \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cindicating that current risk mitigation efforts are insufficient\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The majority (70%) of workers first experienced \u8220\'20\'1Cmoderate to severe work-related pain\u8221\'20\'1D within their first three months on the job, the report said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s injuries occurring on a regular basis, and it\u8217\'20\'19s most definitely associated with the speeds that people are moving,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Payan, director of operations at the Sussex Health and Environmental Network (Shen), an organization based in Delaware and Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Maria Payan, executive director of Shen, noted they were \u8220\'20\'1Cputting more through input \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s more injury\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy, at the same time you\u8217\'20\'19re increasing line speeds, would you eliminate collecting worker safety data?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s going to affect the workers, then why would they stop collecting the data?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One woman worked in poultry processing for 11 years before being fired after getting sick with Covid, she said. She would chop chicken carcasses hanging from a hook \u8211\'20\'13 the same motion, over and over again.\par \par \uc2 Her hands and shoulders still swell regularly, and her hands cramp every night, despite not working the line for five years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re killing you,\u8221\'20\'1D she said of the fast-paced work demands.\par \par \uc2 Under the new rules, workers may process up to 175 birds a minute, a rise from the maximum speed of 140 before 2020. But unlike in 2020, when meatpacking workers were devastated by high rates of illness and death from Covid, there are no shortages of meat.\par \par \uc2 The move will \u8220\'20\'1Creduce burdens on the US pork and poultry industries \u8230\'20\'26 ensuring they can meet demand without excessive government interference\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 USDA\uc2  said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usda.gov/about-usda/news/press-releases/2025/03/17/secretary-rollins-takes-action-streamline-us-pork-and-poultry-processing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There are about 250,000 poultry workers in the US, and in some states, agricultural workers are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.farmworkerjustice.org/overtime-map/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exempt}}}\uc2  from federal labor laws.\par \par \uc2 About 78% of poultry processors surveyed in Alabama said faster line speeds made their work more dangerous, according to a 2013 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/resources/reports/unsafe-these-speeds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Poultry workers suffer five times as many occupational illness cases compared with the average worker in the US. Their rates of carpal tunnel syndrome are seven times higher and repetitive strain injuries are 10 times higher than average workers.\par \par \uc2 Workers also experience allergic rhinitis, or chronic cold-like symptoms, from the cold temperatures and exposure to chemicals. Peracetic acid, a substance used to battle pathogens like salmonella and E coli, was found in the air at rates that exceeded regulatory limits at one in five jobs in all locations, according to the January \uc2 USDA\uc2  report.\par \par \uc2 A 2015 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oxfamamerica.org/livesontheline/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from \uc2 Oxfam\uc2  pointed to increasing line speeds as one of the reasons for injuries.\par \par \uc2 Reported injuries are probably lower than the actual rate, because many poultry processors offer care through on-site medical clinics, which means they may not need to refer workers to outside medical practitioners, the \uc2 Oxfam\uc2  report noted: \u8220\'20\'1CIf companies can avoid doing more than this, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have to record the incident, or report to the \uc2 US government\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Occupational Health and Safety Administration (\uc2 Osha\uc2 ).\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One man worked on the processing line for 15 years. He spent \u8220\'20\'1C15 years doing the same thing five days a week, eight to 10 hours a day\u8221\'20\'1D, he said. He developed pain after eight years, but he kept working.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, he had to undergo surgery for his repetitive stress injuries. He was fired while recovering, he said, with no benefits or severance. He still suffers from back pain, and his family now supports him financially.\par \par \uc2 Recent arrivals are frequently in the lowest of the \u8220\'20\'1Cpecking order\u8221\'20\'1D, as Payan calls it, \u8220\'20\'1Cwhich means, basically, they\u8217\'20\'19re put in the lines where you would do the repeated cuts consistently\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A lack of training and persistent language barriers also contribute to the high rate of injuries, as workers are pushed to move fast as soon as they begin work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a lot of workers who are not being trained properly in their language,\u8221\'20\'1D Maria Payan said. New workers are frequently instructed to imitate the person next to them. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you understand this industry \u8211\'20\'13 these are very, very, very dangerous jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D Payan said.\par \par \uc2 A third man, on his first day working in the sanitation department of a processing plant, was dipping machine parts into caustic chemicals, and he started feeling an itch on his arms. Soon, the burning intensified. He pulled back his sleeves, and the skin of his forearms, from wrist to elbow, was blistered and peeling.\par \par \uc2 His co-worker said he must have raised his hands above his elbows \u8211\'20\'13 which he hadn\u8217\'20\'19t realized was forbidden \u8211\'20\'13 and the chemicals dripped from his gloves down his sleeves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was no proper training at all,\u8221\'20\'1D the man said.\par \par \uc2 The on-site nurse told him to wash the chemicals off with soap, and she later referred him to occupational therapy \u8211\'20\'13 not the emergency room, the man said. He wasn\u8217\'20\'19t able to work for three months.\par \par \uc2 Back home in Haiti, the man was an accountant, but in the US, he will work any job he can. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s about survival,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250408el48001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
