\par\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 UK politics: \uc2 Unite\uc2  hits back at Starmer over Birmingham bin strike, questioning Labour\u8217\'20\'19s backing for \u8216\'20\'18working people\u8217\'20\'19\u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Sparrow (now) and Yohannes Lowe (earlier) \par \uc2 6,353 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2025\par 16:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union says the government should intervene directly and force the council to settle the disputeTrump claims Starmer \u8216\'20\'18very happy\u8217\'20\'19 about tariffs\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  will be speaking to international leaders this weekend to discuss how they should respond to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump tariffs}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has said. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efc3e28f0843b305e4ab5d#block-67efc3e28f0843b305e4ab5d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12.54pm}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has refused to confirm President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claim that \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery happy\u8221\'20\'1D about the treatment the UK is getting under the new US global tariff regime. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efc28a8f082d3642a7815d#block-67efc28a8f082d3642a7815d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12.34pm}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  , the foreign secretary, has suggested that President Trump has taken US economic policy back almost 100 years with his \u8220\'20\'1Creturn to protectionism\u8221\'20\'1D. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ef9b6b8f08f7ab2f00d4f9#block-67ef9b6b8f08f7ab2f00d4f9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.499am}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The FTSE was down almost 5% at close of play today, representing the biggest daily drop since early in the Covid pandemic, amid ongoing concern about the impact of the Trump tariffs, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/apr/04/us-business-stock-markets-nyse-blog-trump-tariffs-asian-markets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the business live blog reports.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has criticised the \uc2 Unite union\uc2  for its conduct in the ongoing bin strike in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/birmingham"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Birmingham}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and \uc2 Unite\uc2  has hit back. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efcca78f0843b305e4abeb#block-67efcca78f0843b305e4abeb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  1.32pm}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efec918f082d3642a7845e#block-67efec918f082d3642a7845e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  3.45pm}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/apr/04/abortion-campaigner-livia-tossici-bolt-buffer-zone-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 An activist whose case had been cited by the US state department over \u8220\'20\'1Cfreedom of expression\u8221\'20\'1D concerns in the UK has been convicted of breaching a buffer zone outside an abortion clinic.}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 IFS\uc2  gives its verdict on how \uc2 Gordon Brown\uc2  's tax credits, finally being phased on on Sunday, changed welfare\par \par \uc2 Sunday marks the start of a new tax year. It will be the day the employer national insurance contribution rise takes effect. But \uc2 the Institute for Fiscal Studies\uc2  says it will be significant for another reason too; it says the new financial year will mean the end of the working tax credit and the child tax credits \u8211\'20\'13 two important benefits that are being phased out, and replaced by universal credit.\par \par \uc2 Tax credits were created by \uc2 Gordon Brown\uc2  when he was chancellor. It is often alleged that he did not disclose to \uc2 Tony Blair\uc2  quite how much money they were going to cost, and they had a big impact reducing child poverty. The \uc2 IFS\uc2  has published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ifs.org.uk/articles/how-did-tax-credits-transform-uk-welfare-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a good briefing }}}\uc2  on how they worked and what they achieved. Anyone who thinks politicians never change anything should give it a read.\par \par \uc2 Here is the \uc2 IFS\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s conclusion.\par \par \uc2 The tax credit system, and subsequent increases to benefit entitlements in the 2000s, significantly boosted benefit income for families with children, reducing child poverty. It was able to do this without drastically harming work incentives by implementing higher benefit entitlements for families in and out of work \u8211\'20\'13 meaning that the cost of the benefit system increased substantially. Since 2010, there has been a partial reversal of direction, with benefits for larger families in particular significantly reduced. But the legacy of a larger welfare system, more targeted at children and low-income working families, lives on in universal credit.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tory party keeps selecting wrong people to be MPs, says former chief whip \uc2 Simon Hart\par \par \uc2 In their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/newsletter/london-playbook/five-year-keir/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 London Playbook briefing}}}\uc2  for Politico, Andrew McDonald and Bethany Dawson have what they describe as a mini-scoop \u8211\'20\'13 plans from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.conservativestogether.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Conservatives Together}}}\uc2 , a group set up by the former Tory defence secretary \uc2 Grant Shapps\uc2  last autumn. They explain:\par \par \uc2 The not-a-think-tank Conservatives Together \u8212\'20\'14 formed by ex-defence secretary \uc2 Grant Shapps\uc2  to playfully copy Morgan McSweeney\u8217\'20\'19s Labour Together \u8212\'20\'14 is building an \u8220\'20\'1Carmy\u8221\'20\'1D of campaigners and a \u8220\'20\'1Cfellowship\u8221\'20\'1D of prospective Tory candidates. Playbook has seen an internal document that lays out the path ahead for the campaign group.\par \par \uc2 Cons Together is focusing on three areas as it tries to reassure a sharply divided Tory party that it\u8217\'20\'19s there to help rather than fuel factional infighting. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not about policy,\u8221\'20\'1D the document reads. Instead, it will set up a fellowship program for Tory candidates to train them into how to be MPs \u8230\'20\'26 do data analysis that it will pass to CCHQ \u8230\'20\'26 and set up a a branded T-shirt-clad \u8220\'20\'1Carmy\u8221\'20\'1D of campaigners to direct to constituencies and areas with very few activists (which is most of them, right now).\par \par \uc2 The news that Shapps and his organisation are focusing on training for potential MPs will probably be welcomed by \uc2 Simon Hart\uc2  , who was Tory chief whip when \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  was PM. Hart recently published his diaries, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.panmacmillan.com/authors/simon-hart/ungovernable/9781035068791"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ungovernable}}}\uc2 , and while most of the press coverage of it concentrated on his stories about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/6-explosive-claims-top-torys-34721612"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the sexual shenanigans of MPs}}}\uc2 , one of the main arguments of the book was that the Conservative party is very bad at choosing candidates. At one point he writes:\par \par \uc2 Another meeting with \uc2 John Redwood\uc2  about Northern Ireland, about which he knows much more than me. Our meetings are always polite, but completely devoid of humour. Like [Bill] Cash, he purports to be on the government\u8217\'20\'19s side but sincerely believes he knows better than everyone else on every topic. Politics just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work like that. It is a collective endeavour, involving compromise and agreement. It leaves me reflecting, more and more, about how these people get through the process of candidate identification and selection.\par \par \uc2 After recalling other encounters with awkward or unhinged MPs, Hart normally ends up making the same point. In another entry he says:\par \par \uc2 With each passing day I came increasingly to realise that our system of candidate identification, selection, training and mentoring was flawed and is at the root of almost every challenge the party and the government was facing.\par \par \uc2 Hart\u8217\'20\'19s book is sometimes very funny, and worth a read, not least because he comes across as likeable, and mostly a good judge of character. He seems to like journalists (on a meeting with the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Jessica Elgot, he says \u8220\'20\'1Cshe speaks a lot of sense\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 which is true, she does), on \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  he says \u8220\'20\'1Cshe is another one who lives in a permanent sense of outrage, it must be so tiring\u8221\'20\'1D and on Lee Anderson he says: \u8220\'20\'1CI have tried to avoid the conclusion that he is a total knob, but he has made it nearly impossible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Former Tory MSP Jamie Greene defects to Lib Dems, saying they are party of 'ambition, openness and freedom'\par \par \uc2 Severin Carrell is the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Scotland editor.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Greene, the Holyrood MSP who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/03/keir-starmer-uk-trump-tariffs-response-labour-local-elections-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-67eea6908f0843b305e49fe7#block-67eea6908f0843b305e49fe7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quit the Scottish Conservatives }}}\uc2  in protest at the party becoming \u8220\'20\'1CTrumpesque in style and substance\u8221\'20\'1D, has jumped ship to join the Scottish Liberal Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Greene was unveiled as the Scottish Lib Dems opened their spring conference in Inverness on Friday in a carefully choreographed move which exposes the tensions and strains the Tories face as they shift ground to counter the rise of Reform UK.\par \par \uc2 A list MSP for the West of Scotland, he is now the fifth Scottish Lib Dem at Holyrood. He was on the one nation wing of the Scottish Tories, which grew in influence under former leader Ruth Davidson, and had offered qualified support for trans rights, in opposition to the Tories\u8217\'20\'19 growing attacks on gender reform.\par \par \uc2 In his speech, Greene said:\par \par \uc2 In politics, I have worked to make life better for the people I represent and to live up to the values of ambition, openness, and freedom that have driven me all my life.\par \par \uc2 As my former party misguidedly revels in the worst aspects of our politics, I have chosen to join a party whose values align with mine - the Scottish Liberal Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Alex Cole-Hamilton\uc2  , the Scottish Lib Dem leader, said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cover the moon\u8221\'20\'1D and claimed it showed his party, which hopes to win moderate, centre-right voters after losing seats to become Holyrood\u8217\'20\'19s fifth party behind the Greens, was growing in strength and influence.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 Jamie is respected on all sides of the chamber. He is one of the parliament\u8217\'20\'19s most effective communicators and has a selfless compassion which is seldom seen in our line of work.\par \par \uc2 He speaks to a massive constituency of people who voted for Ruth Davidson\u8217\'20\'19s Conservatives and are now dismayed to see that party lurching to extremes. His words have sent a shockwave through our country\u8217\'20\'19s politics.\par \par \uc2 Cole-Hamilton has also welcomed the defection on social media.\par \par \uc2 enltrI am delighted to welcome Jamie into the Scottish Liberal Democrats.\par \par \uc2 There are many who voted for Ruth Davidson\u8217\'20\'19s Conservatives who are dismayed to see the party lurching to extremes.\par \par \uc2 Jamie is one of them, but he sees in the Liberal Democrats a chance to do things differently. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JJUqtULf0l"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JJUqtULf0l}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Alex Cole-Hamilton\uc2  MSP?????? (@agcolehamilton) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/agcolehamilton/status/1908164134459396140?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 4, 2025}}}\uc2  MSP?????? (@agcolehamilton)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Public support for independence in Wales hits 41%, poll suggests\par \par \uc2 Public support for Welsh independence has reached 41%, a poll suggests.\par \par \uc2 YesCymru, which is campaigning for an independent Wales, has released a poll of Welsh voters saying that, in a referendum now, 41% of those expressing a preference would vote for independence, and 59% would vote against. The group says this is one of the highest pro-independence figures ever recorded.\par \par \uc2 But the pro-independence numbers are even higher if independence is linked to EU membership. When respondents were asked to \u8220\'20\'1Cimagine a scenario where Wales could rejoin the \uc2 European Union\uc2  if it became an independent country\u8221\'20\'1D and then asked the same polling question, 51% said yes to independence, and 49% said no.\par \par \uc2 The online polling was carried out by Redfield and Wilton Strategies, with a sample of 1,000.\par \par \uc2 YesCymru chair Phyl Griffiths said:\par \par \uc2 This poll is a milestone moment for the Welsh independence movement. It shows that support is growing at a fast pace, up 5 points from the same company\u8217\'20\'19s poll last year, and that nearly half of working-age adults now have confidence in Wales\u8217\'20\'19s ability to govern itself.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re witnessing a real shift in attitudes, and people across Wales are ready to have a serious conversation about independence.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  hits back at Starmer over Birmingham bin strike, questioning Labour's commitment to 'working people'\par \par \uc2 The war of words between \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  and the \uc2 Unite union\uc2  is escalating. After No 10 issued a strong statement criticising its conduct in the Birmingham bin strike (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efcca78f0843b305e4abeb#block-67efcca78f0843b305e4abeb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.32pm}}}\uc2  ), \uc2 Unite\uc2  has hit back, questioning Labour\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to \u8220\'20\'1Cworking people\u8221\'20\'1D and saying the government should intervene directly in the dispute and force the council to settle.\par \par \uc2 In a statement issued in response to what Downing Street said earlier, Sharon Graham, the \uc2 Unite\uc2  general secretary, said:\par \par \uc2 It is not surprising that many workers in Britian question the Labour government\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to working people when it issues a statement clearly blaming bin workers in a dispute not of their making.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line about this dispute is that these workers woke up one morning to be told they would be taking up to an \u163\'00\'A38,000 pay cut. They are being made to pay the price for austerity and bad decisions by \uc2 Birmingham city council\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Hold the front page, \uc2 Unite\uc2  has already agreed major changes, with the removal of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/job_and_knock"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  job and knock }}}\uc2  and shift pay last year and in \uc2 Unite\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s current proposals there are no equal pay issues. This authority is determined to impose cuts on workers at any cost and has moved the goalposts again.\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s team of decision makers has been in negotiations in good faith for weeks. It is the leader of the council who is missing in action and not been in any of the talks. Indeed the council are only scheduling meetings once a week. \uc2 Unite\uc2  has said it is ready to negotiate anytime and everyday if necessary.\par \par \uc2 The government is going to have to wake up and smell the coffee that they are part of this dispute, as the commissioners report directly to them and they own the \u163\'00\'A33.9bn debt of the council.\par \par \uc2 If the government were really concerned about the residents of Birmingham they would get the decision makers in a room of which they are clearly one, to ensure that \uc2 Unite\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s solutions on the table were adopted.\par \par \uc2 Yet again workers and communities pay the price for government inaction.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Some news away from the continuing fallout from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariff announcement. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/article/uk-pipeline-gas-security-russia-baltic-sea-threat-defense/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico is reporting}}}\uc2  that the UK is reviewing how it defends critical infrastructure such as undersea gas pipelines and data cables.\par \par \uc2 Protecting \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical undersea infrastructure\u8221\'20\'1D will be included in the government\u8217\'20\'19s Strategic Defence Review (SDR), due to be completed by the end of the month, an MoD spokesperson confirmed to the outlet. It will assess the state of the armed forces, the threats the UK faces and the capabilities needed to combat them.\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  last month announced significant cuts to Britain\u8217\'20\'19s international aid budget to help pay for a major increase in defence spending.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said the \uc2 UK government\uc2  would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/feb/23/starmer-unlikely-to-unveil-plan-for-rise-in-defence-spending-this-week-says-minister"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increase defence spending}}}\uc2  to 2.5% of GDP by 2027 \u8211\'20\'13 three years earlier than planned \u8211\'20\'13 as the Trump administration signalled it was no longer primarily focused on European security and that Europe would have to take the lead in defending Ukraine from Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 Starmer has so far resisted pressure to boost defence spending to 3% of GDP, but some defence chiefs want him to go further, amid growing fears of sabotage and other forms of hybrid warfare by Russia.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over the potential sabotage of power cables, telecom links and gas pipelines have been increasing after a string of outages in the Baltic Sea following Russia\u8217\'20\'19s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\par \par \uc2 In January, the UK took the rare step of publicly naming a Russian vessel, the Yantar, as a spy ship operating in the North Sea.\par \par \uc2 Defence minister \uc2 John Healey\uc2  said Yantar, used for intelligence and mapping critical infrastructure on the sea floor, entered British waters on 20 January and the \uc2 Royal Navy\uc2  tracked it for two days until it entered Dutch waters.\par \par \uc2 European authorities are investigating several cases of damage to infrastructure under the Baltic Sea, including to a power cable linking Estonia and Finland. Finnish authorities detained a ship, suspected of being part of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cshadow fleet\u8221\'20\'1D used to avoid sanctions, after that cable and others were damaged.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  , the chancellor, has said the government is \u8220\'20\'1Cworking hard\u8221\'20\'1D to get a trade deal with the US \u8211\'20\'13 but without saying how long this might take.\par \par \uc2 Talking to journalists on a visit in London, she said:\par \par \uc2 Of course, we don\u8217\'20\'19t want to see tariffs on UK exports, and we\u8217\'20\'19re working hard as a government in discussion with our counterparts in the US to represent the British national interest and support British jobs and British industry.\par \par \uc2 Asked how long those talks might take, she replied:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to give a running commentary on those discussions. They\u8217\'20\'19ve been ongoing since our prime minister, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  , had a successful visit to the White House to meet President Trump just a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Those conversations are ongoing. We want to do everything in our power, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to do everything in our power to get the best possible deal for British industry, working closely with them to protect prosperity and jobs here in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Yohannes Lowe is taking over for a bit. I will be back later.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 No 10 defends abortion buffer zone law that led to prosecution attacked by US on free speech grounds\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has defended the laws that led to the prosecution of Livia Tossici-Bolt, the anti-abortion campaigner found guilty today of breaching a buffer zone outside an abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 The US state department has criticised the prosecution as an interference with freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 At the lobby briefing, asked about the case, the PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson said he did not want to comment on the specific court decision. But he defended the law that was being enforced. He said:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s vital that a woman who decides to use abortion services has the right to choose to do so without being subject to harassment or distress.\par \par \uc2 The rules that we introduced in October protect women using these services with a 150-metre buffer zone and restates that the right to protest is a cornerstone of our democracy, but it does not give people the right to harass others.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Four weeks today we will be digesting the results of the local elections in England. Se\u225\'00\'E1n Clarke has a good interactive graphic here explaining the elections that are taking place.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/ng-interactive/2025/apr/04/english-local-elections-2025-is-your-council-up-for-election-and-whats-at-stake"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 English local elections 2025: is your council up for election and what\u8217\'20\'19s at stake?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  criticises \uc2 Unite\uc2  over its conduct in Birmingham bin strike, and urges it to negotiate settlement 'in good faith'\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has criticised the \uc2 Unite union\uc2  for its conduct in the ongoing bin strike in Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 At the morning lobby briefing, the PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson defended the Labour-run council\u8217\'20\'19s reorganisation plans that have led to the strike, and said it was time for the union to start negotiating \u8220\'20\'1Cin good faith\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  is one of the biggest unions affiliated to the Labour party, and a major donor. When \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was asked about the ongoing bin strike at PMQs this week, he defended the council\u8217\'20\'19s decision to declare the strike {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/mar/31/keir-starmer-immigration-yvette-cooper-labour-uk-politics-latest-live-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eabe6e8f08167d07b364b5#block-67eabe6e8f08167d07b364b5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a major incident}}}\uc2 , but this is the first time the government has come out so strongly against the union.\par \par \uc2 Asked why the government was not intervening, the spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 I think we should be clear about why this situation has come about.\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  is striking against \uc2 Birmingham city council\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s decision to reform unfair staff structures that were a major cause of unequal pay claims and left the council liable to hundreds of millions of claims. This was a key factor cited in the council section 114 notice declaring bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 Under the council\u8217\'20\'19s current plans, no worker need lose any money. They\u8217\'20\'19ve all been offered alternative employment at the same pay, for example, training to be an HGV driver, or voluntary redundancy.\par \par \uc2 And the residents of Birmingham are our first and foremost priority.\par \par \uc2 As you will have seen, the local government minister \uc2 Jim McMahon\uc2  was in Birmingham yesterday meeting council leaders and commissioners to discuss the council\u8217\'20\'19s response and make sure this has been gripped.\par \par \uc2 Following that meeting, police installed barriers at the picket line to prevent waste lorries being recklessly blocked from leaving the depots this morning to start dealing with the backlog.\par \par \uc2 Unite\uc2  need to focus on negotiating in good faith, drop their opposition to changes needed to resolve long-standing pay issues and get round the table with the council to bring a strike to an end.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson also said the strike was \u8220\'20\'1Ccausing misery and disruption to residents, which is why we are urgently pressing for an immediate agreement to be made\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 After visiting the council yesterday to discuss the strike, McMahon issued a statement saying:\par \par \uc2 Residents want this rubbish dealt with as soon as possible and I have made it clear in today\u8217\'20\'19s meeting that we\u8217\'20\'19re ready to support to improve conditions on the ground.\par \par \uc2 It is in the interest of all parties, and most importantly Birmingham\u8217\'20\'19s residents, that this strike must be brought to a close with all parties redoubling efforts to get around the table and to find a resolution.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Starmer to hold talks with other global leaders this weekend to discuss response to Trump tariffs, No 10 says\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  will be speaking to international leaders this weekend to discuss how they should respond to the Trump tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to journalists at the morning lobby briefing, the PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson would not give details of who the prime minister would be consulting, but he said the conversations were prompted by the need to work \u8220\'20\'1Ceven more closely\u8221\'20\'1D with allies in the light of what President Trump is doing.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 We are very much aware that the global economic landscape is shifting. It means we have a responsibility to work even more closely with other countries to maintain stability and strengthen our partnerships abroad.\par \par \uc2 And you\u8217\'20\'19ll see the prime minister engaging with international leaders over the weekend on this.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson also reminded reporters of what Starmer said yesterday, when he said the Trump tariffs were not \u8220\'20\'1Ca short-term tactical exercise\u8221\'20\'1D, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/03/keir-starmer-uk-trump-tariffs-response-labour-local-elections-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ee9bcc8f083b47fa85597a#block-67ee9bcc8f083b47fa85597a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the start of a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew era\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asked if Starmer favoured the sort of approach proposed by \uc2 Jim O\u8217\'20\'19Neill\uc2  this morning \u8211\'20\'13 the non-US working together to deepen free trade, sidelining Washington (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efa0038f082d3642a77ed0#block-67efa0038f082d3642a77ed0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10.36am}}}\uc2  ) \u8211\'20\'13 the spokesperson replied:\par \par \uc2 What the prime minister is concentrating on is his engagement with our global partners, and you will see evidence of that this weekend.\par \par \uc2 But, as we\u8217\'20\'19ve said before, we will be maintaining a cool, headed and pragmatic approach, and one that is grounded in our national interest.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson claimed government policies already in the pipeline showed that it was adapting to new circumstances. He said:\par \par \uc2 The global economic landscape is shifting and we need to shift with it. And, as you\u8217\'20\'19ve already seen, through overhauling our planning system, bringing forward our industrial strategy and cutting excess red tape, we already embracing that new area and ready to tackle it.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson also said the government would be \u8220\'20\'1Cturbocharging\u8221\'20\'1D its work to deliver stability and created growth, and that Starmer would be saying more about this \u8220\'20\'1Cin the coming days\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 No 10 confirms government 'disappointed' by US tariff policy, not 'very happy' as Trump claimed\par \par \uc2 Downing Street\uc2  has refused to confirm President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claim that \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery happy\u8221\'20\'1D about the treatment the UK is getting under the new US global tariff regime. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ef867b8f08f7ab2f00d442#block-67ef867b8f08f7ab2f00d442"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  9.32am}}}\uc2 .) Asked about the president\u8217\'20\'19s words at the morning lobby briefing, the PM\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson said that the government had already set out its position yesterday and that it was \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointed\u8221\'20\'1D by the US tariff policy.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Livia Tossici-Bolt has been sentenced at Poole magistrates\u8217\'20\'19 court to a conditional discharge for two years for two charges of breaching a \u8220\'20\'1Cbuffer zone\u8221\'20\'1D outside an abortion clinic in Bournemouth, PA Media reports. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/04/trump-claim-starmer-very-happy-uk-us-global-tariffs-trade-deal-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67efac338f08f7ab2f00d5c7#block-67efac338f08f7ab2f00d5c7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 11.22am}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Labour has pulled 30,000 more children into poverty by not scrapping two-child benefit cap, charity says\par \par \uc2 Labour\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to lift the two-child benefit cap has pulled 30,000 more children into poverty since the general election, the \uc2 Child Poverty Action Group\uc2  has said.\par \par \uc2 The charity published the calculation as \uc2 Liz Kendall\uc2  , the work and pensions secretary, said that she would regard it as a personal failure if child poverty is not falling by the time of the next general election.\par \par \uc2 Last week the \uc2 Department for Work and Pensions\uc2  published figures showing that 250,000 more people, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/live/2025/mar/26/spring-statement-rachel-reeves-budget-labour-benefit-cuts-pmqs-keir-starmer-kemi-badenoch-uk-politics-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67e407c98f08134b916f2bbd#block-67e407c98f08134b916f2bbd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including 50,000 more children}}}\uc2 , will be pushed into poverty by the sickness and disability benefit cuts announced in the spring statement.\par \par \uc2 Introduced by the Tories, the two-child cap means that parents do not get universal credit child allowance, or tax credits, for a third child born after April 2017.\par \par \uc2 The policy is seen by expert as a a major contributor to child poverty. Polling suggests voters favour the cap, but most Labour MPs want to see it scrapped, and many are disappointed that has not happened already.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cpag.org.uk/news/new-costings-scrapping-two-child-limit-far-most-cost-effective-way-cut-child-poverty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a news release}}}\uc2 , \uc2 CPAG\uc2  said that other interventions to reduce child povert would fail without the cap also being removed. It explained:\par \par \uc2 This is because as the two-child limit applies to third or subsequent children born after April 2017, it is effectively still being rolled out, with more and more children affected every day. This means the policy acts as a brake on any alternative steps government may take. The number of children affected by the policy will continue to increase until 2035 when the first children born under the two-child limit turn 18.\par \par \uc2 Every day 109 more children are pulled into poverty by the policy.\par \par \uc2 CPAG\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s analysis finds that on the eighth anniversary of the two-child limit (Sunday 6 April), an estimated 30,000 more children will have been pulled into poverty by the policy since the government took office.\par \par \uc2 CPAG\uc2  has published details of its analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cpag.org.uk/sites/default/files/2025-04/Reducing_child_poverty_role_of_two-child_limit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In its manifesto Labour said it would \u8220\'20\'1Cdevelop an ambitious strategy to reduce child poverty\u8221\'20\'1D. A child poverty taskforce has been set up by the government, and it is due to publish its strategy this spring.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inews.co.uk/news/politics/kendall-rules-out-lifting-two-child-benefit-cap-but-says-child-poverty-a-priority-3622945"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an interview with the i}}}\uc2  published today, Kendall refused to commit to lifting the two-child benefit cap. But she said:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve got a clear manifesto commitment to tackle poverty and drive child poverty down and that is what we will deliver. Child poverty will be going down.\par \par \uc2 Asked if she would regard it as a personal failure if child poverty rates are rising at the time of the next election, she replied: \u8220\'20\'1CYes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.52am BST\par \par \uc2 London mayor to get new powers to overrule councils that block pubs and clubs from opening late\par \par \uc2 London councils that block pubs and clubs from opening later could see their decisions overturned by the capital\u8217\'20\'19s mayor, \uc2 Sadiq Khan\uc2  , under new powers from the government, \uc2 PA Media\uc2  reports. PA says:\par \par \uc2 Ministers are attempting to boost the hospitality industry by allowing the London mayor to \u8220\'20\'1Ccall in\u8221\'20\'1D licensing decisions for key nightlife areas in the capital.\par \par \uc2 The move could see bars open later and more al fresco dining in London and, if successful, could be extended to other parts of the country such as Greater Manchester or the West Midlands.\par \par \uc2 A source close to the mayor said: \u8220\'20\'1COver recent years, London\u8217\'20\'19s pubs, restaurants, clubs and music venues have been seen as a problem to be managed, rather than as a vital part of London\u8217\'20\'19s economic and social scene. From al fresco dining, to late opening hours, to the impact of burdensome licensing conditions, we need to look at how we can support our venues, not work against them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.41am BST\par \par \uc2 There were three council byelections yesterday. Andrew Teale wrote a preview {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://andrewspreviews.substack.com/p/previewing-the-three-council-by-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on his Substack blog.}}}\uc2  And here are the results, from Britain Elects. The Liberal Democrats won two seats from Labour, and Reform UK won one seat from the Lib Dems.\par \par \uc2 enltr? Reform GAIN from Liberal Democrat\par \par \uc2 Sutton South East (St Helens) council by-election result:\par \par \uc2 REF: 44.5% (+44.5)\par \par \uc2 LAB: 36.3% (+0.7)\par \par \uc2 LDEM: 14.6% (-34.7)\par \par \uc2 CON: 4.6% (-10.5)\par \par \uc2 +/- 2022\par \par \uc2 Estimated turnout: ~18% (-12) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/QSSdXxbUXt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/QSSdXxbUXt}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Britain Elects (@BritainElects) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BritainElects/status/1907923830376472738?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 3, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr? Liberal Democrat GAIN from Labour\par \par \uc2 Park (Lincoln) council by-election result:\par \par \uc2 LDEM: 35.7% (+28.5)\par \par \uc2 LAB: 27.3% (-36.1)\par \par \uc2 REF: 17.6% (+17.6)\par \par \uc2 CON: 8.5% (-9.0)\par \par \uc2 +/- 2023\par \par \uc2 Estimated turnout: ~17% (-3) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/QSSdXxbUXt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/QSSdXxbUXt}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Britain Elects (@BritainElects) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BritainElects/status/1907924108462981479?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 3, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr? Liberal Democrat GAIN from Labour\par \par \uc2 Cwmllynfell and Ystalyfera (Neath Port Talbot) council by-election result:\par \par \uc2 LDEM: 34.0% (+34.0)\par \par \uc2 PC: 30.2% (-26.5)\par \par \uc2 REF: 13.3% (+13.3)\par \par \uc2 LAB: 12.7% (-30.6)\par \par \uc2 IND: 8.7% (+8.7)\par \par \uc2 *Lab defence was of 2nd-placed seat in 2-member ward\par \par \uc2 +/- 2022\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Britain Elects (@BritainElects) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BritainElects/status/1907933845812387980?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April 3, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Anti-abortion campaigner cited by US state department as free speech case found guilty of breaching buffer zone law\par \par \uc2 An anti-abortion campaigner at the centre of a free speech controversy involving the \uc2 US government\uc2  has been found guilty of breaching a \u8220\'20\'1Cbuffer zone\u8221\'20\'1D outside a Bournemouth abortion clinic, PA Media reports. PA says:\par \par \uc2 Livia Tossici-Bolt was convicted at Poole magistrates\u8217\'20\'19 court of two charges of breaching the Public Spaces Protection Order on two days in March 2023.\par \par \uc2 The case involved the 64-year-old from Bournemouth holding a sign saying \u8220\'20\'1CHere to talk, if you want\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Her case was highlighted by the Bureau of Democracy, Human Rights, & Labour (DRL), a bureau within the \uc2 US Department of State\uc2  , which posted a statement on X saying: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are monitoring her case. It is important that the UK respect and protect freedom of expression.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 District Judge Orla Austin told the court of the defendant: \u8220\'20\'1CShe lacks insight that her presence could have a detrimental effect on the women attending the clinic, their associates, staff and members of the public.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CI accept her beliefs were truly held beliefs. Although it\u8217\'20\'19s accepted this defendant held pro-life views, it\u8217\'20\'19s important to note this case is not about the rights and wrongs about abortion but about whether the defendant was in breach of the PSPO (Public Spaces Protection Order).\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week the \uc2 Telegraph\uc2  reported a claim that US objections to this prosecution were holding up the proposed UK/US trade deal. But Jonathan Reynolds, the business secretary, said this case was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/01/uk-politics-labour-conservatives-trump-tariffs-trade-deal-keir-starmer-latest-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eb9f778f08167d07b36cde#block-67eb9f778f08167d07b36cde"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not part of the trade talks}}}\uc2  he was involved in.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.36am BST\par \par \uc2 UK and other G7 allies should sideline US by deepening their own free trade links, says former Treasury minister \uc2 Jim O'Neill\uc2  allies should sideline US by deepening their own free trade links, says former Treasury minister\par \par \uc2 Jim O\u8217\'20\'19Neill\uc2  , the former \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chief economist and former Treasury minister, has said Britain and other non-US G7 nations should respond to the Trump tariffs by deepening their own free trade links.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on the Today progamme, he said that it was perfectly feasible for leading nations still committed to free trade to in effect sideline the US, and trade more with each other.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Neill, who served as a Treasury minister in \uc2 David Cameron\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s government for a year and who is now a crossbench peer, said that G7 countries could take the lead in this, but that India and China should be included too.\par \par \uc2 He said the government was right to carry on talks with the US about a possible trade deal. But he went on:\par \par \uc2 But I think our approach should be to slightly stand back and think, what does Britain want, and can it get from the rest of the world, and what can we contribute?\par \par \uc2 And in that regard, it\u8217\'20\'19s important to realise that the rest of the G7 , except the US, collectively are the same size as the United States. And I would have thought a very sensible thing to be doing is having a serious conversation with the other members about actually lowering trade barriers between ourselves, especially for cross-border services, which is what the UK has a marginal advantage in, which would be very healthy for all of those countries because it\u8217\'20\'19s the one area of global trade where most countries haven\u8217\'20\'19t done enough in.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Neill said that the US was on a \u8220\'20\'1Ckamikaze path\u8221\'20\'1D and that its tariffs were \u8220\'20\'1Crather insane\u8221\'20\'1D. But other countries had the clout to resist, he suggested.\par \par \uc2 Asked if it was possible to just ignore the US, he replied:\par \par \uc2 The US is the biggest economy in the world still, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not anything like as important for global trade as it is in global finance and global security.\par \par \uc2 So if the US wants to do this [impose global tariffs], then it\u8217\'20\'19s perfectly within the bounds of feasibility for other large economies to structure themselves, stop this addiction to the US consumer, and start to consume more themselves, as well as between each other.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Neill said, by turning his back on free trade, Trump was turning his back on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe major thing which has made the United States so prosperous over the last 40 to 50 or more years\u8221\'20\'1D. Other countries \u8220\'20\'1Cshouldn\u8217\'20\'19t get sucked into the same game\u8221\'20\'1D, he said, because overall, \u8220\'20\'1Cwhether it\u8217\'20\'19s life expectancy or wealth\u8221\'20\'1D, the whole world has benefited from this model.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/02/keir-starmer-pmqs-us-tariffs-donald-trump-latest-live-uk-politics-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ecdf5f8f087bcb8730ab54#block-67ecdf5f8f087bcb8730ab54"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Liberal Democrats}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/03/keir-starmer-uk-trump-tariffs-response-labour-local-elections-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ee67078f0848e9ebbcce79#block-67ee67078f0848e9ebbcce79"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Greens }}}\uc2  have both said \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  should respond to the Trump tariffs by strengthening economic links with countries like Canada and the EU. But Starmer has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/02/keir-starmer-pmqs-us-tariffs-donald-trump-latest-live-uk-politics-news?page=with%3Ablock-67ed1c1e8f08d1ca047134fb#block-67ed1c1e8f08d1ca047134fb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  rejected this idea}}}\uc2 , saying it would be wrong to choose between being close to the US and being close to Europe.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.39am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.57am BST\par \par \uc2 Plan to increase access to \uc2 NHS\uc2  dentists in England \u8216\'20\'18a complete failure\u8217\'20\'19, MPs say\par \par \uc2 The official plan to increase access to \uc2 NHS\uc2  dental services in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 England}}}\uc2  has been a \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplete failure\u8221\'20\'1D, and some of the government\u8217\'20\'19s initiatives have worsened the crisis, a damning report warns, Andrew Gregory reports.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2025/apr/04/plan-increase-access-nhs-dentists-england-complete-failure-mps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Plan to increase access to NHS dentists in England \u8216\'20\'18a complete failure\u8217\'20\'19, MPs say}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Lammy suggests Trump has taken US economic policy back almost 100 years with 'return to protectionism'\par \par \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  , the foreign secretary, is at the Nato HQ in Brussels this morning, where \uc2 Nato\uc2  foreign ministers are meeting. Speaking to the media, he said President Trump was taking US economic policy back almost a century by embracing protectionism. He said:\par \par \uc2 The United Kingdom, like France, is a great maritime nation.\par \par \uc2 We are a nation that believes in open trade, and I regret the return to protectionism in the United States, something that we\u8217\'20\'19ve not seen for nearly a century.\par \par \uc2 As you know, we are consulting with business and industry. At this time, we are engaged in discussions with the United States to strike an economic agreement and an economic deal.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we have been absolutely clear that all options are on the table as we ensure the national interests of the British people, who will be very concerned at this time about how this affects the bottom line for them and their economic welfare.\par \par \uc2 We will put their national interest first, and it\u8217\'20\'19s in their national interests to be negotiating with the United States an economic agreement at this time, but keeping all options on the table.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.05am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Minister won\u8217\'20\'19t back Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claim that Starmer \u8216\'20\'18very happy\u8217\'20\'19 about how UK affected by US global tariffs\par \par \uc2 Good morning. The \uc2 British government\uc2  , like the rest of the world, is still preoccupied with trying to absorb the consequences of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s decision to obliterate global free trade with a blizzard of tariffs. In London, the stock market has opened, and shares are still heading down. Graeme Wearden has the latest on that on his business live blog.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2025/apr/04/us-business-stock-markets-nyse-blog-trump-tariffs-asian-markets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FTSE 100 hits lowest level since January as IMF warns Trump tariffs \u8216\'20\'18a significant risk\u8217\'20\'19 to global economy \u8211\'20\'13 business live}}}\par \par \uc2 James Murray, a Treasury minister, has been giving interviews this morning. In terms of explaining the government\u8217\'20\'19s policy, he did not say anything that went beyond what \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  and Jonathan Reynolds {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2025/apr/03/keir-starmer-uk-trump-tariffs-response-labour-local-elections-uk-politics-latest-updates-news?page=with%3Ablock-67eeb6368f082d3642a777b3#block-67eeb6368f082d3642a777b3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  were saying yesterday}}}\uc2 . While not ruling out retaliatory tariffs, the government views them as a last resort and hopes that the trade deal it is negotiating with Washington will lead to the UK tariffs being reduced, or removed completely.\par \par \uc2 But Murray did have to answer a question about whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was right when he told reporters on Air Force One yesterday that Starmer was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery happy about how we treated them with tariffs\u8221\'20\'1D. It is rare to hear anyone from the \uc2 UK government\uc2  say anything negative about Trump in public, but even the ultra-loyalist, fourth-most-senior Treasury minister drew the line at pretending Trump was right about this. In response to the question, Murray told Times Radio:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re disappointed at tariffs being imposed globally. We are in a better position than many other economies moving forward because we\u8217\'20\'19re on the lowest band of tariffs. But our focus is to get that economic deal.\par \par \uc2 There is not much formally in the diary today (parliament is not sitting), but politics never stops, and there is bound to be news, on the Trump tariffs and other matters. There will be a lobby briefing at 11.30am.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid that, for the next few weeks or months, on most days staff shortages mean that comments will only be open on the blog between 10am and 3pm.\par \par \uc2 If you want to contact me, please post a message below the line, when comments are open, or message me on social media. I can\u8217\'20\'19t read all the messages BTL, but if you put \u8220\'20\'1CAndrew\u8221\'20\'1D in a message aimed at me, I am more likely to see it because I search for posts containing that word.\par \par \uc2 If you want to flag something up urgently, it is best to use social media. You can reach me on Bluesky at @andrewsparrowgdn.bsky.social. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/nov/13/the-guardian-no-longer-post-on-x-twitter-elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 given up posting from its official accounts on X}}}\uc2  but individual Guardian journalists are there, I still have my account, and if you message me there at @AndrewSparrow, I will see it and respond if necessary.\par \par \uc2 I find it very helpful when readers point out mistakes, even minor typos. No error is too small to correct. And I find your questions very interesting too. I can\u8217\'20\'19t promise to reply to them all, but I will try to reply to as many as I can, either BTL or sometimes in the blog.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250404el44001gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
