\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers push for settlement in wage theft claims\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2025\par 16:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rideshare drivers held demonstrations from San Francisco to San Diego amid ongoing negotiations affecting 250,000\par \par \uc2 Rideshare drivers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/los-angeles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Angeles}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/san-francisco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Francisco}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/san-diego"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Diego}}}\uc2  held demonstrations in front of city hall buildings on Wednesday as the California state attorney general, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , city attorneys and attorneys representing rideshare drivers continue negotiations next week with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/uber"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Uber}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lyft}}}\uc2  to settle thousands of claims of wage theft from drivers.\par \par \uc2 At least 250,000 individual rideshare drivers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2  who drove for the apps between 2016 and 2020 are estimated to be eligible for the settlement for wage theft claims of tens of billions of dollars, according to Rideshare Drivers United in California.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, more than 5,000 drivers filed wage and hour claims with the California labor commission office against \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , alleging they were misclassified as independent contractors prior to the passage of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/11/california-proposition-22-uber-lyft-doordash-labor-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Prop 22}}}\uc2 , a ballot initiative that created an exemption for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/the-gig-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gig workers}}}\uc2  to be classified as independent contractors.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Russell worked full time as a rideshare driver for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  between 2016 and 2020 in San Bernardino, California, and now works as a teacher and drives part time. He estimates being owed about $300,000 from both companies in unpaid wages, including unpaid overtime, meal and rest breaks, and mileage reimbursement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe more I drove, it seemed, the more I got manipulated,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the big ways you\u8217\'20\'19re losing is the depreciation on your car.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 During that time, Russell said he worked 70-to-80-hour weeks, often chasing bonuses or trying to make up for the unreliable and inconsistent pay, and put 270,000 miles (435,000km) on a single vehicle in just over three years before it broke down, just as he finished paying it off.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI get very frustrated when I hear rideshare companies claim it\u8217\'20\'19s a part-time job, it\u8217\'20\'19s not meant to be full time. No. They created a market and business that is relying on full-time labor, so they have to take accountability for that full-time labor,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CThey built this massive company on the back of our labor. They were undercutting cab companies. They took losses for years to make this happen. Hopefully they will be held accountable for the law they weren\u8217\'20\'19t following before they wrote their own law, Prop 22. Drivers are entitled to that money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not just about the earnings \u8230\'20\'26 It\u8217\'20\'19s about changing the way that these companies operate\par \par \uc2 Ben Valdez, \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver since 2015\par \par \uc2 Ben Valdez, an \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver in Los Angeles since 2015 who was working 20 to 25 hours a week during the four-year period, said he\u8217\'20\'19s owed nearly $200,000 from the wage theft claim he filed against the company.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe companies need to pay the drivers what they what they\u8217\'20\'19re owed. They took advantage of a lot of drivers during the period of time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Valdez. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just about the earnings. There should be compensation for time based on the hours you actually work. The state of California needs to know that it\u8217\'20\'19s not just about the money owed. It\u8217\'20\'19s about changing the way that these companies operate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ibrahim Diallo, a rideshare driver based in San Francisco, claimed his pay has declined over the years during his time working for the apps.\par \par \uc2 Studies, including those from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://laborcenter.berkeley.edu/gig-passenger-and-delivery-driver-pay-in-five-metro-areas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 University of California, Berkeley}}}\uc2 , and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nationalequityatlas.org/prop22-paystudy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Equity Atlas}}}\uc2 , have shown that pay for rideshare and delivery apps have remained significantly low, often below hourly minimum wage rates with expenses, taxes and active working time taken into account, including after Prop 22 was enacted in California. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://protectdriversandservices.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/BRG-Economic-Impact-Analysis_FINAL_1.21.25.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disputed}}}\uc2  low hourly average wages for drivers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have struggled recently against homelessness while trying to still work as a driver,\u8221\'20\'1D Diallo said to Rideshare Drivers United. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter expenses, many of us are earning less than minimum wage. This is inhumane. We need accountability and for the state to force these companies to make better standards for work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Uber\uc2  said in an email that \u8220\'20\'1Cdrivers come to \uc2 Uber\uc2  precisely because of the unique flexibility that it provides. Prop 22 safeguarded their choice to work independently, while ensuring important new protections. The voters of California have spoken \u8211\'20\'13 overwhelmingly \u8211\'20\'13 and we look forward to putting these years-old matters behind us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250326el3q001xj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
