\par\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Starmer is warned against \u8216\'20\'18appeasing\u8217\'20\'19 Trump with tax cut for US tech firms\b0\par\par\uc2 Rowena Mason Whitehall editor \par \uc2 1,143 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 March 2025\par 22:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Labour MP and Lib Dem leader express concern social media companies could be let off hook just as benefits are cut\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has been warned against \u8220\'20\'1Cappeasing\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he considers reducing a major tax for US tech companies while cutting disability benefits and public sector jobs.\par \par \uc2 His chancellor, \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 , confirmed on Sunday that there were \u8220\'20\'1Congoing\u8221\'20\'1D discussions about the UK\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A31bn-a-year digital services tax that affects companies including \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She expressed optimism that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/18/uk-steelmakers-say-trumps-tariffs-already-driving-away-business"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 25% tariffs on British steel}}}\uc2  could be removed in any deal, but did not deny there could be changes to the digital services tax, which the US has lobbied against. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ve got to get the balance right,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 While any changes would not take place in this week\u8217\'20\'19s spring statement, the Liberal Democrats warned Labour was \u8220\'20\'1Cin danger of losing its moral compass\u8221\'20\'1D and it would be \u8220\'20\'1Ctantamount to robbing disabled people to appease [Elon] Musk and Trump\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Labour MPs are already on the brink of rebellion over \u163\'00\'A35bn of welfare cuts to disability benefits and trade unions are concerned about the \u163\'00\'A32bn of Whitehall cuts leading to about 10,000 civil service job losses, plus the risk of wider redundancies in arm\u8217\'20\'19s-length bodies and local government.\par \par \uc2 The Labour MP and former shadow cabinet minister \uc2 Rachael Maskell\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CWith the chancellor saying that she is still looking at the digital services tax, just days before the spring statement, then I would be concerned if relief was granted in what would be seen as a dash to let the US tech companies off the hook, while at the same time as making disabled people pay for the revenue loss, with their lifelines being cut.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI recognise that the chancellor has to rebuild the economy but who pays for this matters, and it must never be the poorest in our society, which will be the outcome should the \uc2 Department for Work and Pensions\uc2  proposals go through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Clive Lewis, the Labour MP and another former shadow cabinet minister, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis was entirely predictable given how desperate the government is to appease the Trump administration and tech oligarchs around it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is extractive politics at it worst and exactly the kind of deal the Maga [Make America Great Again movement] wants. Rather than move closer to Europe and stand together we\u8217\'20\'19re allowing ourselves to be ripped off.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said it was \u8220\'20\'1Can abject surrender\u8221\'20\'1D even before the government chooses tax cuts for big US tech and austerity for the most vulnerable, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CFrankly, it\u8217\'20\'19s unacceptable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 Chi Onwurah\uc2 , the technology committee chair, warned on Sunday that concerns about the safety of artificial intelligence were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/mar/23/labour-chi-onwurah-warning-ai-concerns-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at risk of being ignored by ministers}}}\uc2 , as the government delayed regulating the industry to curry favour with Trump.\par \par \uc2 The Liberal Democrats led opposition criticism of the tax cut idea, with Ed Davey, the party\u8217\'20\'19s leader, telling his spring conference: \u8220\'20\'1CNow Labour\u8217\'20\'19s even talking about scrapping Britain\u8217\'20\'19s tax on social media giants. Well, appeasement never works with bullies, and it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work with Trump. And you can see that he\u8217\'20\'19s already put his tariffs on British steel.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Davey suggested the UK should respond to the threat of steel tariffs from 2 April with tariffs on the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  electric car company, which is part-owned by Musk.\par \par \uc2 He said Starmer\u8217\'20\'19s approach to Trump appeared to be \u8220\'20\'1Clet\u8217\'20\'19s be nice to him and hope he won\u8217\'20\'19t hurt us\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In an interview this weekend, Starmer told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/mar/23/keir-starmer-says-he-likes-and-respects-donald-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he personally \u8220\'20\'1Clikes and respects\u8221\'20\'1D}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  and understood what he was trying to achieve. \u8220\'20\'1CPresident Trump has a point when he says there needs to be a greater burden borne by European countries for the collective self-defence of Europe,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The spending review is likely to focus on higher defence expenditure, paid for through cuts to international aid, and Reeves said on Sunday that the backdrop to her economic plans and lower than expected growth is that the \u8220\'20\'1Cworld has changed\u8221\'20\'1D. The UK is planning three days of intensive military preparations to support a possible ceasefire in Ukraine before a meeting of the so-called coalition of the willing countries in Paris on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Starmer told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that it was a critical moment for the UK and it would not be right to pick either Europe or the US to side with. \u8220\'20\'1CChurchill didn\u8217\'20\'19t do it. Attlee didn\u8217\'20\'19t do it. It\u8217\'20\'19d be a big mistake, in my view, to choose now,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s special envoy said this weekend that Starmer\u8217\'20\'19s plan for a \u8220\'20\'1Ccoalition of the willing\u8221\'20\'1D to support a ceasefire in Ukraine was just a \u8220\'20\'1Cposture and a pose\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/22/steve-witkoff-trump-gaza-ukraine-envoy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Steve Witkoff}}}\uc2 , who is special envoy to the Middle East but also playing a role in Ukraine talks, said the idea was based on a \u8220\'20\'1Csimplistic\u8221\'20\'1D notion of the UK prime minister and other European leaders thinking: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have all got to be like Winston Churchill.\u8221\'20\'1D Witkoff also praised \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , saying he liked the Russian president and did not \u8220\'20\'1Cregard Putin as a bad guy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Talks have resumed among officials in Saudi Arabia between the US, Russia and Ukraine, with higher level discussions to start on Monday. Trump is said to be seeking a deal by Easter.\par \par \uc2 No 10 had no comment on Witkoff\u8217\'20\'19s remarks. But Reeves told BBC One\u8217\'20\'19s Sunday with \uc2 Laura Kuenssberg\uc2  that she was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot put off by that\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will need to make sure that if there is a ceasefire it can be enforced, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what our prime minister, along with allies around the world, is trying to secure, and of course the US has got to be an important part of that,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut it is right that European countries, including the UK, [are] upping defence expenditure because in this uncertain world our national security, our domestic security, is incredibly important for so many things, including a strong economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The business secretary, \uc2 Jonathan Reynolds\uc2 , has been talking to trade counterparts in Washington this week. On the possibility of a cut to the digital services tax, a Treasury spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CAll taxes are kept under review and the 2025 review of the digital services tax has been planned since it was implemented in 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 so it would be wrong to imply any intention to repeal the tax from this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A Labour source said the Lib Dems \u8220\'20\'1Cwere living on another planet and not credible\u8221\'20\'1D by opposing the national insurance rise on employers to fund the \uc2 NHS\uc2  and wanted more money for public services while opposing inheritance tax changes for rich landowners.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250323el3n00232\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
