\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Greenpeace must pay at least $660m over Dakota pipeline protests, says jury\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Leingang and Nina Lakhani \par \uc2 1,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2025\par 22:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Non-profit, which will appeal decision, says lawsuits like this are aimed at \u8216\'20\'18destroying the right to peaceful protest\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A jury in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/north-dakota"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Dakota}}}\uc2  has decided that the environmental group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/greenpeace"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Greenpeace}}}\uc2  must pay hundreds of millions of dollars to the pipeline company Energy Transfer and is liable for defamation and other claims over protests in the state nearly a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Energy Transfer Partners\uc2 , a Dallas-based oil and gas company worth almost $70bn, had sued Greenpeace, alleging defamation and orchestrating criminal behavior by protesters at the Dakota Access pipeline in 2016 and 2017, claiming the organization \u8220\'20\'1Cincited\u8221\'20\'1D people to protest by using a \u8220\'20\'1Cmisinformation campaign\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace, which had denied the claims, said in a statement after the verdict that lawsuits like this were aimed at \u8220\'20\'1Cdestroying the right to peaceful protest\u8221\'20\'1D; constitutional rights experts had expressed fears that the case could have a wider chilling effect on free speech.\par \par \uc2 The nine-person jury in Mandan, North Dakota, found in favor of Energy Transfer on most counts after more than two days of deliberations. It awarded Energy Transfer at least $660m, according to calculations from Greenpeace.\par \par \uc2 The environmental group, which had expressed concerns before the trial about getting a fair hearing in oil and gas country, said that a loss and an enormous financial award could bankrupt their US operation. \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2  sued three Greenpeace entities, claiming that they are a single organization rather than independent members of the Greenpeace network.\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace will appeal the decision, the organization said.\par \par \uc2 The case has been closely watched by the wider non-profit community and first amendment experts amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/20/greenpeace-energy-transfer-dakota-pipeline-trial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 concerns}}}\uc2  over how it could affect activism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we saw over these three weeks was Energy Transfer\u8217\'20\'19s blatant disregard for the voices of the Standing Rock Sioux Tribe. And while they also tried to distort the truth about Greenpeace\u8217\'20\'19s role in the protests, we instead reaffirmed our unwavering commitment to non-violence in every action we take,\u8221\'20\'1D said Deepa Padmanabha, Greenpeace\u8217\'20\'19s senior legal adviser.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe should all be concerned about the future of the first amendment, and lawsuits like these aimed at destroying our rights to peaceful protest and free speech. Greenpeace will continue to do its part to fight for the protection of these fundamental rights for everyone,\u8221\'20\'1D Padmanabha said.\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace International\uc2  was one of the three entities sued by \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2 . Its general counsel, Kristin Casper, said the organization\u8217\'20\'19s fight would continue: \u8220\'20\'1CEnergy Transfer hasn\u8217\'20\'19t heard the last of us in this fight. We\u8217\'20\'19re just getting started with our anti-Slapp [strategic lawsuits against public participation] lawsuit against \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attacks on free speech and peaceful protest. We will see \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2  in court this July in the Netherlands. We will not back down, we will not be silenced.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Energy Transfer thanked the judge and jury in a statement, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we are pleased that Greenpeace has been held accountable for their actions against us, this win is really for the people of Mandan and throughout North Dakota who had to live through the daily harassment and disruptions caused by the protesters who were funded and trained by Greenpeace. It is also a win for all law-abiding Americans who understand the difference between the right to free speech and breaking the law. That the disrupters have been held responsible is a win for all of us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trey Cox, Energy Transfer\u8217\'20\'19s counsel for the case, said the jury\u8217\'20\'19s verdict was \u8220\'20\'1Cresounding\u8221\'20\'1D and showed Greenpeace\u8217\'20\'19s actions had been unlawful. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is also a day of celebration for the constitution, the state of North Dakota and Energy Transfer,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 During jury selection, potential jurors appeared to largely dislike the protests, and many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/27/greenpeace-dapl-jurors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had ties to the fossil fuel industry}}}\uc2 . In the end, more than half the jurors selected to hear the case had ties to the fossil fuel industry, and most had negative views of anti-pipeline protests or groups that oppose the use of fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s verdict is not a reflection of wrongdoing on Greenpeace\u8217\'20\'19s part, but rather the result of a long list of courtroom tactics and propaganda tricks that Energy Transfer used to deny Greenpeace its right to a fair trial,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kirk Herbertson, a New York attorney and the US director for advocacy and campaigns for EarthRights International. \u8220\'20\'1CWe hope that the North Dakota supreme court will question why this case ever made it to trial in the first place.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Concerns over finding an unbiased jury plagued the case even before it began, given the rightward political leanings of Mandan, North Dakota, and the distaste for the protests among local residents. Mysterious rightwing mailers, made to look like a newspaper called \u8220\'20\'1CCentral ND News\u8221\'20\'1D, that contained articles slanted against the pipeline protest or in favor of Energy Transfer were also sent to residents in recent months, which Greenpeace alleged could taint the jury pool.\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace sought to move the trial to another venue in North Dakota multiple times, but was shot down by the county court and the North Dakota supreme court. The judge, James Gion, who was brought in to preside over the case after all Morton county judges recused themselves, denied requests for livestreaming, which the state supreme court also denied.\par \par \uc2 Legal sources {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/20/greenpeace-energy-transfer-dakota-pipeline-trial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have said}}}\uc2  the case is a classic example of a Slapp \u8211\'20\'13 a form of civil litigation increasingly deployed by corporations, politicians and wealthy individuals to deliberately wear down and silence critics including journalists, activists and watchdog groups. These cases, even when the entities suing lose, cause significant legal costs for defendants and can have a chilling effect.\par \par \uc2 The five-week trial saw Energy Transfer attempt to tie a host of misdeeds or disruptions caused by the protests to Greenpeace, which has maintained that its involvement was small and at the request of the Standing Rock Tribe.\par \par \uc2 Standing Rock {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standingrock.org/2025/03/03/standing-rock-sioux-tribal-chairwoman-janet-alkire-statement-on-the-energy-transfer-lawsuit-against-greenpeace/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released a statement}}}\uc2  after the trial began affirming it had led the protests and claiming the tribe had had ongoing issues with getting safety information from \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2 . The pipeline company was \u8220\'20\'1Cfrivolously alleging defamation and seeking money damages, designed to shut down all voice supporting Standing Rock. The case is an attempt to silence our Tribe about the truth of what happened at Standing Rock, and the threat posed by DAPL to our land, our water and our people. The Standing Rock Sioux Tribe will not be silenced,\u8221\'20\'1D the tribal chairperson, Janet Alkire, wrote.\par \par \uc2 In the final days of the case, \uc2 Kelcy Warren\uc2 , \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s billionaire founder and a major donor to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 , said in a video deposition that his company had offered financial incentives \u8211\'20\'13 including money, a luxury ranch and a new school \u8211\'20\'13 to the Standing Rock Sioux Tribe to stop the protests, which the tribe declined, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.trialmonitors.org/energy-transfers-legal-assault-on-greenpeace-threatens-free-speech-rights-trial-monitors-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to}}}\uc2  a group of monitors attending the trial because of concerns over its fairness. Warren said he believed the tribe refused the offer because it was offered more by \uc2 Earthjustice\uc2 , which has served as a legal representative of the tribe.\par \par \uc2 The trial-monitoring committee released a statement after the verdict saying that the trial had been \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply flawed\u8221\'20\'1D and denied Greenpeace the ability to present a full defense. The committee monitored every part of the trial and concluded the jury had been biased in favor of Energy Transfer and the judge lacked full legal knowledge of the complex issues at hand.\par \par \uc2 Marty Garbus, a longtime first amendment lawyer who is part of the monitoring group, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIn my six decades of legal practice, I have never witnessed a trial as unfair as the one against Greenpeace that just ended in the courts of North Dakota \u8230\'20\'26 Greenpeace has a very strong case on appeal. I believe there is a good chance it ultimately will win both in court and in the court of public opinion.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The trial came after \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2  first filed a Rico lawsuit in federal court in 2017. The federal racketeering case was dismissed on 14 February 2019, but seven days later \uc2 Energy Transfer\uc2  refiled a virtually identical suit in North Dakota state court.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250319el3j003pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
