\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Cataclysmic\u8217\'20\'19: environmentalists fear effects of Trump cuts on Great Lakes\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Perkins in Detroit \par \uc2 1,051 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2025\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Advocates warn firings and funding freezes already risk poisoning drinking water and decimating fish population\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s attacks on federal agencies and funding freezes will be \u8220\'20\'1Ccataclysmic\u8221\'20\'1D for the environment of the sensitive Great Lakes region if not reversed, industry and environmental advocates in the region warn.\par \par \uc2 Initial actions taken since Trump returned to the White House in January \u8211\'20\'13 and put Musk in charge of slashing the federal government \u8211\'20\'13 already risk poisoning drinking water, decimating fish populations, and risking the jobs and health of tens of millions of people who rely on the lake system, they add.\par \par \uc2 Trump and Musk\u8217\'20\'19s cost-cutting agency, the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge), promised to dramatically reduce federal spending and workforce levels, and it has already fired hundreds of federal workers while targeting hundreds of millions of dollars of funding dedicated to protecting the waters of the Great Lakes.\par \par \uc2 The administration and Doge\u8217\'20\'19s sledgehammer approach is particularly dangerous for the ecologically sensitive lake system that spans about 1,200 miles (1,900km) from Minnesota to central New York, said Christy McGillivray, political director for \uc2 the Sierra Club\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Michigan chapter.\par \par \uc2 The vital lakes provide drinking water to more than 40 million people in the US and Canada, hold about 90% of the US\u8217\'20\'19s freshwater, and are home to 3,500 species of plants and animals. They also directly support at least $750bn in annual commerce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ignorance is staggering, and they have no idea what the federal government does for this region,\u8221\'20\'1D McGillivray said.\par \par \uc2 Among the first Doge casualties was the \uc2 US Fish and Wildlife Service\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 12-person sea lamprey control unit. Lampreys, called \u8220\'20\'1Cthe vampires of the Great Lakes\u8221\'20\'1D, are a deadly invasive species that threatens to devastate the $7bn Great Lakes fishing industry that supports 75,000 jobs because they prey on and suck blood from native fish. The unit was already struggling to keep the lamprey population under control after Covid slowed its work.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, a multibillion-dollar project in Illinois aimed at repelling invasive silver Asian carp that destroy native fish populations because they deplete food sources {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/great-lakes-invasive-carp-trump-pritzker-5357d23d4c960c0762bfae668a205463"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been shelved}}}\uc2  after federal funding was frozen.\par \par \uc2 Doge eliminated National Oceanic Atmospheric Agency (Noaa) staff for programs that monitor the lakes\u8217\'20\'19 ice cover and operate water-level gauges and weather buoys \u8211\'20\'13 functions \u8220\'20\'1Ccritical to navigation safety\u8221\'20\'1D, a spokesperson for the Lake Carriers\u8217\'20\'19 Association, which represents the US-based Great Lakes shipping industry, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bridgemi.com/michigan-environment-watch/great-lakes-scientists-among-latest-round-trump-cuts-michigan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  Bridge Michigan. \u8220\'20\'1CA degradation of the services that we currently get for navigation safety would be detrimental,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/07/world/canada/trump-trudeau-canada-51st-state.html?smid=nytcore-ios-share&referringSource=articleShare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  wants to shred treaties with Canada that detail how the nations, states and provinces manage the Great Lakes, which could upend the shipping, fishing and tourism industries and damage the environment, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 Drinking water is also at risk. Musk\u8217\'20\'19s Doge cut Noaa staff that help protect it from toxic algal blooms that are difficult for wastewater treatment plants to address, and can cause vomiting, liver problems and kidney damage. Fertilizer runoff from farmland has driven algae problems in Lake Erie, and as recently as 2014 left 400,000 people around Toledo without water for several days. Federal efforts to rein in the fertilizer runoff will likely be targeted, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Environmental Protection Agency has proposed cutting 675 out of 1,035 \uc2 EPA\uc2  staff members who work in Michigan, said Nic Cantello, a union president representing \uc2 EPA\uc2  workers in that state and the upper midwest. Already it has eliminated 18 enforcement staff dedicated to the Great Lakes, including six attorneys, which is grinding to a halt enforcement against the region\u8217\'20\'19s worst polluters.\par \par \uc2 David Ullrich\uc2 , a former deputy administrator at the \uc2 EPA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s region 5 office in Chicago, fears agency employees that manage programs that work with local municipalities to prevent raw sewage from overflowing into the lakes may be next.\par \par \uc2 Ullrich started working at the \uc2 EPA\uc2  in the early 1970s, around when the agency was formed and the Clean Water Act was passed. Around the time, Lake Erie was declared \u8220\'20\'1Cdead\u8221\'20\'1D and some waterways were so polluted that they burned. The government has been \u8220\'20\'1Cchipping away and chipping away\u8221\'20\'1D at the pollution while restoring the lakes, in what Ullrich called a rare decades-long bipartisan effort.\par \par \uc2 The Great Lakes cuts are a \u8220\'20\'1Cdramatic problem\u8221\'20\'1D in part because the progress can be undone much more quickly than the lakes can be restored, and rebooting the programs after they are scrapped is \u8220\'20\'1Ctremendously inefficient\u8221\'20\'1D, Ullrich said. Taxpayers will spend much more than if the programs continued.\par \par \uc2 During his first term, Trump sought to scrap the Great Lakes Restoration Initiative, which, since its early inception under \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 , has coordinated federal agencies\u8217\'20\'19 actions in the region, and funneled $5bn in funding for more than 8,000 projects. Many of those efforts focused on cleaning up pollution hotspots in the lakes and on their shores.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan group of lawmakers thwarted Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to cut the program during his first term, but there is less Republican willingness to stand up to the president\u8217\'20\'19s whims so far, Democrats say.\par \par \uc2 Help isn\u8217\'20\'19t coming at the state level. Federal funding makes up about $750m of the $1bn budget for Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s state environmental agency. The administration is proposing to cut 40% of the $750m, including about $285m for clean drinking water and pollution prevention, Cantello said. Meanwhile, Republicans control the state house of representatives, and just proposed a budget that would fully defund the state\u8217\'20\'19s environmental agency.\par \par \uc2 The climate crisis is leading to swings in lake levels, increased heavy rainfall events, and flooding along the shores that threatens homes and wildlife, as well as economic activity. The administration is already targeting programs that protect the region from those effects, as well as greenhouse gas-reducing measures.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe impact is going to be huge, and it\u8217\'20\'19s important to put this in context,\u8221\'20\'1D Ullrich said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Great Lakes is one of the wonders of the world, it\u8217\'20\'19s our Yellowstone or Grand Canyon here in the midwest, and the impact goes beyond what you can imagine \u8211\'20\'13 there\u8217\'20\'19s an incredible economic, social and environmental significance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250318el3i00260\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
