\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 US Postal Service\uc2  helped build the Black middle class. Trump could end that legacy\b0\par\par\uc2 Gloria Oladipo \par \uc2 1,375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2025\par 21:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump is expected to privatize the USPS, where Black people make up 29% of the staff, and cut down on the number of jobs\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the fate of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/us-postal-service"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 United States Postal Service (USPS)}}}\uc2 , a revered and vital public institution, has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/21/trump-disbanding-usps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uncertain}}}\uc2 . Since the start of his second presidency, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  has launched major changes to the federal government. Along with billionaire {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge), the president has carried out widespread layoffs at agencies such as the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  and the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 , with the purported goal of cutting costs and boosting efficiency. Now, Trump is turning his focus to the post office, an agency he has long been critical of and one that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2025/02/20/trump-usps-takeover-dejoy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he may be privatizing}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition to delivering upwards of 343.5m pieces of mail and packages a day, the post office is responsible for administering official government forms such as passport applications, and providing banking services, such as money orders. As of 2025, it employs 640,000 people. Black people, in particular, make up 29% of its staff, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inequality.org/article/postal-service-jobs-black-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 while making up just 12% of the national workforce}}}\uc2  overall.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2025/02/20/trump-usps-takeover-dejoy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first reported}}}\uc2  that Trump is expected to take control of the USPS, likely signing an executive order in the coming weeks to place the independent agency under the Commerce department. With privatization, Trump would probably curtail postal services, said Arrion Brown, the national support services director for the \uc2 American Postal Workers Union\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe privatization definitely would require less employees,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat would cut down on the number of postal jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s potential plans for the USPS could threaten the agency\u8217\'20\'19s rich legacy of Black employment, from which generations of Black families have secured wealth and benefits through service. \u8220\'20\'1CThe postal service workforce is more diverse racially [and] ethnically than the labor force in this country as a whole,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brian Renfroe, the national president of the National Association of Letter Carriers.\par \par \uc2 The gains in the post office with regards to diversity aren\u8217\'20\'19t accidental. One reason for the high rate of Black employment at the postal office is because the USPS recruits veterans, a large percentage of whom are Black, said \uc2 Monique Morrissey\uc2 , an economist at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re vetted and they\u8217\'20\'19re trustworthy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Morrissey of veterans. \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t want somebody messing with mail. The consequences are harsh if somebody does that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Because of federal protections, the post office, like other federal agencies, also has less discrimination in hiring compared to private sector employment. Anti-discrimination language in USPS\u8217\'20\'19s collective bargaining agreements promotes employee diversity, said Renfroe. \u8220\'20\'1CHundreds of thousands of good middle-class jobs have been offered to people all over the country, without regard to race or any other demographic [information]\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Increased access to postal service employment means that Black families can access solid benefits, especially as generations have been \u8220\'20\'1Cblocked from other ways of creating wealth\u8221\'20\'1D, said Morrissey. \u8220\'20\'1CThe benefits matter a lot to African American communities because of historical barriers to wealth creation and access to financial security through health benefits,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. USPS also pays relatively well compared to other jobs that do not require a college degree, Morrissey added, given training and trust required for the job. USPS also has union-protected pay increases based on how long a person has been employed with the post office, Renfroe noted. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a place to really make a solid middle class career,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we talk about collective bargaining rights being attacked, we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about people\u8217\'20\'19s ability to have a middle class career.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Diversity in the postal service is also a direct result of \u8220\'20\'1Cgeographic diversity\u8221\'20\'1D, said Renfroe. Many of the more than 33,000 post offices in the US are \u8220\'20\'1Cheavily concentrated in New York, Los Angeles, Chicago and other urban areas\u8221\'20\'1D, regions where Black people are more likely to live and be employed from.\par \par \uc2 The wealth building relationship between Black Americans and the postal service dates back to the 19th century, says Phillip Rubio, a history professor at \uc2 North Carolina A&T State University\uc2 . In 1865, Congress passed a law allowing Black people to be employed as postal workers, reversing an 1802 bill that previously declared that mail carriers could only be \u8220\'20\'1Cfree white persons\u8221\'20\'1D. Formerly enslaved people then started \u8220\'20\'1Ctaking advantage of what was a patronage job\u8221\'20\'1D, said Rubio, who\u8217\'20\'19s an expert on Black postal workers. \u8220\'20\'1CThe way the postal service opens up to African Americans is [that] they open the door,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CThe post office [was] attractive when so many private sector jobs were closed to [Black people].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Black people who were already employed by the post office began helping other Black folks obtain jobs, including studying with them for the postal exam. In 1872, Congress revoked the Freedman\u8217\'20\'19s Bureau Act, which provided labor contracts between freed Black people and landowners. With those opportunities gone, \u8220\'20\'1C[the postal office] becomes a means of wealth accumulation and transfer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rubio, particularly for Black people in the south. \u8220\'20\'1CIt becomes a means of higher education for them and their families, also for their children, [and] becomes an avenue to home ownership,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. Black people moving north during the great migration also found work at northern post offices in urban cities.\par \par \uc2 In the 1940s, 14% of middle-class Black people were employed by the post office, according to research by the economists Leah Boustan and Robert Margo. Even still, Black employees were largely denied workplace advancement, including for clerk positions and other higher-ranking roles. Black postal workers, particularly in the south, fought to get into local unions and keep local branches from being segregated.\par \par \uc2 The civil rights movement of the 1960s contributed to improved conditions for Black people in the postal service. President John F Kennedy\u8217\'20\'19s Executive Order 10925 prohibited discrimination in the hiring of federal employees, with USPS offices across the country posting Equal Employment Opportunity posters and notices where employees could submit complaints. Black postal employees were also promoted to higher ranking jobs and elected to local and national leadership positions. From 1960 to 1966, USPS was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://postalmuseum.si.edu/research-articles/the-history-and-experience-of-african-americans-in-america%E2%80%99s-postal-service/1940s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 largest employer of Black people}}}\uc2  in the US, and by 1970, Black people were 2.5 times more likely to work for the post office than white people (the rate is higher in especially segregated cities). By the year 2000, Black postal employees workers were also in the top 25% of earners of Black workers in the US.\par \par \uc2 Today, the postal office\u8217\'20\'19s legacy of diversity and equity are under threat. The tradition of career-long employment, where postal workers remain in service for several decades, could be eliminated as jobs across the board are slashed under privatisation. A reduction in jobs would mean \u8220\'20\'1Cless middle, good paying, benefit jobs in Black communities\u8221\'20\'1D, said Brown, where several distribution centers are concentrated. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s plans could rollback achievements for workers, specifically hard-fought benefits. \u8220\'20\'1CLower wage, non-career employees\u8221\'20\'1D could be drafted into service, said Morrissey, versus workers with a long-term interest in postal service.\par \par \uc2 The ability to negotiate a contract is also a critical feature of the post office employment that could be eliminated, especially given Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/16/trump-anti-worker-actions-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-union actions}}}\uc2 . The current postmaster general \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  recently announced that he would be stepping down, which could result in Trump appointing a postmaster general who \u8220\'20\'1Crefuses to recognize collective bargaining rights, either in part or in whole\u8221\'20\'1D, said Renfroe. The agreement was a direct result of the 1970 postal strike, an eight-day, nationwide demonstration involving 200,000 workers who fought against low wages. Referring to a rollback of union protections, Renfroe warned: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to be prepared for any attacks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, such changes would not only affect the practicality and efficiency of the post office, and its benefits to millions of Americans, but specifically Black families, who\u8217\'20\'19ve long depended on the institution for upward financial mobility. With privatization a likely outcome, Brown said, \u8220\'20\'1CWe couldn\u8217\'20\'19t make the argument in the Black community that postal jobs are safe, good, reliable jobs in the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250311el3b001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
