\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Top City watchdogs drop new diversity and inclusion rules for firms\b0\par\par\uc2 Joanna Partridge \par \uc2 894 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 March 2025\par 18:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 FCA and Bank of England\u8217\'20\'19s PRA join retreat from efforts to help underrepresented groups, citing \u8216\'20\'18regulatory burdens\u8217\'20\'19Peers working for City firms dominate Lords scrutiny panelFCA scraps plan to \u8216\'20\'18name and shame\u8217\'20\'19 UK firms under investigationBusiness live \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par \uc2 The City\u8217\'20\'19s top two regulators have said they will not bring in new diversity and inclusion rules for financial firms because they want to avoid imposing extra \u8220\'20\'1Cregulatory burdens\u8221\'20\'1D and costs, in the latest sign of a retreat from efforts to help underrepresented groups.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s regulatory arm, the \uc2 Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)\uc2 , and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/financial-conduct-authority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Financial Conduct Authority}}}\uc2  (FCA) said they would instead support \u8220\'20\'1Cvoluntary industry initiatives\u8221\'20\'1D aimed at boosting diversity and inclusion in the financial sector.\par \par \uc2 The announcement coincides with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/14/diversity-equity-inclusion-dei-us-trump-google-amazon-walmart"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rollback in the US}}}\uc2  of diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) policies that has accelerated under Donald Trump, and with a push by the \uc2 UK government\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/mar/11/crackdown-on-quangos-part-of-radical-government-proposal-to-reform-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reduce regulation to help stimulate economic growth}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sam Woods\uc2 , a deputy governor at the Bank who leads the \uc2 PRA\uc2 , wrote in a letter to \uc2 Meg Hillier\uc2 , the chair of parliament\u8217\'20\'19s Treasury committee, and the \uc2 FCA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 Nikhil Rathi\uc2 , were choosing to \u8220\'20\'1Cremain alert to the risks of groupthink\u8221\'20\'1D within its existing supervisory framework rather than asking companies to report what measures they were taking to improve representation of women and minorities in their organisations.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/14/parliament-launches-new-inquiry-into-city-sexism-after-scandals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inquiry into sexism and misogyny in the City}}}\uc2 , by the influential Treasury committee, which examined barriers faced by women in financial services, and looked into whether progress had been made on the gender pay gap, stigma against working mothers, and the sector\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Calpha male\u8221\'20\'1D culture.\par \par \uc2 The committee renewed a 2018 inquiry into gender inequality in financial services, after a spate of sexual harassment allegations that rocked the business world, and explored what role regulators, including the \uc2 FCA\uc2 , should play in \u8220\'20\'1Ccombating sexual harassment and misogyny\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 However, a March 2024 report found that \u8220\'20\'1Cnot much\u8221\'20\'1D had changed in the intervening period, and it welcomed proposals by the \uc2 PRA\uc2  and the \uc2 FCA\uc2  to strengthen their non-financial misconduct rules and improve their ability to take action against perpetrators of sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 In the letter, Woods wrote that the \uc2 PRA\uc2  and \uc2 FCA\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/12/financial-watchdog-scraps-plan-to-name-and-shame-uk-firms-under-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scrapped a plan to \u8216\'20\'18name and shame\u8217\'20\'19 UK firms under investigation}}}\uc2  on Wednesday \u8211\'20\'13 believed diversity and inclusion initiatives could benefit firms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe continue to think that an appropriate focus on diversity and inclusion in the culture of the firms we regulate can deliver improved internal governance, decision-making and risk management,\u8221\'20\'1D Woods wrote.\par \par \uc2 He added that such focus \u8220\'20\'1Ccan support both safety and soundness \u8211\'20\'13 through reduced risk of groupthink \u8211\'20\'13 and the competitiveness of UK financial services over the medium to long term\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 However, after consultations with the companies it regulates, and with the Treasury committee, Woods said that financial firms did not want regulators to introduce new rules including gender and ethnicity pay gap reporting, at a time when the government was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/feb/04/labour-plans-extend-equal-pay-rights-black-asian-minority-ethnic-staff"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 planning to bring in new legal reporting requirements}}}\uc2  for companies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany of those who responded to our consultation wanted us to align our regulatory approach with related initiatives, to avoid duplication and unnecessary costs,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote, in a move that could be interpreted as a response to the government\u8217\'20\'19s call for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/16/rachel-reeves-summons-regulator-chiefs-to-downing-street-to-push-growth-agenda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK regulators to do more to support growth}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Woods added: \u8220\'20\'1CThere is also a growing emphasis in our work on reducing regulatory burdens on firms while still delivering our objectives, and adding significant new requirements in this area could be seen as in tension with that approach.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Treasury committee has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm5804/cmselect/cmtreasy/240/summary.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previously expressed its concerns}}}\uc2  that City regulators could require financial firms to collect and report diversity and inclusion data, and set targets, which it said \u8220\'20\'1Cwell-run firms\u8221\'20\'1D should already be doing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe do not currently plan to publish new rules on diversity and inclusion, and do not intend to return to this question until after the substantive implementation of any new legislation in this area,\u8221\'20\'1D Woods wrote.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump has prompted a rollback of DEI programmes in the US, after he signed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/26/trump-executive-orders-dei"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 series of executive orders}}}\uc2  overturning such measures. Several large US corporations, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/15/walmart-dei-diversity-equity-inclusion-rollback"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Walmart}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/jan/06/mcdonalds-diversity-programs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2 , Ford and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jan/10/amazon-ending-dei-programs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2 , followed suit in either scrapping or scaling back their DEI schemes, while the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  owner, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/10/meta-ending-dei-program"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Meta}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jan/07/meta-facebook-instagram-threads-mark-zuckerberg-remove-fact-checkers-recommend-political-content"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overhauled its content moderation}}}\uc2  to remove restrictions on topics such as gender and immigration.\par \par \uc2 Separately on Wednesday, it emerged the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  has offered staff a 3% pay rise in 2025-26, a deal which may disappoint some staff as it only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/19/uk-inflation-increases-rising-prices-squeeze-households"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 matches the UK\u8217\'20\'19s current level of inflation}}}\uc2 , as the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s own forecasters have predicted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/19/uk-inflation-increases-rising-prices-squeeze-households"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prices will start rising again}}}\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 The pay rise, reported by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 , is lower than the previous year, as a result of \u8220\'20\'1Cbudget constraints\u8221\'20\'1D according to a Bank spokesperson, although it added that the majority of \uc2 Unite union\uc2  members have voted in favour of the deal.\par \par \uc2 It highlights the pressures on the central bank to retain staff and attract new employees in the race for talent, at the same time as keeping its budget under control, and after its governor, \uc2 Andrew Bailey\uc2 , was criticised for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/feb/04/bank-of-england-boss-calls-for-wage-restraint-to-help-control-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggesting workers should avoid asking for big pay rises}}}\uc2  in order to help control inflation.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250312el3c001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
