\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Sixty years after Bloody Sunday, civil rights leaders in Selma continue fight\b0\par\par\uc2 George Chidi in Selma, Alabama \par \uc2 1,711 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2025\par 14:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Election of Trump and threats to democracy front of mind as activists remember peaceful marchers attacked by police\par \par \uc2 What would \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  do today?\par \par \uc2 On a Sunday morning 60 years ago, activists rewrote the story of the civil rights movement in their own blood on the streets of Selma, Alabama. State troopers turned their truncheons on a peaceful march across the Edmund Pettus Bridge at the behest of Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s stridently and infamously racist governor George Wallace, protecting Alabama segregation and white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , then the chairman of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, later served in Congress \u8211\'20\'13 and as a moral force in American politics \u8211\'20\'13 for decades. He bore scars from the beating he took that day through the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 How should Americans meet today\u8217\'20\'19s challenges to civil rights and democracy? That question percolates among the activists and visitors crowding Selma\u8217\'20\'19s streets this weekend as the Trump administration wages an intense, wide-ranging war on America\u8217\'20\'19s anti-discrimination policies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a tyrant that\u8217\'20\'19s running the federal government,\u8221\'20\'1D said Delvone Michael, a political strategist from Maryland. Lewis would be going from campus to campus, mobilizing for a confrontation, he said. Michael described the moment as a stress test of American resolve that isn\u8217\'20\'19t being met with sufficient opposition either in Congress or the street.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t see the people that are actually standing up like what happened here, where everyday people stood together and raged against the machine,\u8221\'20\'1D Michael said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re not seeing that right in this moment, at the scale necessary to stop this guy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Progressive congressional leaders are hearing this criticism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got to make sure that we\u8217\'20\'19re not normalizing this moment and stepping up and being unafraid, and I think we are still here playing by the same book when they don\u8217\'20\'19t follow one,\u8221\'20\'1D said Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2  as she walked up the bridge on Sunday afternoon. Williams serves in the Atlanta house seat vacated by Lewis\u8217\'20\'19s death in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The moment today is designed to create chaos, she said. Activists say it should be answered with the kind of effort civil rights leaders of Lewis\u8217\'20\'19s era presented in the face of strident, violent opposition, if the gains of that generation are to be maintained.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t get me wrong, the last 70 years have not brought us where we should be, but the last 70 years made significant progress in this country, and we have been the beneficiaries,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Marc Morial\uc2 , former mayor of New Orleans and president of the \uc2 National Urban League\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhat falls to us is to ensure that our children and children\u8217\'20\'19s children are not going to litigate the battles of our grandparents.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has led the civil rights division of the justice department to end investigations of discrimination against racial minorities in cases like the \u8220\'20\'1Cgoon squad\u8221\'20\'1D assaults in Mississippi and the death of Tyre Nichols in Memphis. Trump fired two commissioners of the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 in violation of the law \u8211\'20\'13 and its acting chairman has pledged to root out \u8220\'20\'1Cunlawful DEI-motivated race and sex discrimination\u8221\'20\'1D instead of its prior role protecting workers of color against race-based discrimination.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI got a lot of folks saying, what are we going to do?\u8221\'20\'1D Morial said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd they almost are looking for a one-punch knockout. Isn\u8217\'20\'19t that one lawsuit? Isn\u8217\'20\'19t there one political move? This is a long trench warfare.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  last year established a doctrine of absolute immunity for acts committed by a president \u8220\'20\'1Cwithin their core constitutional purview\u8221\'20\'1D, and at least presumptive immunity for official acts within the outer perimeter of their official responsibility. The 6-3 ruling creates a predictable flash point in the conflict between an authoritarian government and American civil rights: a moment when Trump faces a court decision he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t like.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to fight in the courts wherever we can, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to be enough,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Margaret Huang\uc2 , president and CEO of the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re actually going to have to mobilize people around the country. There are a lot of people who are upset right now, but they\u8217\'20\'19re not standing up, they\u8217\'20\'19re not seeing themselves as protagonists of this story.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Caleb Jakes, 20, of Newark, described generational despair.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToo many of us are stuck between fear and anxiety,\u8221\'20\'1D he said of other Black twentysomethings looking at the world on fire. \u8220\'20\'1CSo many of us are paralyzed just from imagining that even if I went for it, it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to happen \u8230\'20\'26 The regular way of trying to get the system to work in my favor just aren\u8217\'20\'19t appealing enough any more to merit the effort to stay after it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 People under 20 have known nothing except losses \u8211\'20\'13 economic crisis, civil rights rollbacks and political turmoil, added Donneio Perryman, 20, from Chicago. \u8220\'20\'1CMost of us, if we are not stricken by poverty and trying to get out, are just trying to make a way for ourselves and our families right now as our main goals and not the entirety of our race.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Activists regularly suggest that Trump may be looking for a rationale to invoke the Insurrection Act, declare martial law and turn troops or police on protesters, tempering their enthusiasm for mass demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some of us who are going to be willing to bleed,\u8221\'20\'1D Huang said. \u8220\'20\'1CNot everybody has to. We are not asking everyone to lay down their lives, but we are saying to everyone that this is your fight, and you have to be counted when the chips are down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sixty years ago, demonstrations that led to conflicts also led to progress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe went where we were called,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Andrew Young\uc2 , former ambassador to the United Nations and a contemporary of \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  and Lewis who marched in Selma in 1965. \u8220\'20\'1CAmelia Boynton came to see Dr King just before that Christmas, and she told him that Jim Clark would not let them hold meetings in their churches because he said they were too political.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Boynton was a young Selma activist who organized the march from Selma to Montgomery after an Alabama state trooper killed Jimmie Lee Jackson while he tried to protect his mother from being beaten after an earlier protest. Selma was considered beyond salvation by civil rights activists at the time, but Jackson\u8217\'20\'19s murder and Boynton\u8217\'20\'19s plea drew marchers.\par \par \uc2 King told Young not to go, Young said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe thought they would just turn us around,\u8221\'20\'1D Young said of the state troopers. \u8220\'20\'1CThey didn\u8217\'20\'19t even give them time to pray, or to discuss anything. They just immediately charged.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The beatings on the Edmund Pettus Bridge shocked the nation. Pictures of Lewis on the ground with a trooper looming over him, or of Boynton lying broken in the street amid teargas and horses, ran on the front page of newspapers across the country. ABC News interrupted a documentary about Nazi war crimes as the attack unfolded, cutting to show the violence live in Selma.\par \par \uc2 The metaphor was not lost on the public or lawmakers. Lyndon Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act of 1965 five months later.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 What is the next Selma?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know. And I shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t know,\u8221\'20\'1D Young replied. \u8220\'20\'1CI mean, there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing, yet I see all the signs. I see the things that people are saying.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The streets of Selma fill with marchers every year on this Sunday in March, commemorating the events of 1965. This year has drawn an unusually large crowd. Hotel rooms are full for 30 miles in every direction.\par \par \uc2 Selma itself is caught in amber. Like many towns in Alabama\u8217\'20\'19s Black belt, Selma has been losing population for decades. Many buildings downtown are a ruin, pock-marked by damage from a tornado in 2023 and unchanged since Bloody Sunday. The march has become a carefully stage-managed parade, with politicians jockeying with the scions of the movement for position at the front of the procession where they can be seen by throngs of photographers from across the country.\par \par \uc2 The march was scheduled in waves, with thousands of people grouped in time slots by the hour for their chance to walk and be seen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think part of the reason that so many of us came to Selma is because we really draw inspiration in people who had no reason really to believe that they could get freedom,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Jocelyn Frye\uc2 , president of the \uc2 National Partnership for Women and Families\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThey were facing what may have looked like an all-powerful force, but they had a faith and desire to work for something bigger and better, and I think we\u8217\'20\'19re here because we have that same spirit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The memorial of Bloody Sunday gives the city an economic boost, but after the crowds leave, little changes, said Gary Ransom, a Selma photographer pushing for redevelopment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve always been in the spotlight because of all the historic things that happened here,\u8221\'20\'1D Ransom said. But between the hurricane and a growing gun violence problem, Selma has been bleeding population, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause the kids don\u8217\'20\'19t have nowhere to go or nothing to do\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 For Bernard Lafayette, a civil rights leader and attorney who marched in Selma on Bloody Sunday, the answer is to build a Black economic fortress that can withstand attack.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re still commemorating Bloody Sunday 60 years later,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s time to make a paradigm shift.\u8221\'20\'1D Lafayette has been raising capital to build a factory to manufacture solar panels in Selma. Asked what a 20-year-old activist like Lewis might be expected to do today, Layfayette\u8217\'20\'19s answer was blunt.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey should be saying, \u8216\'20\'18To hell with them and focus on manufacturing,\u8221\'20\'1D Lafayette said. \u8220\'20\'1CFocus on our resources. Focus on the $2tn per year that we generate. White folks are never going to help us. They enslaved us. We have to come to our own rescue. We have to save ourselves, and we can only do that by using our resources for our people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250310el3a001pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
