\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Musk? He\u8217\'20\'19s horrendous\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 Martha Lane Fox\uc2  on diversity, tech bros and International Women\u8217\'20\'19s Day\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Partington \par \uc2 1,101 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2025\par 12:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The investor and former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board member believes rejecting DEI initiatives is simply denying US firms talent\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  grinned in the Oval Office, one of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s most influential tech investors looked on in horror. \u8220\'20\'1CHe is absolutely horrendous. I have said it multiple times: I think it is horrifying what is happening,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Martha Lane Fox\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the British peer and ex-\uc2 Twitter\uc2  board member, the sight of Musk holding forth from the bully pulpit of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s White House shows the Silicon Valley dream has gone sour.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe richest man in the world, who can stand there alongside the president, and kind of carte blanche make jokes about how he\u8217\'20\'19s carving up people\u8217\'20\'19s jobs in the government. Then he can be there with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/23/cpac-elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chainsaw laughing on stage\u8230\'20\'26}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is really, really alarming, and I find it extremely unpleasant at a values-based level \u8211\'20\'13 but also, just how can we be watching this in plain sight? It makes me feel very anxious. I think it is gross.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the Observer to mark International Women\u8217\'20\'19s Day, the president of the \uc2 British Chambers of Commerce (BCC)\uc2  warned the diversity pushback orchestrated by Trump and his tech bro acolytes will not only damage society, but also the economy at large.\par \par \uc2 What Musk is doing is really alarming. How can we all be watching this in plain sight? I think it is gross\par \par \uc2 Since his return to the White House, the US president has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/22/trump-orders-immediate-closure-of-all-us-federal-diversity-offices"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shut down all federal diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) initiatives,}}}\uc2  while Musk\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge) is ripping up funding schemes.\par \par \uc2 Some of the world\u8217\'20\'19s biggest companies are following suit. Amid a wider pushback against everything from environmental targets to sustainable development, among the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/14/diversity-equity-inclusion-dei-us-trump-google-amazon-walmart"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most prominent}}}\uc2  taking part are US finance and tech companies, including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Accenture\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/28/uk-based-british-pharma-gsk-pauses-diversity-work-citing-trump-orders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK businesses such as GSK have also fallen in line}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe needs to be contained,\u8221\'20\'1D Lady Lane Fox says of Musk\u8217\'20\'19s role in the rollback. \u8220\'20\'1CI find it extraordinary that the richest man in the world is trampling all over these things and that we still have kind of fanboying from the tech sector. It\u8217\'20\'19s already been corrosive for society, and I would argue it is going to continue to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For businesses, she says the bottom line is that companies that take diversity seriously appeal to the widest possible employee talent pool and are better placed to target a broad range of customers. This, she adds, is about profit as much as social justice. However, she has a broader concern about the future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe first thing, it\u8217\'20\'19s financial. But the second thing, it\u8217\'20\'19s about power and money \u8211\'20\'13 like everything, right?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re looking at a sector like the digital sector, where there\u8217\'20\'19s the growth in jobs, growth in opportunity \u8211\'20\'13 it is the growth sector in the economy. Yet you are not including a whole bunch of people in that. Then you are going to be creating inequality. Full stop. So it\u8217\'20\'19s financial and it\u8217\'20\'19s a question of social justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Given the close ties between Britain and the US, there is a view that where corporate America treads, the UK naturally follows. But there are signs that some UK businesses \u8211\'20\'13 and even the British operations of some US companies \u8211\'20\'13 are prepared to stand apart.\par \par \uc2 The accountancy firm \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  instructed staff working on contracts for the \uc2 US government\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/11/deloitte-staff-us-government-contracts-pronouns-emails-trump-anti-diversity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remove pronouns from their emails}}}\uc2 , while also announcing the end of its DEI programme. But its UK boss told staff its British operations remained \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to [its] diversity goals\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels as though global companies rooted in the US are making a politically motivated slight shift in emphasis and tilt, through to rowing back everything. And it does feel a bit more tempered here,\u8221\'20\'1D says Lane Fox.\par \par \uc2 UK businesses have an opportunity to do something different, she says, which could bring financial benefits. \u8220\'20\'1CI think we\u8217\'20\'19ll build more robust companies, attract talent and have a much better shot at building the most resilient companies of the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For almost three decades, Lane Fox has built a career \u8211\'20\'13 and multimillion-pound fortune \u8211\'20\'13 in tech. She made her first big money {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2000/mar/14/qanda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 floating Lastminute.com}}}\uc2 , the online travel site co-founded alongside fellow Oxford graduate \uc2 Brent Hoberman\uc2  in 1998.\par \par \uc2 She {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2016/apr/08/twitter-board-new-martha-lane-fox-hugh-johnston"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joined the board of Twitter}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 now X \u8211\'20\'13 in 2016, landing herself a huge payday in Musk\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/oct/27/elon-musk-completes-twitter-takeover"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $44bn hostile takeover}}}\uc2  in 2022, before he dissolved the board and appointed himself the sole director.\par \par \uc2 Seeing Musk in the Oval Office, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/feb/18/elon-musk-kids-work-pr-strategy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 parading his son X on his shoulders}}}\uc2 , made her question the gender divide. \u8220\'20\'1CCan you imagine if that was a woman? Can you imagine what that would look like? I mean, I just think the whole thing is really gross.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But while railing against Musk in a personal capacity, the BCC president does not suggest this approach is for everyone. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is really tricky to navigate. You have a responsibility to your customers and your employees that might be different to our personal view sometimes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Government regulation to enshrine diversity targets is also a bad idea, she says, preferring instead that companies report their progress. \u8220\'20\'1CKeeping it in the light, keeping up the reporting, is important \u8211\'20\'13 keeping up good investors, looking at the right metrics and investing in the right companies all helps.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, not enough progress is being made. Analysis this week showed that worsening unemployment and workforce participation for women has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/mar/03/uk-awarded-its-lowest-ranking-for-workplace-gender-equality-in-a-decade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushed the UK behind Canada}}}\uc2  to its lowest global ranking for workplace equality among large economies in a decade.\par \par \uc2 The gender pay gap has been declining slowly over time, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/earningsandworkinghours/bulletins/genderpaygapintheuk/2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 average pay is still 7% less}}}\uc2  for women than for men. It is a challenge Lane Fox is all too aware of. \u8220\'20\'1CLook at the data and it is really freaking depressing \u8211\'20\'13 and it is not moving,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat worries me is that it\u8217\'20\'19s far too easy to find numbers that I thought we were moving on from.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn this week of International Women\u8217\'20\'19s Day, we see representation at the executive level has gone back. I see progress on boards is still good at the FTSE 100 level, but bad at FTSE 250 and 350 level.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know there will be people in the sector thinking: \u8216\'20\'18Oh, here she goes again.\u8217\'20\'19 That\u8217\'20\'19s true of many women [that people think that]. But it is so important to keep making these arguments.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250311el3a002mt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
