\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 Is This Working? by Charlie Colenutt review \u8211\'20\'13 labours of love in unexpected places\b0\par\par\uc2 Ian Cobain \par \uc2 996 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2025\par 17:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The author\u8217\'20\'19s study of UK workers and their feelings about their jobs uncovers mushrooming red tape and an enduring sense of fatigue, but also a sense of pride\par \par \uc2 A little over 50 years ago, the American broadcaster {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/studsterkel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Studs Terkel}}}\uc2  published an oral history based on interviews with 133 workers across the US. This was a time of automation and global competition, a new era of enormous change, and Terkel wanted to discover how the world of work might offer ordinary people a sense of purpose; of what he described as \u8220\'20\'1Cdaily meaning as well as daily bread\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What he discovered is that there were people doing \u8220\'20\'1Cgood\u8221\'20\'1D jobs, sometimes performed with grace and beauty \u8211\'20\'13 the piano tuner, for example, the stone mason, the firefighter \u8211\'20\'13 but that most workers were trying merely to survive the day. As one of his interviewees told him: \u8220\'20\'1CMost of us\u8230\'20\'26 have jobs that are too small for our spirit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Terkel\u8217\'20\'19s book \u8211\'20\'13 whose full title is Working: People Talk About What They Do All Day and How They Feel About What They Do \u8211\'20\'13 later spawned a Broadway musical, a graphic novel and then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.netflix.com/gb/title/81130576"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Netflix series}}}\uc2  presented by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now it has inspired an examination of the world of work in the UK in the 2020s; one that is again based upon extensive interviews, across a landscape that has largely been deindustrialised. This too is published at a time when the workplace is being revolutionised, this time by artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 In his book, Charlie Colenutt aims, like Terkel, to discover the ways in which our employment can nourish us, or leave us drained. As in the US half a century ago, many people seem to suffer the latter. A panel beater says he aches all the time and has too little time for his children; a chef says most of his colleagues are alcoholics or drug addicts; an accountant laments the loneliness of working from home during lockdown: \u8220\'20\'1CI know everyone\u8217\'20\'19s on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  posting a picture of their coffee with their dog at their feet, sun shining through the kitchen window. It looks like you\u8217\'20\'19re having a nice morning but what are you like at 6pm when it\u8217\'20\'19s gloomy and you haven\u8217\'20\'19t spoken to anyone all day?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even a derivatives trader, paid a vast fortune in the City while still in his 20s, endures existential disquiet: \u8220\'20\'1CI struggle to see the point of my job. I think it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really need to be done. It basically serves only to make myself and my company\u8217\'20\'19s shareholders richer. I\u8217\'20\'19m totally checked-out, to be honest.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A chef says most of his colleagues are alcoholics or drug addicts; an accountant laments the loneliness of working from home during lockdown\par \par \uc2 While Terkel heard frequently how gruelling manual labour could be \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cstrictly muscle work\u8221\'20\'1D, as one steel mill employee put it \u8211\'20\'13 most of Colenutt\u8217\'20\'19s 68 interviewees work in the service economy, and he encounters many complaints about mushrooming bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 A midwife says that whenever she gives an expectant mother a back massage, she must fill in a form. A childminder has three filing cabinets in her kitchen \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cand that\u8217\'20\'19s before you go to my attic\u8221\'20\'1D. A construction site manager has 1,600 unread emails in his inbox. A church minister in Northern Ireland says he has less and less time to study his Bible, as \u8220\'20\'1Can awful lot of ministry today is like being an administrator\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In this way, occupations that looked very different a generation ago are now coming to resemble one another. They also entail similarly increased workloads. A lawyer usually sends her last email at 2.30am. A primary school teacher, who was previously a soldier, says: \u8220\'20\'1CThere were some tough times in the army, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve never felt under more pressure. I\u8217\'20\'19ve probably cried in front of my headteacher more than anyone else in my life. It was far less stressful in Afghanistan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Colenutt believes that the mountains of paperwork are being generated by organisations\u8217\'20\'19 fears of failure: of being sued or publicly criticised by a regulator. As a consequence, workers are doing less of their actual work, instead spending their time inputting data, writing reports and ticking boxes. Morale is sapped and staff turnover rises.\par \par \uc2 But he finds too that the joy and pride of work can be found in unexpected places. A food delivery rider says he is often exhausted and occasionally very wet, but that he loves cycling around London. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a chance to see the sights. I\u8217\'20\'19ve crossed Tower Bridge a hundred times. You feel a freedom on the bike. You are not strapped to a desk. That\u8217\'20\'19s the best thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A cleaner knows she is making a difference in her clients\u8217\'20\'19 lives and says: \u8220\'20\'1CI get a real sense of fulfilment when I look back at a room I\u8217\'20\'19ve cleaned. I think, oh yeah, lovely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Perhaps one of the most satisfied workers in Colenutt\u8217\'20\'19s book is a joiner, who employs one apprentice and who finds that the work he delivers, and the training he gives, results in a life of deep contentment. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want a fleet of 10 vans,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be on a million pounds a year. I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be any higher than I am. I\u8217\'20\'19m happy. Everything\u8217\'20\'19s balanced nicely, work, social life, family, gym, and still time for other things.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that what balances can easily topple over, but if that happens, I\u8217\'20\'19ll deal with it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ian Cobain is the author of Anatomy of a Killing (Granta, \u163\'00\'A3\par \par \uc2 10.99)\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Is This Working?: The Jobs We Do, Told by the People Who Do Them by Charlie Colenutt is published by Picador (\u163\'00\'A320). To support the Guardian and Observer order your copy at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/is-this-working-9781035015047/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guardianbookshop.com}}}\uc2 . Delivery charges may apply\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250312el390033e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
