\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Who are the US federal workers at risk of losing their jobs and what do they do?\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani with visuals by Andrew Witherspoon \par \uc2 1,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2025\par 12:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump, Musk and Doge are making cuts \u8211\'20\'13 here\u8217\'20\'19s what we know about the current government size and demographics\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of US federal employees could lose their jobs after the Trump administration moves to reduce the size of the federal workforce dramatically.\par \par \uc2 With {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2  at the helm of the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdepartment of government efficiency\u8221\'20\'1D (Doge), which is overseeing efforts to cull federal employment, the White House has embarked on multiple plans to cut down on virtually all areas of federal employment. There are no official numbers of how many federal employees will be affected, and many of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Musk\u8217\'20\'19s efforts have been stalled in court, but the impact could be big and long-lasting.\par \par \uc2 To understand what the cuts will do to federal operations, we first have to understand who works for the federal government and what they do. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what we know about the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 How big is the federal workforce?\par \par \uc2 There are about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://data.bls.gov/dataViewer/view/timeseries/CES9091000001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 3 million federal employees}}}\uc2  in the US, about 2% of the entire US workforce, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ), the data division of the Department of Labor.\par \par \uc2 Much of the federal workforce falls under the executive departments, the leaders of which make up the president\u8217\'20\'19s cabinet. The \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  is the largest employer, as it houses the US military, which includes the army, navy and air force, and has more than 750,000 employees. This figure encompasses the civil workforce within the defense department. There\u8217\'20\'19s an additional 1.3 million active-duty US military personnel that \uc2 BLS\uc2  does not include when counting the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Veterans Affairs is the next largest employer, with approximately 480,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 The number of federal workers has largely remained unchanged over the last 50 years, with exceptions every 10 years for when there\u8217\'20\'19s an influx of employees working on the US Census.\par \par \uc2 What are the demographics of the federal workforce?\par \par \uc2 When compared with the overall employed workforce, federal workers skew slightly older. More than 50% of federal workers are over 45, compared with 43% of the overall workforce.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the racial demographics of the federal workforce largely reflect the demographics of the country as a whole, with one key exception: the federal government has a higher percentage of Black employees than the private sector.\par \par \uc2 This is likely because the federal government implemented laws after the civil rights era to prevent bias in workplace hiring, allowing more Black workers to enter the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signed two sweeping {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/26/trump-executive-orders-dei"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 executive orders}}}\uc2  that ended diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) programs inside the federal government. He overturned multiple executive orders, including one from the civil rights era that required private contractors to practice equal employment opportunity when hiring.\par \par \uc2 While the federal layoffs are separate from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s DEI actions, they both encompass dramatic efforts from the Trump administration to curtail the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s role as an employer.\par \par \uc2 Where do federal workers live?\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a common misconception that most federal workers live in Washington DC. While federal workers make up a huge chunk of Washington\u8217\'20\'19s population, the vast majority of federal workers live in other states across the country. They range from a national parks ranger in Montana to an infectious disease researcher in Georgia to a doctor working at a hospital run by the veterans affairs department. The \uc2 US Department of Agriculture (USDA)\uc2  alone has more than 100,000 employees across 4,500 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usda.gov/about-usda/general-information/our-agency#:~:text=The%20U.S.%20Department%20of%20Agriculture,across%20the%20country%20and%20abroad."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offices}}}\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 How do federal employees compare\par \par \uc2 with state and local government employees?\par \par \uc2 Federal employees make up just a small fraction of all government employees in the US. The vast majority of government workers, about 15 million, work for local governments, while nearly 6 million work for state governments.\par \par \uc2 Local governments manage police and fire departments, parks and recreation and housing services. State governments often work with local governments to help with things such as regulating highways and education.\par \par \uc2 What about federal contractors?\par \par \uc2 BLS\uc2  does not consider those who work for federal contractors, or private companies that have agreements to do work for the federal government, as federal government employees. There is no official number for how many private contractors there are for the federal government, but a recent \uc2 Brookings Institution{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/articles/is-government-too-big-reflections-on-the-size-and-composition-of-todays-federal-government/#the-contractor-workforce-826"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis}}}\uc2  estimated that there were approximately 5.2 million private contractor employees in 2023.\par \par \uc2 What does the Trump administration want to do to the federal workforce?\par \par \uc2 Musk is leading the White House\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to downsize the federal government, all supposedly for the sake of saving money. Musk said in October 2024 at a campaign event for Trump in New York City that he wants to cut the federal government budget by $2tn. Keep in mind that discretionary spending, or the amount that Congress has control over, was $1.7tn in 2023.\par \par \uc2 In February, Musk {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/11/trump-doge-executive-order-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  from the Oval Office: \u8220\'20\'1CThe people voted for major government reform, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what the people are going to get.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There are two main initiatives that Musk\u8217\'20\'19s Doge is trying to carry out. The first is offering federal employees buyouts for voluntarily leaving their jobs, and the second is laying off probationary employees, or employees who have been in their job for less than a year and don\u8217\'20\'19t have civil service protection.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear how many employees these initiatives will affect. The White House has said about 75,000 employees accepted buyouts, which will give workers a few months of pay and benefits if they leave their jobs imminently, what is known as \u8220\'20\'1Cdeferred resignation\u8221\'20\'1D. A federal judge temporarily blocked the program, which the Trump administration will now have to defend in court.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/13/trump-probationary-government-workers-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 layoffs}}}\uc2  of probationary employees appears to have caused chaos across many parts of the federal government. The \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  (IRS), Department of Health and Human Services and Department of Defense all said they each had more than 5,000 employees on probation who could be laid off.\par \par \uc2 The White House said that military service members, postal service workers and those whose positions relate to national security, immigration and public safety would be exempt from the buyout and layoff programs.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250307el37001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
