\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US unemployment rate rises to 4.1% as economy added 151,000 new jobs in February \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 5,929 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2025\par 16:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2025.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US dollar hits four-month low after February\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report shows rise in jobless rate and hiringUS job market grows amid threats of mass federal layoffs and Trump tariffs\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 After a red letter week for the European markets, and beyond, it\u8217\'20\'19s time to wrap up.\par \par \uc2 A quick recap:\par \par \uc2 The US labor market continued to grow in February even as threats of mass layoffs in the federal government and uncertainty around {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-tariffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s tariff policies}}}\uc2  rattle the US economy.\par \par \uc2 In February, 151,000 jobs were added to the economy, up from an adjusted 125,000 jobs gained in January. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/usemployment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unemployment}}}\uc2  rate was 4.1%, little changed from 4% in January.\par \par \uc2 A total of 10,000 federal jobs were lost over the month but those losses were countered by hiring in healthcare, financial activities, transportation and warehousing, and social assistance.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/07/us-february-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US job market grows amid threats of mass federal layoffs and Trump tariffs}}}\par \par \uc2 European stock markets have slumped after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s second reversal on tariffs caused deep uncertainty among investors, while the euro was on track for its biggest weekly rise since the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax index is down 1.9% in late trading, as weak factory data added to the signs of difficult economic conditions. France\u8217\'20\'19s Cac 40 is down 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 In London, stocks have shrugged off their earlier losses, but that still leaves the FTSE 100 on track for its worst week this year.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/07/european-markets-euro-trump-tariff-ftse-100-dax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European markets fall and euro soars amid Trump tariff delays}}}\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s foreign minister has accused the US of \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo-faced\u8221\'20\'1D behaviour, condemning the imposition of tariffs on Chinese goods and warning against the \u8220\'20\'1Claw of the jungle\u8221\'20\'1D that could emerge from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica First\u8221\'20\'1D policy.\par \par \uc2 Wang Yi was speaking after the latest trade data showed a drop in Chinese imports, suggesting trade war fears were hitting demand.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/mar/07/china-foreign-minister-condemns-us-tariffs-two-faced"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s foreign minister condemns US imposition of tariffs as \u8216\'20\'18two-faced\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\par \par \uc2 UK house prices unexpectedly fell last month as concerns over the sluggish economy outweighed an anticipated rush of people trying to complete purchases before stamp duty increases in April.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/mar/07/uk-house-prices-fall-unexpectedly-says-halifax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK house prices fall unexpectedly, says Halifax}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s flip-flopping over tariffs is causing chaos for farmers and food producers on both sides of the US-Canada border, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Trump slapped 25% tariffs on most Canadian and Mexican goods on Tuesday, only to then delay the new duties on many products on Thursday,\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the biggest challenge we\u8217\'20\'19ve ever faced and I\u8217\'20\'19ve been doing this for over 20 years,\u8221\'20\'1D said John Nickel, a Manitoba hog producer who sells exclusively to the US.\par \par \uc2 His first of two weekly shipments of piglets crossed the border tariff-free on Monday, but he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know whether a 25% markup will appear on the invoice for the animals he sent Thursday morning. More {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-03-07/trump-tariffs-throw-us-canada-food-supply-chain-into-chaos?srnd=homepage-uk&sref=Xl91GI8N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has reversed its earlier small fall:\par \par \uc2 enltrU.S. STOCK INDEXES TURN POSITIVE; S&P 500 UP 0.3%, NASDAQ UP 0.5%, DOW UP 0.2%\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 First Squawk (@FirstSquawk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FirstSquawk/status/1898028140448117043?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Reuters: Recession risks rise for all three North American economies over US tariff chaos\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s threatened trade war against Mexico and Canada risks dragging both countries into recession, and the US with it, economists fear.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of economists across North America found that the risks to the Mexican, Canadian and American economies are piling up, and that the chaotic implementation of U.S. tariffs that has created deep uncertainties for businesses and decision-makers.\par \par \uc2 Reuters polled 74 economists across Canada, the U.S. and Mexico, and 70 said that the risk of a recession in their respective economy had increased.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatens Russia with additional sanctions and tariffs\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has another target for tariffs \u8211\'20\'13 Russia.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 On his Truth Social site}}}\uc2 , the US president has just threatened to impose new sanctions on Moscow unless a peace deal with Ukraine is agreed soon.\par \par \uc2 Trump, who has faced criticism for being much harder on Ukraine then Russia, writes:\par \par \uc2 Based on the fact that Russia is absolutely \u8220\'20\'1Cpounding\u8221\'20\'1D Ukraine on the battlefield right now, I am strongly considering large scale Banking Sanctions, Sanctions, and Tariffs on Russia until a Cease Fire and FINAL SETTLEMENT AGREEMENT ON PEACE IS REACHED.\par \par \uc2 To Russia and Ukraine, get to the table right now, before it is too late. Thank you!!!\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2025/mar/07/ukraine-watershed-moment-bomb-gare-du-nord-eurostar-europe-news-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump \u8216\'20\'18strongly considering\u8217\'20\'19 Russia sanctions until Ukraine ceasefire agreed \u8211\'20\'13 Europe live}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens down, again\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report hasn\u8217\'20\'19t changed the mood on Wall Street, it seems.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have opened in the red, adding to losses yesterday as trade war fears rattle investors\u8217\'20\'19 nerves.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average has dipped by 138 points, or 0.33%, to 42,440 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index is also slightly lower, down 0.12%, while the tech-focused Nasdaq Composite is flat.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll probably have to only wait a month to see the impact of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s axing of federal government services in the jobs data.\par \par \uc2 Sara Pineros, economist at the CEBR thinktank, predicts March\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll (due on 4 April), will include a DOGE effect.\par \par \uc2 Pineros says:\par \par \uc2 Nonfarm payroll employment in the US increased by 151,000 in February, falling short of expectations, while the unemployment rate edged up slightly to 4.1%. These were the first full-month figures under the new Trump administration and come amidst large-scale cuts to federal government jobs by the \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, these cuts did not influence the February data much, as they mostly commenced after the mid-month snapshot. They will instead be evident in the March data published next month.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the impact of government job cuts, Cebr expects the ongoing uncertainty around US economic policy, specifically tariffs, to pose a downside risk to the labour market and the wider economy in the coming months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US jobs report: what the experts say\par \par \uc2 The US jobs market is \u8220\'20\'1Cstill in decent shape\u8221\'20\'1D argues Thomas Ryan, North America economist at Capital Economics :\par \par \uc2 The modest 151,000 rise in non-farm payrolls in February and 0.1%-point rise in the unemployment to 4.1% confirms the economy started the year soft but is not plummeting towards a recession.\par \par \uc2 Some of those fears may resurface in the March Employment Report, when recent federal government layoffs will be a much larger drag on employment than they were last month. But with private-sector hiring still running at a fairly healthy three-month average pace of 169,000, it suggests the labour market can handle it.\par \par \uc2 Neil Birrell\uc2 , chief investment officer at Premier Miton Investors, says the jobs report could allay some fears over the health of the US economy:\par \par \uc2 The earnings number was solid as well, which is supportive for the consumer sector. Clearly we need to see more evidence on lay-offs in the public sector and to understand what impact they will have overall, but this number means we can breathe more easily as we go into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Richard Flynn, managing director at \uc2 Charles Schwab UK\uc2 , calls the report \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointing\u8221\'20\'1D, though:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s jobs figures are below expectations, indicating that demand in the labour market is lower than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 This disappointing news comes at a time when the market is in need of a pick-me-up. Bearish sentiment has begun to grow as new tariffs have prompted concerns about the outlook for economic growth and a spike in initial jobless claims figures last week called into question the health of the labour market. Today\u8217\'20\'19s numbers may be seen to reinforce anxieties on both fronts. With investors already concerned about a growth slow down, we will likely see greater sensitivity to economic data in the coming days and weeks.\par \par \uc2 As a result, this report could further weigh on the market after a forlorn February.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 This may sent alarm bells ringing: The number of Americans employed part time for economic reasons increased by 460,000 to 4.9 million in February.\par \par \uc2 These individuals would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs.\par \par \uc2 Heather Long, economic columnist at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , says this can be an \u8220\'20\'1Cearly warning sign\u8221\'20\'1D of a weakening economy:\par \par \uc2 enltrOne worrying sign in February jobs report =\par \par \uc2 People working part-time for "economic reasons" (meaning they could not find a full-time job) jumped by +460,000 to 4.9 million.\par \par \uc2 This metric can be an early warning sign.\par \par \uc2 It's currently at the highest level since May 2021. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Ul2AE02TWp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Ul2AE02TWp}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1898011738378756469?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Where were the 151,000 new jobs created?\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a breakdown of some of the sectors of the US economy that added jobs in February.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Healthcare added 52,000 jobs in February\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in financialactivities rose by 21,000, although commercial banking lost 5,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Transportationandwarehousing employment rose by 18,000,.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in socialassistance rose by 11,000\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Warehouseclubs, supercenters, and other generalmerchandiseretailers added 10,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 But there were losses in other sectors too:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Within government, federalgovernmentemployment declined by 10,000 in February.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in retailtrade fell by 6,0000\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in foodandbeverageretailers declined by 15,000, \u8220\'20\'1Clargely due to strike activity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltr151,000 jobs gained in February. A solid number.\par \par \uc2 January was revised down by 18k to 125,000.\par \par \uc2 -->Healthcare continues to be the big hiring driver. A large cut to Medicaid could really hurt that.\par \par \uc2 Health care +52,000\par \par \uc2 Financial +21,000\par \par \uc2 Local gov't +20,000\par \par \uc2 Construction +19,000\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1898007688358854968?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US dollar hits election low after jobs report\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is continuing its recent swoon, after the US added slightly fewer jobs than expected in February.\par \par \uc2 Following today\u8217\'20\'19s news that payrolls rose by 151,000, and the jobless rate rose, the dollar dropped further against other major currencies.\par \par \uc2 The euro is now up 0.7 of a cent today at $1.0853, adding to earlier gains and seemingly cementing this week as its best against the dollar in 16 years.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has also weakened against the Japanese yen, to 147.29 yen to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 This has pulled the US dollar index down by 0.37% today, to its lowest level since 5 November \u8211\'20\'13 the day of the presidential election\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 enltrDollar drops post NFP, lowest since Election Day\par \par \uc2 Under 104 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/SrZm1LW9Z5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/SrZm1LW9Z5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mike Zaccardi, CFA, CMT ?? (@MikeZaccardi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MikeZaccardi/status/1898005639814676940?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not unusual for non-farm payroll data to be revised.\par \par \uc2 And this month, we have upward revisions to December\u8217\'20\'19s data, wiped out by a trimming of January\u8217\'20\'19s jobs growth.\par \par \uc2 The report says:\par \par \uc2 The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for December was revised up by 16,000, from +307,000 to +323,000, and the change for January was revised down by 18,000, from +143,000 to +125,000.\par \par \uc2 With these revisions, employment in December and January combined is 2,000 lower than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings up 4% per year\par \par \uc2 Happily for US workers, wages continues to rise last month.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report says:\par \par \uc2 In February, average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls rose by 10 cents, or 0.3 percent, to $35.93. Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 4.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Another disappointing aspect of today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report is that the labor force participation rate fell last month, from 62.6% to 62.4%.\par \par \uc2 That means fewer people were either in work or looking for a job (and had thus dropped out of the labor market altogether).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US unemployment rate at 4.1%\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows that the US unemployment rate has risen to 4.1%, up from 4% in January.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s still pretty low in historic terms, taking the jobless rate back up to its level in December.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 BLS\uc2  point out, the US unemployment rate has remained in a narrow range of 4.0 percent to 4.2 percent since May 2024.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 At 151,000, job creation in February is weaker than the average monthly gain of 168,000 over the prior 12 months \u8211\'20\'13 the last year of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Federal government employment declined by 10,000 in February.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows that federal government employment declined by 10,000 in February.\par \par \uc2 That could that be an early impact of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s DOGE programme, which has led to layoffs at a number of US federal agencies in recent weeks\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/13/elon-musk-doge-delete-agencies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump and Musk launch mass layoffs at several US federal agencies}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US jobs report released\par \par \uc2 NEWSFLASH: Hiring across the US economy picked up slightly at the start of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s second term in office.\par \par \uc2 The US economy added 151,000 new jobs in February, the latest non-farm payroll report shows, slightly below the 160,000 expected by economists.\par \par \uc2 But that\u8217\'20\'19s a pick-up compared to January \u8211\'20\'13 payroll growth that month has been revised down to 125,000, from 143,000 first reporter.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 Employment trended up in health care, financial activities, transportation and warehousing, and social assistance. Federal government employment declined.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Peter Berezin\uc2 , chief global strategist and director of research at \uc2 BCA Research\uc2 , suggests the markets don\u8217\'20\'19t actually expect the jobs report to come in close to the consensus (for a 160,000 rise in payrolls):\par \par \uc2 enltrI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the market is expecting anything close to the 160K consensus for NFP. Anything above 100K would be a \u8220\'20\'1Cbeat\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Peter Berezin\uc2  (@PeterBerezinBCA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PeterBerezinBCA/status/1897992635479962024?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the US jobs report in 10 minutes, Derren Nathan, senior equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s public sector efficiency drive that\u8217\'20\'19s impacting (the job market). Cuts are also being felt in retail and technology.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile weaker (jobs) data may support the case for further Fed rate cuts, job creation remains a key engine of economic growth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Investors brace for US jobs report\par \par \uc2 Tensions is building in the financial markets as investors await the latest US jobs report.\par \par \uc2 February\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll (NFP) \u8211\'20\'13 covering the first full month of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s second presidency \u8211\'20\'13 is expected to show a rise in employment. The data is due at 1.30pm UK time, or 8.30am on the east coast.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street economists predict the NFP rose by 160,000 in February, up from a 143,000 increase in January.\par \par \uc2 But, there are fears that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s push to reduce the size of the government and to cut government spending through his Doge initiative could be weakening the labour market.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, a survey of private sector payroll growth was much weaker than expected \u8211\'20\'13 with hiring slowing to just 77,000 new jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the US jobs market is a crucial factor determining how quickly the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cuts interest rates this year.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll also get an updated unemployment rate reading, and estimates of how much wages rose last month.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Innes, managing partner at SPI Asset Management, says:\par \par \uc2 The consensus for today\u8217\'20\'19s NFP print sits at +160K jobs, with unemployment steady at 4%, and wage growth holding at 4% YoY.\par \par \uc2 A negative surprise could send the dollar tumbling, especially after this week\u8217\'20\'19s policy-driven repricing across markets.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 World food prices rose in February\par \par \uc2 The rising cost of sugar, dairy and vegetable oil pushed up global food commodity prices in February, the UN\u8217\'20\'19s Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) reported today.\par \par \uc2 The FAO Food Price Index, which tracks a basket of food commodities, rose by 1.6% in February. While the meat price index remained stable, all other price indices rose, with the most significant increases recorded for sugar, dairy and vegetable oils, the FAO says.\par \par \uc2 Sugar prices jumped by 6.6% in the month, due to concerns over tighter global supplies in the 2024/25 season.\par \par \uc2 The FAO explains:\par \par \uc2 Declining production prospects in India and concerns over the impact of recent dry weather on the upcoming crop in Brazil, which exacerbated the seasonal effect, underpinned the increase in prices. Additionally, the strengthening of the Brazilian real against the United States dollar, which tends to affect exports from Brazil, further contributed to the overall increase in global sugar prices.\par \par \uc2 The Vegetable Oil Price Index rose 2%, driven by higher prices for palm, rapeseed, soy and sunflower oils.\par \par \uc2 The report says:\par \par \uc2 After a brief decline in January, international palm oil prices rebounded moderately and maintained their premiums over competing oils. The increase was largely underpinned by seasonally lower outputs in Southeast Asian producing countries and expectations of increased demand from the biodiesel industry in Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 Dairy prices rose by 4%, driven by higher prices across all major dairy products.\par \par \uc2 According to the FAO :\par \par \uc2 International cheese prices increased for the third consecutive month, rising by 4.7 percent from January. The rise was fueled by strong import demand, as recovering production in Europe was offset by seasonal output declines in Oceania.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump says tariffs could go up over time -Fox Business interview\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said U.S. tariffs could go up over time but gave no other details, according to an excerpt of a Fox Business interview taped on Thursday that aired on Friday, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether businesses could get clarity about his tariff plan, Trump said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I think so. But, you know, the terms could go up as time goes by, and they may go up and, you know, I don\u8217\'20\'19t know if it\u8217\'20\'19s predictability.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 (One can assuse Trump of many things, but predictability would not be at the top of the list)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is \u8220\'20\'1Closing its crown\u8221\'20\'1D, reports \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB:\par \par \uc2 US stocks might be dragging other markets with it, but the decline in the dollar has allowed the euro to roar back to life. EUR/USD is higher by 3.3% this week and is the second best performing currency in the G10. $1.10 is looking like the next major level for this pair. The currency has been boosted by Germany\u8217\'20\'19s planned fiscal bazooka to reboot its flagging economy.\par \par \uc2 Although the European bond market has stabilized at the end of the week, Eurozone sovereign yields have still surged 32 bps so far this week. The bond market does not seem to care about the details of Germany\u8217\'20\'19s spending plans, the timeline or the fact it has been announced by \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2 , even though he has not yet managed to form a government. This could lead to a short-term recovery in bond yields, although the euro is continuing to rise, suggesting that the euro\u8217\'20\'19s rise has untethered from Europe\u8217\'20\'19s bond market.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 Good news! The eurozone grew faster than previously thought at the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone GDP rose by 0.2% in October-December, new data from statistics body \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  shows, up from a previous estimate of 0.1% growth.\par \par \uc2 The change follows a big upgrade to Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s GDP in Q4 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 it grew by 3.6%, due to growth among its multinationals.\par \par \uc2 However, France (-0.1%) and Germany (-0.2%) are both on the brink of recession after contracting in October-December.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 European stock markets are on track for a weekly loss.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European Stoxx 600 index has dropped by around 1% this week.\par \par \uc2 Today, Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX has dropped by 1.5% after the early-morning drop in factory orders ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2025/mar/07/china-imports-trade-war-fears-market-selloff-us-jobs-payroll-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-67ca9fb38f088ad37ff99f33#block-67ca9fb38f088ad37ff99f33"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see earlier post}}}\uc2  ), but is still up over 2% this week, cheered by the \u8216\'20\'18big bazooka\u8217\'20\'19 fiscal loosening plan.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 The euro has now nudged a new four-month high of $1.086 against the US dollar.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 BofA Global Research has raised its forecast for the euro to reach $1.15 by the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 In a note published today, \uc2 BofA\uc2  argue that German\u8217\'20\'19s new fiscal package is \u8220\'20\'1Ca watershed moment\u8221\'20\'1D for the currency, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Euro on track for best week in 16 years\par \par \uc2 After a seismic week for the future of Europe, the euro is on track for its best week since the financial crisis 16 years ago.\par \par \uc2 The euro has climbed by 4.6% so far this week against the US dollar, from $1.0375 a week ago to $1.086 today.\par \par \uc2 That would be its biggest gain, in percentage terms, since the week to 20 March 2009, when the financial markets were being rattled by the recession following the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 In value terms, it\u8217\'20\'19s on track to be the sixth best week in at least the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 enltrThis week has seen the sixth biggest weekly upmove in EURUSD since the Euro\u8217\'20\'19s inception in 1999!\par \par \uc2 - The top four were all during the financial crisis \u8211\'20\'13 with the biggest upmove on record being the +692 points rise on the week ending 20th March 2009\par \par \uc2 - Outside the Financial crisis \u8211\'20\'13\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Philippe Loustaunau (@philoust) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/philoust/status/1897952534829904370?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 This week, the euro has benefitted from the slide in the value of the US dollar as Donald Trumps\u8217\'20\'19s threatened trade wars hurt the US currency.\par \par \uc2 But the euro has also gained against sterling, up to 83.95p, from 82.46p a week ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe euro continues to benefit from the apparent change in the eurozone\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal stance,\u8221\'20\'1D says Achilleas Georgolopoulos, senior market analyst at Trading Point.\par \par \uc2 There was a fiscal sea change in Germany this week, where major parties are pushing plans to reform the country\u8217\'20\'19s debt brake, to allow for higher defense spending.\par \par \uc2 Last night, European leaders agreed to a massive increase to defence spending, to bolster Europe\u8217\'20\'19s defence industry and increase its military capability.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/mar/06/watershed-moment-eu-leaders-close-to-agreeing-800bn-defence-plan-ukraine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Watershed moment\u8217\'20\'19: EU leaders agree plan for huge rise in defence spending}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 All Eurostar Paris trains cancelled on Friday amid French rail disruption over suspected WW2 bomb \u8211\'20\'13 Europe live\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s significant disruption to Eurostar services today, after an suspected unexploded bomb from the second world war was found on railway tracks in the commune of Saint Denis.\par \par \uc2 Eurostar\uc2  has just confirmed to the Guardian that \u8220\'20\'1Call {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/eurostar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eurostar}}}\uc2  trains are cancelled to and from Paris today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That includes all services on lines: London-Paris, Paris-London, Brussels-Paris and Paris-Brussels.\par \par \uc2 Our Europe live blog has all the action:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2025/mar/07/ukraine-watershed-moment-bomb-gare-du-nord-eurostar-europe-news-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 All Eurostar Paris trains cancelled on Friday amid French rail disruption over suspected WW2 bomb \u8211\'20\'13 Europe live}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 Oil on track for biggest monthly drop since October\par \par \uc2 This has also been a rough week for the oil price.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the international benchmark, has dropped by 4% so far this week to just over $70 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 That would be its biggest weekly drop since last October.\par \par \uc2 Oil has been hit by concerns that a trade war would hurt economic growth, dampening global demand for energy. The Opec+ group\u8217\'20\'19s plan to raise production also weighs on crude prices.\par \par \uc2 Oil is struggling with \u8220\'20\'1Ca mix of tariffs, US growth concerns, the potential lifting of US sanctions on Russia, and OPEC+ opting to increase output,\u8221\'20\'1D reports Tony Sycamore, analyst at IG.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 There is \u8220\'20\'1Ca sense of chaos\u8221\'20\'1D in the markets today, reports \uc2 Neil Wilson\uc2 , analyst at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tipranks.com/dashboard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TipRanks.com}}}\uc2  , after yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s Wall Street wobble:\par \par \uc2 Trump paused tariffs and the market sold off. The old adage that the market hates uncertainty rings true. How the heck are you supposed to make investments or plan?\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq ended the session down 2.6%, while the S&P 500 finished 1.78% lower. Both are headed for weekly losses of around the 4% mark.\par \par \uc2 The Mag 7 stocks are down 15% from recent highs. European stock markets were lower Friday after the DAX hit a record high on Germany\u8217\'20\'19s bumper spending plans and the \uc2 ECB\uc2  cut rates. The dollar continues to feel pressure while China\u8217\'20\'19s imports tumbled amid trade war concerns.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 on track for worst week of 2025\par \par \uc2 Unless we get a turnaround rally today, the FTSE 100 share index will post its biggest weekly loss of the year so far.\par \par \uc2 Since Monday morning, the \u8220\'20\'1CFootsie\u8221\'20\'1D has dropped by 1.8% - its biggest weekly drop since 16-20 December last year.\par \par \uc2 Richard Hunter, head of markets at interactive investor, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell-worn though the phrase may be, markets hate uncertainty \u8211\'20\'13 and investors are getting it in spades.\par \par \uc2 The constant changes of tack emanating from the White House on tariffs are beginning to test investors\u8217\'20\'19 patience, not least because the differing messages have different implications for highly interconnected global markets. In addition, the US has seen a recent run of weakening economic data, quite apart from any inflationary and recessionary fears that investors are harbouring following President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive tariff actions so far.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 Shares have opened lower in London, at the start of the final trading day of the week.\par \par \uc2 After losses on Wall Street last night, the UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 share index is down 27 points, or 0.3% at 8655.\par \par \uc2 European markets are also lower, with the Stoxx 600 index dropping 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is weakening again today, down 0.25% against a basket of currencies.\par \par \uc2 The pound is up 0.2 cents to $1.2904, towards the four-month high touched yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The euro has gained half a cent, to $1.0831, which is also the strongest since last November.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK house price dip 0.1%\par \par \uc2 In the UK, house prices edged down last month, lender Halifax reports.\par \par \uc2 The average UK house price dipped by 0.1% in February, lowering the average property price down to \u163\'00\'A3298,602, down from \u163\'00\'A3298,815 in January.\par \par \uc2 Annual house price inflation remained unchanged at 2.9%, while in Scotland they rose by 3.8% \u8211\'20\'13 the fastest pace in 13 months.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Bryden, head of mortgages at Halifax, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFebruary\u8217\'20\'19s figures highlight the delicate balance within the UK housing market. While there\u8217\'20\'19s been talk of a last minute rush on new mortgages ahead of the changes to stamp duty, inevitably we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen some of the demand that was brought forward start to fade as the April deadline ticks closer, given the time needed to complete a purchase.\par \par \uc2 That may help to explain why growth in first-time buyer property prices eased in February, falling to +2.4%, in contrast to homemover price inflation which accelerated, reaching +3.7%.\par \par \uc2 enltrHouse price growth remains hesitant as UK prices took a -0.1% step back in February, making the average property price now \u163\'00\'A3298,602 according to lender {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HalifaxBank?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @HalifaxBank}}}\par \par \uc2 Annual growth however remained steady at +2.9%, but Scotland saw house prices rise at the fastest pace in 13\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/rmFfZSNOl9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/rmFfZSNOl9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1897912628573319550?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 German factory orders tumble\par \par \uc2 Factory orders in Germany have slumped, showing the challenge facing chancellor-in-waiting \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 German industrial orders fell by 7.0% month-on-month in January, the federal statistics office reported this morning. Excluding large orders, incoming orders were 2.7% lower than in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, orders were 2.6% lower than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 deltrDer reale {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Auftragseingang?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Auftragseingang}}}\uc2  im Verarbeitenden Gewerbe ist im Januar 2025 gegen\u252\'00\'FCber Dezember 2024 nach vorl\u228\'00\'E4ufigen Angaben um 7,0 % gefallen. Haupturs\u228\'00\'E4chlich daf\u252\'00\'FCr sind R\u252\'00\'FCckg\u228\'00\'E4nge von Neuauftr\u228\'00\'E4gen im Maschinenbau (-10,7 %) und Sonstigen Fahrzeugbau (-17,6 %). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KktDAYxGbl"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/KktDAYxGbl}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JsWjZ4e1TY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JsWjZ4e1TY}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Statistisches Bundesamt (@destatis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/destatis/status/1897908077594021892?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Chinese foreign minister Wang Yi has accused Washington of \u8220\'20\'1Cmeeting good with evil\u8221\'20\'1D, as anger over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tariffs continues to bubble up.\par \par \uc2 Wang told a press conference today, on the sidelines of the country\u8217\'20\'19s annual parliamentary session, that China will continue to retaliate to the United States\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Carbitrary tariffs\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Associated Press reports:\par \par \uc2 Wang said China\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to help the U.S. contain its fentanyl crisis have been met with punitive tariffs, which are straining their ties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo country should fantasize that it can suppress China and maintain a good relationship with China at the same time,\u8221\'20\'1D Wang said. \u8220\'20\'1CSuch two-faced acts are not good for the stability of bilateral relations or for building mutual trust.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 China's imports tumble as demand falls and trade war heats up\par \par \uc2 China imports have fallen sharply at the start of this year, as the prospect of a trade war with the US hits its economy.\par \par \uc2 Imports fell 8.4% year-on-year in January and February, new customs data shows, weaker than the 1% growth expected by economists.\par \par \uc2 That suggests that China\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturing base could be cutting back on buying raw materials and parts, concerned that demand for their wares would fall due to new tariffs at the US border.\par \par \uc2 Lynn Song, chief economist for Greater China at ING, says:\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s economy got off to a weak start in 2025 as exports grew just 2.3% in the first two months of the year. A sharp slump in imports, meanwhile, resulted in a bigger-than-expected trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 Looking into the detail of the import data, Song explains:\par \par \uc2 We still saw strong imports in tech-related imports, with a 54.4% YoY ytd surge in automatic data processing equipment imports. And an overall 6.4% YoY ytd growth in hi-tech product imports. However, most other categories came in weak.\par \par \uc2 Commodities imports generally contracted over the first two months of the year, with crude oil (-10.5%), natural gas (-13.8%), and steel (-7.9%) all still soft. We\u8217\'20\'19re already seeing a slump in soybean imports, which fell by -14.8% YoY ytd. This was even before the impacts of China\u8217\'20\'19s retaliatory tariffs on US agricultural products.\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s exports rose, though, in the first two months of 2025 \u8211\'20\'13 up 2.3%.\par \par \uc2 frltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/China?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #China}}}\uc2  exports: +2.3Y, imports: -8.4%Y, trade surplus (Jan + Febr): $170bn, chart {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ING_Economics?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ING_Economics}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Ly0QgAngAr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Ly0QgAngAr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ACEMAXX ANALYTICS (@acemaxx) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/acemaxx/status/1897891045062451241?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2025}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/quote/CNCXUS:"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Exports to the US}}}\uc2  rose to almost $76bn, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports, the highest total for January and February since 2022 when the Covid-19 pandemic was upending global trade.\par \par \uc2 US data yesterday showed that America\u8217\'20\'19s trade deficit swelled to a record high in January, as firms tried to front-run tariffs by importing more goods.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Trade war fears drive up volatility\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The markets continue to be buffeted by fears of a global trade war, as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  vacillates over the imposition of tariffs on major US trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Last night in New York, the S&P 500 index fell 1.8% to its lowest level since early November \u8211\'20\'13 the post-election Trump bump has well-and-truly vanished. Tech stock slid, pushing the Nasdaq index into a correction (more than 10% below its record high).\par \par \uc2 Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s fear index, the CBOE Volatility index, closed at its highest level since 18 December, showing investors are jittery.\par \par \uc2 They may also be flummoxed, after Trump temporarily delayed tariffs on many goods from Canada and Mexico yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/06/trump-canada-mexico-tariff-delay-exemptions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump delays tariffs on many products from Mexico and Canada}}}\par \par \uc2 Despite that u-turn, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe great unwind of US equity evolves and gathers momentum\u8221\'20\'1D, says Chris Weston, analyst at brokerage Pepperstone.\par \par \uc2 Weston explains:\par \par \uc2 Confusion reigns around the Trump Administration policy agenda, and while we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen yet another pause on Canadian and Mexican tariffs until 2 April, the lack of consistency to hold policy firm further limits the visibility US businesses have to position margins and to make strategic planning decisions.\par \par \uc2 Trump detailed that he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cnot even looking at the stock market\u8221\'20\'1D \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s easy to be sceptical on that call, but Trump needs to portray control when putting through the hard policies.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s never a great sign when politicians start blaming malignant forces when the financial markets give their policies the thumbs down. But that was Trump\u8217\'20\'19s message yesterday; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2025/03/06/trump-blames-globalists-for-stock-market-sell-off.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked if his tariffs were scaring the markets, Trump replied}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, a lot of them are globalist countries and companies that won\u8217\'20\'19t be doing as well.\par \par \uc2 Because we\u8217\'20\'19re taking back things that have been taken from us many years ago.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 European stock markets are expected to drop today, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ig.com/uk/indices/markets-indices/ftse-100"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  FTSE 100 index forecast to fall 0.55% or 48 points}}}\uc2 . Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei has fallen over 2% today, to its lowest level since last September.\par \par \uc2 Investors are poised for the latest US jobs report. The consensus is that hiring picked up in February, lifting non-farm payrolls by around 160,000 last month.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 former Director of the \uc2 US National Economic Council\uc2  turned Fox News host \u8211\'20\'13 suggested the NFP report could be flat, or even negative\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 enltrKudlow: "Some very smart people are telling me that the February jobs number coming out Friday could be flat, even negative. The GDP tracker from the \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  is showing for the first quarter a -2.5 or -2.8%. And we've had lousy numbers on things like housing and business\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zKFN5h6wJv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/zKFN5h6wJv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jeffrey Jonah (@JeffreyJonah5) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JeffreyJonah5/status/1897535429563572349?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 6, 2025}}}\uc2  The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am GMT: Halifax index of UK house prices in February\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Eurozone GDP Q4 2024 (3rd estimate)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT: US non-farm payroll for February\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020250307el37000uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
